/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"Versioning and documentation","d":"Microcontroller projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyectos-microcontrolador#documentacion","x":"05 Versioning and documentation Item What it must contain Version control The code in a repository, with small changes and messages that say why. Every delivered version, tagged. Version in the device The version number visible on the display or through a serial command. Without it, you cannot tell what is running on each unit. Schematic and board Source files and PDF, with the revision marked on the board itself. User manual How to operate it, what each indication means and what to do about each error message. Technical manual How to calibrate it, how to update the program, what is checked during maintenance and what spare parts it uses. Backup Code, schematics, manufacturing files and component datasheets, all together and stored away from the working machine. The acid test That another person can pick up the project, understand it, compile it and modify it without asking anything. If that is not possible, the project is incomplete even though the device works. And it is worth measuring it for real: hand the folder to a classmate and see how far they get on their own."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Microcontroller projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyectos-microcontrolador#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Requirements Take a project idea and write its requirements in a verifiable way, with numbers and conditions. Swap with another group: if someone can interpret a requirement in two different ways, it is badly written and must be corrected. Lab 2 · From a blocking sequence to a state machine Write a simple controller with waits and verify that the display and the buttons stop responding while it runs. Rewrite it as a non-blocking state machine and check that everything responds at the same time. Lab 3 · Watchdog timer Enable the watchdog timer and cause a deliberate lock-up inside a function. Verify that the device resets by itself and logs the reset. Then move the watchdog refresh to a wrong place and check that it no longer protects. Lab 4 · The course capstone project Take a project of your own through the seven stages, delivering each document. The whole path is assessed, not just whether the device powers up: requirements, tests and documentation weigh as much as the operation itself."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Microcontroller projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyectos-microcontrolador#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Starting to program without requirements The project never ends: something that nobody defined is always missing. Integrating everything at once When it fails, you do not know which of the ten blocks is at fault. Each block is tested alone first. Blocking waits in the main loop The interface stops responding and the control loop loses its timing. States without a maximum time The device can wait forever for an event that never arrives. Refreshing the watchdog timer in an interrupt The main program can be hung and the watchdog never acts. Not validating input ranges A disconnected sensor reads zero and the controller acts on false data. No version number in the device It is impossible to know which program each unit in the field has. Testing only on the bench, for a few minutes Problems of endurance, temperature and power loss appear later, at the customer’s site."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Microcontroller projects","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyectos-microcontrolador#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What makes a requirement verifiable? That it has numbers and conditions: range, permissible error, response time, operating conditions. It is what gets tested later and what defines whether the device is finished. Why is each block tested separately before integration? Because if everything is integrated at once and it fails, there is no way to know which block is the problem. Testing in parts shortens the diagnosis enormously. What advantage does a state machine have over a sequence with waits? It does not block: it can be called continuously from the main loop while the display, communication and buttons are served. With waits, everything else stops. Why must each state have a maximum time? So that the system does not wait indefinitely for an event that is not going to arrive. When the time runs out, it moves to a safe fault state. What is cooperative scheduling? A main loop where each task runs at its own pace by comparing times, with no waits. It requires that no function takes too long. Where is the watchdog timer refreshed, and why? In a single place in the main loop. If it were refreshed from an interrupt, the loop could be hung and the watchdog would not detect it. Why must the range of the inputs be validated? Because a disconnected or faulty sensor usually reads zero or full scale, and the controller would take that value as good and act on false data. Name three tests that cannot be left out. Power loss at critical moments, disconnecting sensors "},{"t":"Fault types, and the case of the lost neutral","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#fallas","x":"01 Fault types, and the case of the lost neutral A distribution network runs for kilometres outdoors or under the sidewalk, and it fails for three kinds of reasons: external (wind, branches, lightning, vehicle impacts, excavations, animals), ageing (dried-out insulation, loose connectors and splices, broken insulators) and excess load. The network’s protection —fuses, reclosers, circuit breakers with relays— clears the fault, but only someone who understands it can say what failed and where. Each kind of fault leaves a different signature in the voltages and currents, and that signature can be measured. Fault What happens What you notice Broken conductor (open phase) A conductor breaks (wind, branch, impact, a splice that gives way) or a fuse blows. The users on that phase lose voltage. If the loose end touches the ground or an object, it is also a ground fault. No power on one of the three phases. Three-phase motors keep turning on two phases: they hum, lose torque and overheat. The network may not trip, because there is no overcurrent. Phase to earth An energized conductor touches the ground, a pole or a branch, or its insulation is punctured. It is the most frequent fault. The current depends on where it occurs and on how the system neutral is earthed (solidly, through an impedance, or isolated). The earth-fault protection trips if the current reaches its setting. If the contact is resistive (a tree, dry soil), the current may stay below the setting: the fault persists wit"},{"t":"Finding the fault: from the complaint to the measurement","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#busqueda","x":"02 Finding the fault: from the complaint to the measurement The method is that of any diagnosis (see Fault diagnosis and detection: rule out, do not confirm), applied to a network that cannot be switched off as a whole or looked at from a bench. It is done in four steps: 1 · The complaint Before leaving, what is known: how many users are affected and where (a block, a branch, a whole neighbourhood?), since when, what happened (a storm, sparks, a bang, a smell, lights going up and down), which protection operated and whether there were automatic reclosures. A single user without power is almost always a problem of their service connection or their own installation; several on the same phase, of the low-voltage branch; a whole neighbourhood, of the feeder or the transformer station. 2 · Section-by-section elimination The network is divided into sections between switching devices and measured at intermediate points: if there is voltage, the fault is downstream; if not, upstream. Each measurement rules out half of what is left. 3 · Measurement Voltages between each phase and neutral, and between phases, under load; and, with the section dead, verified and earthed, continuity and insulation. The signature of each fault is in the table below. With bisection each measurement rules out half of the remaining sections, so the number of measurements is the exponent to which 2 must be raised to reach the number of sections: eight sections, three measurements ( ); sixteen, four. Checking "},{"t":"Underground cables: locating what cannot be seen","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#cables","x":"03 Underground cables: locating what cannot be seen On an overhead line the fault is visible: a conductor on the ground, a broken insulator. In a buried cable nothing is visible, and opening trenches “to see” along kilometres is not viable. It is located in three stages: Classify the fault. With the cable dead, verified, discharged and earthed (a medium-voltage cable is a capacitor and holds charge), the insulation of each conductor against the screen and between conductors is measured with an insulation tester, along with continuity. The result places the fault in one of three classes: open conductor, short circuit or low-resistance fault, or high-resistance fault that only appears at service voltage or at a test voltage. Pre-locate (how far from the end it is), by reflectometry or by bridge. Pinpoint on the ground, following the real route of the cable. Reflectometry (TDR). The instrument sends a pulse down the cable and listens for what comes back. Wherever the impedance of the cable changes —an open end, a short circuit, a splice, a fault— part of the pulse is reflected. The time between the pulse sent and the echo, with the propagation speed of the pulse in that cable, gives the distance: v: propagation speed of the pulse in that cable (cable data, in m/µs) · t: time between the sent pulse and the echo · the 2 is because the pulse goes there and back. The speed is a property of the cable (it depends on its insulation, and is usually of the order of half to two thirds of "},{"t":"Resolve, restore and record","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#resolver","x":"04 Resolve, restore and record Finding the fault is half of it. The intervention is ordered by priorities, and that order prevents the urgency to “get the lights back” from causing a second accident: Safety. The section is isolated with the switching devices, locked out, verified dead and earthed with short-circuiting (the five golden rules of Occupational health and safety ). The work area is marked and protected for the public. Restore the healthy users. Before repairing, whatever has no fault is re-supplied by another path. In a medium-voltage ring network, which is normally operated open at one point, the disconnectors on both sides of the faulty section are opened and the normally open point is closed: the two healthy stretches are fed from opposite sides and only the faulty section stays out of service. Repair. Replace the conductor, the insulator or the connector; a splice or terminal on an armoured cable is made by qualified personnel with the manufacturer’s kit. A fuse is replaced by another of the same rating and type: fitting a larger one leaves the network unprotected. Verify before energizing. Continuity and insulation of the repaired section are checked again, along with the identification and order of the phases (swapping two phases reverses the rotation of every motor), and that the earthing has been restored and no tools or temporary connections are left behind. Re-energize and measure. The switching device is closed from a safe place, and voltages and curren"},{"t":"Safety on public streets: siting, heights and distances","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#emplazamiento","x":"05 Safety on public streets: siting, heights and distances All the safety of a line installed in the street comes down to three ideas: that a person cannot touch a live part, that they cannot come so close that current jumps through the air (the higher the voltage, the greater the distance), and that nothing that hangs, rises or moves in the street gets in the way of the line. Siting —where the line is placed— and distances are the concrete way of meeting them. Overhead lines. They run on poles, on the side of the sidewalk set by the utility, with the conductors higher the higher the voltage. In low voltage, bundled insulated cable is preferred, which tolerates a branch brushing it and reduces the risk of contact; in medium voltage, in tree-lined areas, covered or protected conductor. Poles are placed where an impact is unlikely, and where that is not possible they are protected and marked. Crossings of streets, roads, railways and other lines (telephone, cable TV) are made with the distances and permits that each regulation sets. Underground lines. They run under the sidewalk, at the depth set by the standard, with mechanical protection above (bricks or slabs) and a warning tape that alerts whoever is digging before they reach the cable. Road crossings are made in ducts, and splices and pull points are grouped in covered chambers. They are separated from other buried services (water, gas, sewers, telecommunications) by the regulatory distances, and before digging on any side"},{"t":"Work in the street, third parties and a downed wire","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#calle","x":"06 Work in the street, third parties and a downed wire Signing and fencing the work. A trench or work on a line over the sidewalk or the roadway is a hazard to anyone who does not know it is there: the distracted pedestrian, the blind person, the child, the driver arriving at night. Signing is set up from the outside in and removed in the reverse order: Advance warning far enough ahead of the work: signs and cones that tell traffic what is coming. Detour of traffic and a protected pedestrian path with a firm surface, so as not to push people onto the roadway. Continuous fence around the work area: tape alone is not enough for an open hole. A trench is covered or fenced with no open stretches. Own light at night (flashing beacons) and reflective elements, and personnel in high-visibility clothing. Sign of the person in charge of the work, with their phone number, kept up outside working hours as long as there is an open hole or equipment half assembled. This adds to what Law 19.587 and Decree 351/79 require for the protection of workers (see Safety, hygiene and quality in installation work ) and to what the municipality requires for occupying the public road, which is arranged before starting. Risks to third parties. Almost all accidents with networks in the street involve people outside the trade doing something everyday near a line: Who or what The risk Prevention Trees A branch that touches the line connects it to earth or between phases, and a wet branch conducts. Pruning "},{"t":"In the workshop and on site","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#taller","x":"07 In the workshop and on site Lab 1 · The lost neutral in miniature Materials: isolating transformer with adjustable output (variac), two 12 V lamps (one 5 W and one 21 W), AC voltmeter, lamp holders, single-pole switch and leads. Steps: 1) Set the output to 20.8 V: the lamp voltage multiplied by the square root of 3, the same relation as between line and phase voltage in the network. 2) Connect the two lamps in series across those 20.8 V: this is the equivalent of the two homes with the neutral broken. 3) Before measuring, calculate the voltage each lamp should receive, from the rated power and voltage of each (the resistance is the voltage squared divided by the power). 4) Close the switch and measure both voltages for a few seconds only: the 5 W lamp is overstressed. Acceptance criterion: the smaller lamp receives the higher voltage; the readings match the calculated ones within 15 % (the filament is not a constant resistance: its hot resistance is about ten times its cold resistance). Even at low voltage, the circuit is built and modified with the source off. Lab 2 · Locating a fault with the Murray loop Materials: 100 m of two-conductor cable of 2.5 mm² (joined at the far end), two resistance decade boxes (or a teaching Wheatstone bridge), a 1.5 V cell with a 10 Ω resistor in series (limits the current through the loop), a 100 Ω resistor, an earthing lead and a digital multimeter on the millivolt range instead of the galvanometer. Steps: 1) The teacher marks, without sa"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Working on a section “dead” without verifying with an instrument and earthing A switching operation, a recloser or back-feed energizes it again. It is one of the main causes of accidents on networks. Believing that a downed wire without sparks is dead A resistive fault does not trip the protection and the conductor stays energized; getting close or touching it is enough. Running or taking long steps away from a downed wire The step voltage between the feet increases. You leave with short steps and feet together. Measuring the three phase-to-neutral voltages and not looking at the line voltages A lost neutral (normal line voltage) cannot be told from a supply problem (low line voltage). Replacing a fuse or a breaker with a larger one “so it doesn’t trip” The protection is removed from the circuit; the next fault burns the cable instead of being cleared. Reading the distance to the fault from the TDR as a straight line on the street The cable has bends, crossings and reserves: the excavation ends up metres from the right place. Re-energizing a repaired cable without checking the phase order Motors turn backward and loads are fed with swapped phases. Leaving a trench with only tape or no light at night Pedestrians and vehicles fall into a hole that cannot be seen. Copying a minimum height or safety distance from memory It may be the value for another voltage, another location or a standard already replaced. It is read in the current standar"},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Faults and safety in distribution networks","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/redes-via-publica#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment In a 3×380/220 V network the neutral breaks upstream. Home A (phase L1) draws 1100 W and home B (phase L2), 2200 W. What voltage does each receive? Resistances at 220 V: 44 Ω and 22 Ω. They are in series across the 380 V between L1 and L2, with the voltage shared in proportion to resistance. A, the lighter load, receives 253 V (15 % above nominal) and B, 127 V. The breaker and the RCD notice nothing. How can a lost neutral be told from an open phase by measuring voltages? With a lost neutral the phase-to-neutral voltages are uneven (some high, some low) but the voltages between phases are still about 380 V. With an open phase, one phase-to-neutral is almost 0 V and between that phase and the other two there are about 220 V instead of 380 V; between the two healthy phases the 380 V is kept. A TDR, with the cable speed set to 170 m/µs, shows an echo at 8 µs. How far away is the fault and what does the echo polarity say? The pulse goes there and back, so you divide by 2: An inverted echo indicates a short circuit; one of the same polarity, an open conductor. On a 600 m cable, the Murray bridge balances with R a = 200 Ω (arm to the faulty conductor) and R b = 600 Ω. Where is the fault? 300 m from the end where the bridge is. Measured along the cable, not in a straight line: it is carried over onto the route plan. With the data of the downed-wire example (100 A, 100 Ω·m), what voltage is there between the feet when moving from 2.0 m to 2.5 m from the contact poi"},{"t":"Law, decree, regulation and standard","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#jerarquia","x":"01 Law, decree, regulation and standard In everyday talk “the standard” is used for everything, and that mixes things that bind in very different ways. They need to be told apart, because who you complain to and what you can demand depend on it. Instrument Issued by What it does Examples Law The national Congress or a provincial Legislature. Sets the principle and the obligations. Applies to everyone within its territory. Law 19.587 on occupational health and safety (national). Law 10.281 on electrical safety (Córdoba). Decree Executive branch: the President or the Governor. Regulates the law: says how it is complied with in practice. Decree 351/79, which implements Law 19.587. Resolution A regulator or an enforcement authority, within its competence. Details procedures and adopts technical regulations. Resolution SRT 592/2004 (live-line work). General resolutions of ERSeP. Technical standard Standards bodies (IRAM, IEC) and technical associations (AEA). Defines how things are designed, built and tested. By itself it has no force of law. AEA 90364. IRAM 10005 (safety colours and signs). IEC 60364. IRAM and IEC IRAM (Argentine Institute for Standardization and Certification) writes Argentine standards and represents the country before international bodies. The IEC (International Electrotechnical Commission) writes electrical standards of worldwide scope. Many IRAM standards are translations or adaptations of IEC ones. AEA The Argentine Electrotechnical Association is a civil a"},{"t":"Regulators and distribution utilities","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#entes","x":"02 Regulators and distribution utilities Between the power plant and the socket there are several actors. Some operate the grid; others oversee those who operate it. For the electricity used by a house in Córdoba, the path is this: Actor What it is What it does ENRE National Electricity Regulatory Entity. Created by Law 24.065, the electricity regulatory framework. Oversees high-voltage transmission and distribution under national jurisdiction. It does not oversee EPEC. CAMMESA The company that administers the wholesale electricity market. Coordinates dispatch of the power plants and transactions between generators and utilities. ERSeP Regulator of Public Services of the Province of Córdoba. Oversees the province's utilities on tariffs and service quality, handles complaints and enforces the provincial electrical safety law. EPEC Empresa Provincial de Energía de Córdoba, the distribution utility. Operates the grid, connects services, installs the meter, bills and handles outages. In the rest of the province, electric cooperatives also distribute. Owner and installer Whoever owns the installation and whoever built it. Maintain and answer for everything downstream of the meter. Who do you complain to? An outage or abnormal voltage on the grid is first claimed from the utility; if it does not answer, from the regulator of its jurisdiction, which in Córdoba is ERSeP. A fault in the interior installation is not the utility's: it is the owner's, and the installer who certified it a"},{"t":"Inside AEA 90364","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#aea","x":"03 Inside AEA 90364 The Regulation for the execution of electrical installations in buildings (AEA 90364) is not a book read from cover to cover: it is a system of parts consulted according to the question. It follows the organization of the international standard IEC 60364. Part What it covers What it is consulted for 1 Scope, object and fundamental principles. Which installations it applies to and which safety principles rule. 2 Definitions. What each term means: exposed conductive part, protective conductor, indirect contact. 3 Determination of general characteristics. Power, earthing system, external influences of the place. 4 Protection for safety. Against electric shock, overcurrent, overvoltage. 5 Selection and erection of electrical equipment. Conductors, wiring systems, panels, earthing. 6 Verification of installations. What tests and inspection to carry out before energizing and periodically. 7 Particular rules for special locations and premises. Dwellings, offices and premises, bathrooms, pools, hospitals, fire-risk areas. How it is read: from general to particular Parts 1 to 6 apply to every installation. The sections of Part 7 add, and sometimes replace, rules for one type of place. If the particular section says nothing about a point, the general rule applies. For a dwelling, office or single premises the section to consult is AEA 90364-7-771. Each section is published and updated separately, so you must always consult the current edition of the part you need, n"},{"t":"Condominium buildings (horizontal property)","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#ph","x":"04 Condominium buildings (horizontal property) In an apartment building several units coexist, each with its owner, and common areas belong to all and are run by the owners' association. The installation regulation adds to what has been seen rules of its own for that coexistence: each unit's energy is metered separately, common areas have their own supply, and the cable routes cross places that do not belong to a single owner. Element What it is and what to watch in the design Service connection The link between the utility grid and the building. The utility defines it according to the total expected power. Meter cabinet or room Gathers the meters of all units and the one for common services. It must be accessible to utility staff, ventilated, lit and with working space; its location is checked before designing. Risers The vertical lines that carry energy from each unit's meter to its panel. They run in common-use shafts or conduits, protected, identified and separated from communications. Unit sub-panel Each apartment's panel: from there the unit is designed like a dwelling, with its degree of electrification and its circuits ( see dwelling project ). Common-services main panel Feeds lifts, water pumps, landing and stair lighting, emergency lights, door entry and whatever hangs from the association. It has its own meter and its maintenance belongs to the association. Earthing One earthing installation for the building, to which the panels and the exposed parts of the common "},{"t":"The licensed electrician and the certificate of suitable installation","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#habilitado","x":"05 The licensed electrician and the certificate of suitable installation Córdoba provincial Law 10.281, on electrical safety, provides that certain installations must have proof of compliance with the regulation, signed by a person with professional responsibility. That person is the Licensed Electrical Installer ( Instalador Electricista Habilitado ), registered in the register kept by ERSeP, and the proof is the Certificate of Suitable Electrical Installation ( Certificado de Instalación Eléctrica Apta ). What it certifies That the installation was built and verified according to the current technical regulation: inspection, protective conductor continuity, insulation, residual-current protection and earthing. By signing it, the installer takes responsibility for what is declared. When it is needed To request a new connection, a power increase or a reconnection from the utility, for the installations the law covers. Deadlines and the cases where it is required have been amended: check the ERSeP website. Document Made by What it contains Report and drawings Designer Loads, circuits, calculations, single-line diagram. See installation project. Measurement protocol Installer Measured insulation, continuity, RCD and earthing values, with the instrument used. Certificate of Suitable Electrical Installation Licensed Electrical Installer The declaration that the installation complies, with the property's and installer's data. Service request Owner Request to the utility, with the "},{"t":"In the workshop","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#taller","x":"06 In the workshop Practice 1 · Find the rule at the source Material: a current edition of the relevant section of AEA 90364 (in the school library or an authorized copy). Steps: choose five design decisions of a dwelling (degree of electrification, number of circuits, minimum conductor size, protection of a socket circuit, panel location) and, for each, note the clause where the regulation sets it. Acceptance: five decisions with their reference to the article and the edition; none “from memory”. Practice 2 · Classify the request Material: ten situations written on cards (“a neighbour complains of only 180 V”, “the kitchen installation has no earth”, “the meter seal is broken”, “the job has no certificate”…). Steps: for each, decide whether it is a matter for the utility, the regulator, the owner or the installer, and which instrument (law, decree, resolution, standard) governs it. Acceptance: each card with a responsible party and a source; the class discusses the doubtful ones. Practice 3 · File for a building Material: the plan of a building with four to ten apartments and a calculator. Steps: locate the meter cabinet, draw the single-line diagram from the service connection to each sub-panel and the common-services panel, and calculate the demand with a diversity factor given by the teacher (as in the worked example). Acceptance: legible single-line diagram with every element labelled, and service current calculated two ways (total and per phase) that agree apart from ro"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Saying “the standard says so” without knowing which level it comes from Someone is asked for something that does not apply, or something that does apply is not asked. Cite the instrument and the clause. Using an old edition of the AEA The project meets a superseded rule and ends up non-compliant. Always consult the current edition of the part needed. Believing that meeting the AEA replaces the law or the utility's requirement You can meet the technical standard and still go unconnected for lack of the certificate or for not respecting the meter cabinet. Confusing ENRE with ERSeP The claim goes to a body with no authority over EPEC. In Córdoba the regulator of the provincial utility is ERSeP. Adding all powers in a building without diversity Oversized service connection and riser; the budget rises for no reason. Designing the meter cabinet without consulting the utility It has to be redone on site, with the building nearly finished. Signing a certificate without having measured Professional liability for the installer, and a dangerous installation with paperwork in order. Tampering with the meter or its seal An offence, risk of electrocution and of a short circuit upstream, where nothing protects."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Regulations for electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/reglamentacion-aea#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the difference between a law, a decree, a resolution and a technical standard? A law is passed by Congress or a Legislature and sets general obligations; a decree is issued by the executive and implements the law; a resolution is issued by a regulator within its competence and details procedures; a technical standard (IRAM, IEC, AEA) defines how things are done and by itself does not bind: it does when a law or resolution cites it. AEA 90364 is written by a civil association. Why must it be followed? Because laws, regulators' resolutions and utility requirements refer to it, and because after an accident it is taken as the correct way to build the installation. Not following it leaves the installer without a defence. Which regulator oversees EPEC, and why is it not ENRE? ERSeP, the regulator of Córdoba's public services. ENRE regulates high-voltage transmission and distribution under national jurisdiction; the provincial utility answers to the provincial regulator. Where does the utility's responsibility end? At the meter, included: grid, service connection and metering. From the meter's output terminals inward the installation belongs to the owner, and whoever certified it answers for its execution. Name three elements found in an apartment building and not in a house. Any of these: the shared meter cabinet or room, the risers in common shafts, the common-services main panel with its own meter, and the diversity factor applied to the building's dem"},{"t":"Conductors, insulators and semiconductors","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#materiales","x":"01 Conductors, insulators and semiconductors What determines whether a material conducts or not is how free its valence electrons are — those in the outermost shell of the atom. Conductor 1 to 3 valence electrons, practically free. Copper, silver, aluminum. Resistivity. Semiconductor 4 valence electrons. Silicon and germanium. It neither conducts nor insulates: it depends on how it is treated. Resistivity to. Insulator 5 to 8 electrons, tightly bound. Glass, mica, plastic. Resistivity or more. Pure (intrinsic) silicon forms a crystal in which each atom shares its four electrons with four neighbors: covalent bonds. With all the electrons occupied, at 0 K it would be a perfect insulator. At room temperature, thermal agitation breaks some bonds and frees a few carriers; that is why it barely conducts. It is also why semiconductors depend strongly on temperature, a fact that reappears in everything that follows. Electron and hole When an electron breaks free it leaves a vacancy in the bond: a hole. A neighboring electron can jump in to fill it, and then the hole “moves” in the opposite direction. That is why a semiconductor has two types of carriers: electrons (negative charge) and holes (which behave like a positive charge). This does not happen in metals, and it is the key to everything else."},{"t":"Doping: N and P materials","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#dopaje","x":"02 Doping: N and P materials Pure silicon is useless. What makes it useful is adding to it, in a controlled way, a tiny amount of impurities: roughly one foreign atom for every 10 million silicon atoms. This is called doping. N-type material Pentavalent atoms are added (5 valence electrons): phosphorus, arsenic, antimony. Four electrons form bonds and one is left over, almost free. Majority carriers: electrons. Minority carriers: holes (from thermal agitation). The impurity is called a donor. The material remains electrically neutral. P-type material Trivalent atoms are added (3 valence electrons): boron, gallium, indium. One electron is missing to complete the fourth bond: a hole is left. Majority carriers: holes. Minority carriers: electrons. The impurity is called an acceptor. It is also electrically neutral. A common misconception “N-type” does not mean the material is negatively charged, nor “P-type” positively charged. Both are neutral: for every extra free electron there is a nucleus with an extra positive charge that compensates it. The N and the P indicate which type of carrier conducts the current, not a net charge."},{"t":"The PN junction","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#juntura","x":"03 The PN junction If in the same crystal one region is doped P and the adjacent one N, something remarkable happens at the boundary. The excess electrons on the N side diffuse toward the P side and recombine with the holes. In doing so they leave behind fixed ions: positive on the N side, negative on the P side. This region without carriers is called the depletion region, and the internal field that appears is the potential barrier. It is about 0.7 V in silicon and 0.3 V in germanium. It is responsible for all of the diode's behavior: as long as that barrier is not overcome from outside, no current flows."},{"t":"The diode: biasing and characteristic curve","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#diodo","x":"04 The diode: biasing and characteristic curve The three diode models Depending on the accuracy required, one or another is used. In Year 4 you work almost always with the second. Model Forward Reverse When to use it Ideal Short circuit (0 V) Open circuit Quick analysis, when the source is tens of volts. 0.7 V 0.7 V source Open circuit The usual one. A good balance between simplicity and accuracy. With r d 0.7 V + dynamic resistance (a few Ω) Very high resistance Large currents or dissipation calculations. Worked example · Diode in series with a resistor A 12 V source drives a silicon diode in series with a 1 kΩ resistor. Calculate the current and the power dissipated by the diode. With the 0.7 V model, the voltage across the resistor is: With the ideal model it would have given 12 mA: a 6 % error. With sources of 5 V or less, ignoring the 0.7 V already produces large errors and the ideal model stops being useful. The diodes used in the workshop Diode I F max. V RRM Use 1N4148 200 mA 100 V Signal, fast switching. Glass package. 1N4001 1 A 50 V Low-voltage rectification. 1N4007 1 A 1000 V The general-purpose one. It costs the same as the 4001: it is best to stock only this one. 1N5408 3 A 1000 V Higher-current power supplies. 1N4733A — 1 W Zener. 1N5822 3 A 40 V Schottky: drops only 0.3 V. Switching power supplies. The painted band on the body always marks the cathode. It is the only way to identify it, and mixing it up is the most frequent mistake when building a power supply"},{"t":"Rectification","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#rectificacion","x":"05 Rectification Rectifying means converting alternating current into pulsating direct current. It is the first stage of every power supply. Half-wave A single diode. It passes the positive half-cycle and blocks the negative one. Average value at the output of a half-wave rectifier, without a filter. The ripple frequency is the same as the mains: 50 Hz. It uses half of the time, delivers little DC and is hard to filter. It is only used in very small chargers. Full-wave with a center-tapped transformer Two diodes and a secondary with a center tap. Each diode conducts during one half-cycle, so both half-cycles appear across the load with the same polarity. Twice the half-wave value, and with 100 Hz ripple (much easier to filter). Graetz bridge (bridge rectifier) Four diodes and a plain secondary, without a center tap. It is the one used almost all the time. Rectifier Diodes V DC without filter Ripple f Total drop PIV per diode Half-wave 1 0.318 V p 50 Hz 0.7 V V p Full-wave, center tap 2 0.637 V p 100 Hz 0.7 V 2 V p Bridge 4 0.637 V p 100 Hz 1.4 V V p The bridge wins because it needs no center tap (a cheaper, better-utilized transformer) and because the PIV each diode must withstand is half that of the center-tap circuit. It pays with a drop of 1.4 V, which only matters in very low-voltage power supplies. Watch out for the RMS value A “12 V” transformer delivers 12 V RMS. The peak is, and that is what ends up on the filter capacitor (minus 1.4 V from the bridge): about 15.6 V. "},{"t":"Capacitor filter and ripple","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#filtro","x":"06 Capacitor filter and ripple The rectifier output is direct but pulsating: it is good for charging a battery, not for powering a circuit. A large capacitor in parallel with the load charges at the peaks and delivers current in the valleys. The remaining undulation is called ripple (or ripple voltage ). Peak-to-peak ripple. I DC in amperes, with a bridge (50 Hz with half-wave), C in farads. It is a linear approximation, very good if the ripple is small. Average DC voltage at the filter output, with a bridge. Worked example · Sizing the filter capacitor Power supply with a 220/12 V RMS transformer, a bridge rectifier and a load drawing 500 mA. You want a ripple below 1 V peak to peak. 1. Peak voltage:. 2. Required capacitor, solving the ripple formula: 3. Choose the next higher standard value: 4700 µF (ripple 1.06 V, which is acceptable) or 6800 µF if you want some margin. Working voltage: 25 V at minimum. 4. Resulting DC:. 5. Peak current in the diodes: as the capacitor charges, peaks of several amperes flow for about 1 ms. That is why a bridge for 500 mA of DC is chosen rated 2 A or more. 🔋 Regulated power supply with LM317 Walks through the four stages of a real power supply (transformer → bridge → filter → regulator) with the waveform at each point, live controls and warnings about ripple, margin and dissipation. It is this same topic, animated. ›"},{"t":"Zener diode and regulation","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#zener","x":"07 Zener diode and regulation An ordinary diode is destroyed if it is driven into reverse breakdown. The Zener is made to work precisely there: above its voltage V Z it conducts in reverse and keeps that voltage almost constant even as the current changes. That makes it a voltage reference. Zener regulator equations. The current the load does not draw is taken by the Zener, and that is why the circuit stays stable. Worked example · 5.1 V regulator 12 V input, 1N4733A Zener (, ), 20 mA load. A minimum I Z of 10 mA is chosen so that the Zener stays well within the breakdown region. Power in R S: → a 1/2 W resistor. Critical case: if the load is disconnected, everything goes through the Zener., and. That is well below a watt: the design tolerates having the load removed. Always check this case, because it is where Zeners burn out. Limitation of the Zener regulator It is good for small currents (up to about 50 mA) and wastes energy: it draws the same with a load as without one. For more current a transistor is added as a follower ( next topic ) or an integrated regulator such as the 7805 or LM317 is used."},{"t":"Other diodes you will meet soon","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#otros-diodos","x":"08 Other diodes you will meet soon Type What is special about it Where it is used LED Emits light when conducting. Drop of 1.8 V (red) to 3.4 V (blue/white). Does not tolerate reverse voltage (5 V typical). Indicators. Always with a series resistor. Schottky Drop of only 0.3 V and very fast switching. Switching power supplies, reverse-polarity protection. Varicap Its reverse capacitance depends on the applied voltage. Tuning of radios and TVs. Photodiode Its reverse current increases with light. Sensors, remote controls, fiber optics. Freewheeling diode An ordinary diode used in antiparallel with an inductor. Protects the transistor from the overvoltage a relay generates when it is switched off. Mandatory calculation · LED resistor Red LED (, ) powered from 5 V — the resulting value is rounded to the nearest standard value: Without a resistor, the LED conducts without limit and is destroyed in under a second. There are no exceptions to this rule. 🎨 Resistor color-code calculator For reading the bands of the resistors used in these calculations: 4 and 5 bands, with a live drawing, tolerance and range. ›"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#laboratorio","x":"09 In the lab Lab 1 · Identifying and testing a diode With the multimeter on the diode range (symbol ▷|), measure in both directions. In forward bias it should read between 0.5 and 0.7 V (silicon) or 0.2 to 0.3 V (germanium/Schottky); in reverse, OL or out of range. If it reads low in both directions it is shorted; if it reads OL in both, it is open. Verify that the painted band matches the cathode (the black lead of the multimeter in forward bias). Lab 2 · Plotting the characteristic curve Variable supply from 0 to 5 V, a 1N4007 diode in series with a 100 Ω resistor. Raise the voltage in steps of 0.1 V and note V D (with the voltmeter across the diode) and I (calculated from the drop across R). Plot I versus V D. The knee should appear at around 0.6 to 0.7 V, and it is clear that it is not a straight line: the diode does not obey Ohm's law. Lab 3 · Complete 12 V power supply 220/12 V transformer, W04M bridge, 2200 µF/25 V capacitor, 470 Ω load. With the oscilloscope: View the sine wave at the secondary and measure V p and the frequency. View the bridge output without the capacitor: both rectified half-cycles at 100 Hz. Connect the capacitor and measure the ripple with the oscilloscope on AC coupling and high sensitivity. Compare the measured ripple with the one calculated from. Double the load current (lower the resistance to 235 Ω) and verify that the ripple doubles. Safety The transformer primary is at 220 V: wire it with the equipment unplugged, insulate it with heat-shri"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#errores","x":"10 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The power supply delivers nothing A bridge diode backwards, or the primary fuse open. The capacitor exploded or swelled Reversed polarity, or insufficient working voltage (you forgot to multiply the RMS value by √2). The DC is much lower than expected The filter capacitor is missing: you are measuring the average value (0.637 V p ) instead of the peak. 100 Hz hum in the powered equipment Excessive ripple: capacitor too small for the current drawn. The Zener heats up and burns out The no-load case was not checked, where all the current goes through it. The LED lasted a second No current-limiting resistor, or connected in reverse at more than 5 V. The bridge diodes burn out at power-on The initial capacitor charging peak. Add a start-up resistor or choose a bridge with more current margin."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Semiconductor materials theory","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos#autoevaluacion","x":"11 Self-assessment Why does a semiconductor have exactly 4 valence electrons? Because 4 is right halfway: it forms a crystal with complete covalent bonds (so it barely conducts when pure) but with very little energy a carrier is freed (so it conducts if doped or heated). With fewer electrons it would be a conductor and with more, an insulator. Does an N-type material have a negative electric charge? No. It is neutral: each donor atom contributes a free electron but also an extra proton in its nucleus. The N indicates that the majority carriers are electrons, not that there is a net charge. What is the value of the potential barrier in silicon and what does it mean physically? About 0.7 V. It is the potential generated by the depletion region, which is left with fixed ions of one sign on each side. For forward current to flow, the external source has to overcome that barrier; that is why the diode does not “turn on” until 0.7 V. A 9 V RMS transformer feeds a bridge with a filter. What DC voltage is measured?. Minus the 1.4 V of the two diodes that conduct: minus half the ripple. If the ripple is 1 V, about 10.8 V remain. Why is bridge ripple easier to filter than half-wave rectifier ripple? Because the ripple is at 100 Hz instead of 50 Hz: the capacitor has half the time to discharge between peaks. In the formula, when f doubles the ripple is halved with the same capacitor. The load current of a power supply doubles. What happens to the ripple? It doubles, because V r is direc"},{"t":"Three families, three ways of controlling","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#tres-familias","x":"01 Three families, three ways of controlling Field effect An electric field narrows or widens a channel through which the current flows. The gate draws no current: it behaves like a capacitor. They are used to amplify, to switch and as analog switches. Controlled triggering A four-layer structure that, once triggered, keeps conducting on its own until the current drops. They are used to handle mains power: they do not regulate, they switch. Photoelectric They convert light into an electrical signal or the other way around. Their great virtue is not only detecting: it is isolating electrically two circuits that cannot share a ground."},{"t":"The field-effect transistor","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#fet","x":"02 The field-effect transistor It has three legs — gate (G), drain (D) and source (S)— that play a role similar to base, collector and emitter, but the mechanism is different: the gate voltage modulates the width of the channel through which the current flows, with no current flowing through the gate. The first thing to fix in your mind: normally on or normally off JFETs and depletion-mode MOSFETs: with they conduct. A negative voltage (N channel) has to be applied to cut them off. They are said to work in depletion mode. Enhancement-mode MOSFETs: with they do not conduct. The threshold voltage V GS(th) has to be exceeded for the channel to appear. They are the ones used in switching and the ones inside every CMOS integrated circuit. Shockley equation, valid in the saturation region of the JFET. I DSS is the current with the gate at zero, and V GS(off) the voltage that cuts off the channel. The relationship is quadratic, not linear like that of the bipolar. Worked example · Operating point of a JFET A 2N5457 with and, biased at: With: With: Half a volt on the gate cuts the current in half. And there is an important practical consequence: factory spread is enormous. Within a single batch, I DSS can range from 1 mA to 5 mA. That is why the biasing of a JFET always includes a source resistor with feedback, just as with the bipolar: you never fix the gate at a calculated value and call it done. Bipolar transistor FET Controlled by Base current Gate voltage Input impedance Low: kΩ"},{"t":"Thyristors: the SCR","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#tiristores","x":"03 Thyristors: the SCR The silicon-controlled rectifier is a diode that does not conduct until it is given permission. Inside it has four P-N-P-N layers and it is best understood as two transistors with feedback: a pulse on the gate turns one on, that one turns on the other, and the second keeps the first one on. That is why, once triggered, the gate loses control. The four figures that define an SCR V DRM — repetitive peak voltage it withstands without triggering on its own. For 220 V mains, 400 or 600 V devices are used. I T(RMS) — RMS current it can conduct, with its heat sink. I GT — gate current needed to trigger it. Typically between 5 and 50 mA. I H — holding current: below it the SCR switches off by itself. It is the key to its whole behavior. How an SCR is turned off It is not turned off with the gate. It turns off when the anode current falls below I H. In AC that happens by itself, at every zero crossing, a hundred times per second: that is why thyristors are natural for the mains. In DC, on the other hand, you have to force it with a commutation circuit —a capacitor that reverses the anode voltage for an instant— and that complicates the design quite a bit. It is one of the reasons why in DC a MOSFET or an IGBT is preferred today. Phase control It is the central application. If the thyristor is triggered later in each half-cycle, it conducts for less time and the load receives less power. The angle from the zero crossing to the trigger point is called the firing a"},{"t":"Diac and triac","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#triac","x":"04 Diac and triac The SCR conducts in one direction only: on AC, it lets half a cycle through. The triac solves that: it is two SCRs in antiparallel inside the same package, with a single gate. Its terminals are no longer called anode and cathode but MT1 and MT2, because either can be the positive one. Triac Conducts in both directions: it controls the full cycle. It is triggered by a positive or negative pulse, but not with the same sensitivity in all quadrants: quadrants I and III are the good ones; IV is the worst and should be avoided. It turns off by itself at every zero crossing, just like the SCR. Typical: BT136 (4 A), BT139 (16 A), TIC206. Diac A two-terminal component that does not conduct until a certain voltage (about 32 V) and then fires abruptly, its voltage dropping. It is symmetrical: it does the same in both directions. Its function is to generate a clean, uniform pulse for the triac gate. Without it, the triggering depends on the spread of the triac and the dimmer flickers and shows hysteresis. Typical: DB3. This circuit is at mains potential The entire control circuit, including the knob, is connected to the mains. It has no transformer or insulation. Never measure it with a grounded oscilloscope without an isolation transformer: it produces a dead short through the instrument's ground. For the labs, work with an isolation transformer and, better still, with reduced voltage. Inductive loads: the snubber With a motor or a transformer, the current is out of ph"},{"t":"Photoelectric components","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#fotoelectricos","x":"05 Photoelectric components Component What it does Speed Where it is used LED Converts current into light. Forward drop depends on the color: 1.8 V red, 2.1 V green, 3.2 V blue and white. ns Indicators, lighting, emitter of every optical sensor. Always with a current-limiting resistor. LDR Its resistance drops with light: from 1 MΩ in the dark to 1 kΩ when illuminated. Slow: tens of ms Outdoor lighting, brightness control. Cheap and simple, but slow and not very repeatable. Photodiode Generates a current proportional to the light. It is used reverse -biased. Very fast: ns Fiber optics, remote-control receivers, precise light measurement. Phototransistor A photodiode with the gain of a transistor: much more current. Medium: µs Presence sensors, encoders, optocouplers. Photovoltaic cell Generates voltage: 0.5 V per cell in full light. — Solar power. Optocoupler An LED and a phototransistor facing each other inside one package: they transmit a signal with no electrical contact. µs Isolating the control from the power side, breaking ground loops, protecting a microcontroller. Optotriac An LED and a trigger triac: it drives a power triac with full isolation. — Solid-state relays, controlling mains loads from a micro. MOC3021 and MOC3041. Why the optocoupler is so important Galvanic isolation: 2500 to 5000 V between input and output. A fault on the power side does not reach the control circuit. Breaks ground loops: two distant devices with grounds at different potentials cannot be "},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · JFET curve With a 2N5457, a variable supply on the gate (from 0 to −3 V) and 12 V on the drain through 1 kΩ, measure I D every 0.25 V of V GS. Plot it. Compare with the Shockley equation using the measured I DSS ( ) and the measured V GS(off) ( ). Repeat with another transistor from the same batch: the difference between the two is the most important lesson of the lab. Lab 2 · The MOSFET as a switch With an IRFZ44 driving a 12 V lamp, measure the voltage between drain and source with the gate at 12 V and calculate. Then repeat with the gate at 4 V: the transistor does not saturate, the drop goes up and the package heats up. It is the demonstration of why an ordinary MOSFET is not driven directly from a 3.3 V microcontroller and logic level devices are needed. Lab 3 · SCR latching (at low voltage) With 12 V DC, a small SCR (C106) and a lamp: a pushbutton between the gate and the positive supply turns the lamp on. Release the pushbutton: it stays on. A second pushbutton that shorts the anode to the cathode, or that cuts the supply for an instant, turns it off. This is how latching and holding current are understood without touching the mains. Lab 4 · Phase control, with an isolation transformer Build the dimmer with a small incandescent lamp powered through a step-down isolation transformer. With the oscilloscope across the load, turn the knob and watch how the conducted part of each half-cycle grows. Measure the RMS voltage with a True RMS multimeter and "},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The MOSFET gets very hot when switching Insufficient gate voltage: it works in the linear region instead of saturated. Or it switches slowly and the losses are in the transition. The MOSFET always conducts, even with the gate at zero Floating gate (the 10 kΩ gate-to-source pull-down resistor is missing) or a transistor damaged by static. Two FETs in parallel and one gets hotter Threshold difference between individual devices. In switching it is equalized with individual gate resistors. The JFET amplifies differently on each circuit board I DSS spread. Source feedback is missing from the biasing. The SCR never turns off It is powered from DC: the current never drops below I H. Forced commutation is needed, or use another component. The triac triggers by itself with a motor dV/dt at turn-off from the inductive load. The snubber (100 Ω + 100 nF) is missing. The dimmer does not turn on until a certain point and then jumps Hysteresis of the trigger R-C: the capacitor remains charged from the previous half-cycle. It is corrected with the double R-C network that commercial dimmers have. The AM radio buzzes when the dimmer is at half brightness Interference inherent to phase control. The input filter is missing, or you have to switch to integral-cycle control if the load allows it. The optocoupler stops working over the years The LED's CTR falls over time. Design with margin and not at the current limit. The phototransistor responds to the room "},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Special semiconductors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the essential difference between controlling a bipolar and controlling a FET? The bipolar is controlled with base current and draws drive power continuously; the FET is controlled with gate voltage and in steady state it draws no current, because the gate behaves like a capacitor. A JFET with and biased at: how much current flows?. Half of the cutoff voltage gives a quarter of the current: the relationship is quadratic. Why does a power MOSFET dissipate less than a bipolar at the same current? Because its drop is not a fixed saturation voltage but a very small resistance:. With 10 A and 10 mΩ that is 1 W, versus the 5 W of a bipolar with 0.5 V of V CE(sat). How is an SCR turned off and why does that make it ideal for the mains? It turns off when the anode current falls below the holding current I H. In AC that happens by itself at every zero crossing, a hundred times per second, so the control comes down to deciding when to trigger it in the next half-cycle. With on 220 V, what RMS voltage and what power does a resistive load receive?, and since power goes with the square, 50 %. Half the angle is not half the power: the relationship is not linear. What is the diac for in a dimmer? To generate a well-defined trigger pulse: it does not conduct until about 32 V and then discharges the capacitor onto the gate all at once. Without a diac, the triggering depends on the sensitivity of each triac and the circuit ends up erratic and with hysteresis. Why can "},{"t":"The two domains","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#dominios","x":"01 The two domains Time domain Frequency domain What it shows Amplitude moment by moment How much amplitude there is at each frequency Instrument Oscilloscope Spectrum analyzer Used to see Waveform, timing, overshoot, synchronization Harmonics, interference, occupied bandwidth, noise Question it answers What does the signal look like? What is it made of and what place does it occupy in the spectrum?"},{"t":"Fourier: everything is sine waves","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#fourier","x":"02 Fourier: everything is sine waves The idea Any periodic signal can be written as the sum of sine waves whose frequencies are integer multiples of the fundamental: the harmonics. And any non-periodic signal can be described as a continuous sum of sine waves, which is the Fourier transform. It is not a convenient mathematical approximation: it is the reason a filter distorts a square wave, the reason an amplifier “sounds different” and the reason a signal occupies a particular place in the spectrum. Square wave: only odd harmonics, with amplitude proportional to. The triangle wave also has only odd ones, but they decrease as, and that is why it is much “smoother.” Signal Which harmonics it has How they decrease Consequence Sine wave Only the fundamental — A single line in the spectrum. It is the test signal par excellence. Symmetric square wave Odd: 3, 5, 7… Occupies a great deal of spectrum. It is never transmitted “raw” over radio. Triangle wave Odd A much more concentrated spectrum. Sawtooth All Rich in harmonics: used as a sweep and in sound synthesis. Narrow pulse All, widely spread envelope The narrower the pulse, the wider its spectrum. White noise All equally Flat It is the noise floor that every signal competes against. Example · The spectrum of a 1 kHz square wave Fundamental at 1 kHz, with amplitude. Third harmonic at 3 kHz, one third: 0.42 A (−9.5 dB relative to the fundamental). Fifth at 5 kHz, one fifth: 0.25 A (−14 dB). Seventh at 7 kHz… A 4 kHz low-pass filte"},{"t":"Bandwidth","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#ancho","x":"03 Bandwidth It is how much spectrum a signal occupies, and it is the resource that is bought, allocated and paid for. In communications, everything is ultimately measured against the available bandwidth. Relation between the bandwidth required and the rise time of a pulse. A 1 ns edge demands about 350 MHz of bandwidth. Signal Bandwidth Note Telephone voice 300 – 3400 Hz Enough to understand and recognize a voice. The channel is allocated with 4 kHz including guard bands. High-fidelity audio 20 Hz – 20 kHz The limit of human hearing. AM radio 10 kHz per channel Audio limited to 5 kHz: that is why AM sounds “dull.” FM radio 200 kHz per channel Audio up to 15 kHz plus stereo: that is why it sounds much better. Analog video ≈ 5 MHz An image demands a thousand times more bandwidth than voice. WiFi 20 to 160 MHz The wider the channel, the higher the speed and the fewer channels available. More information requires more bandwidth It is a law, not a technological limitation: Shannon's theorem relates the maximum capacity of a channel to its bandwidth and its signal-to-noise ratio. Capacity can be gained by widening the channel or by improving the signal-to-noise ratio, and nothing else. All digital modulation techniques —QAM, OFDM— are ways of approaching that limit, not of surpassing it."},{"t":"The spectrum analyzer","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#analizador","x":"04 The spectrum analyzer What it shows Frequency on the horizontal axis and amplitude —almost always in dBm — on the vertical. Each signal appears as a line or a bump at its place in the spectrum. Main controls: center frequency and span (which stretch of spectrum is viewed), RBW (resolution bandwidth: how fine the lines look) and reference level. What it is for Seeing whether a transmitter is on its frequency and within its bandwidth. Detecting harmonics and spurious signals that interfere with other services. Finding the source of interference: you see it and track it down. Measuring the noise floor and the signal-to-noise ratio. Checking filters: their response is seen directly. Never connect a transmitter directly to the input The input of an analyzer withstands on the order of +30 dBm as an absolute maximum, and its first stage is expensive. A 5 W transmitter is 37 dBm: it destroys it. Always measure with a suitable attenuator, a directional coupler or an antenna at a distance. The same precaution applies to DC: use a blocking capacitor if there is a risk of superimposed DC voltage."},{"t":"Generating signals","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#generacion","x":"05 Generating signals Method How it works When it is used LC or crystal oscillator A feedback loop with a selective network ( oscillators ) Fixed carriers, clocks, references Voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO) A varactor changes the tank frequency Tuning, frequency modulation, PLL loops PLL synthesizer A VCO locked to a crystal through programmable dividers Any frequency with the stability of the crystal: it is what is inside every modern radio DDS (direct digital synthesis) A phase accumulator, a sine lookup table and a DAC Frequency and phase with millihertz resolution, instantaneous changes. Today's laboratory generators are DDS Software generation The samples are calculated and output through a DAC Software-defined radio: modulation is done with mathematics, not with circuits The PLL in four sentences A phase comparator looks at the difference between a stable reference —a divided crystal— and the VCO output divided by N. The error, filtered, corrects the VCO until the two coincide. Since the output ends up at N times the reference, by changing N in software you choose the frequency: that is how a radio, a handheld radio or a cell phone tunes. The programmable dividers are the same counters from Year 5."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Harmonics exist Generate a 1 kHz square wave and pass it through an RC low-pass filter with adjustable cutoff. Observe on the oscilloscope how it becomes rounded as the cutoff is lowered, and note below what frequency it stops looking like a square wave. If a spectrum analyzer is available, look at the lines at 1, 3, 5 and 7 kHz and measure their relative heights: they should match 1,, and. Lab 2 · Building a square wave by adding sine waves With three generators —or with a program— add sine waves of 1, 3 and 5 kHz with amplitudes 1, and and observe the result. It is Figure 2 done on the bench: you do not get a perfect square wave, but you can clearly see where it is heading. Lab 3 · Bandwidth and rise time Measure the rise time of a fast pulse with the oscilloscope and calculate the bandwidth required with. Compare it with the oscilloscope's own rated bandwidth: if they are similar, the instrument is limiting the measurement and the real edge is faster than what you see. Lab 4 · Exploring the spectrum With an inexpensive SDR receiver and its software, view as a waterfall the spectrum of the FM band, the aviation band and the 433 MHz ISM band. Identify signals, measure their approximate width and observe the noise floor. It is the cheapest way to have a spectrum analyzer in the classroom."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The square wave looks rounded The bandwidth of the cable, the circuit or the oscilloscope itself cuts off the high harmonics. The transmitter interferes with other services Harmonics: the output must be filtered. They are seen immediately on the spectrum analyzer. A digital signal is transmitted unfiltered Fast edges occupy an enormous spectrum. All digital modulation is filtered before transmission. Trying to get more speed in the same channel Shannon rules: without more bandwidth or a better signal-to-noise ratio, there is no more capacity. The analyzer was damaged Power above its limit was applied. Always use an attenuator. Lines appear that do not exist Intermodulation products generated inside the analyzer itself by excess level. Lower the input level and see whether they disappear."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Signal generation and processing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does Fourier analysis say about a periodic signal? That it can be written as a sum of sine waves whose frequencies are integer multiples of the fundamental: the harmonics, each with its own amplitude and phase. Which harmonics does a symmetric square wave have, and how do they decrease? Only the odd ones —3, 5, 7…— with amplitude proportional to. The triangle wave also has only odd ones, but they decrease as. A 2 kHz square wave goes through a 5 kHz filter. What is left? The 2 kHz fundamental and nothing else, because the third harmonic is at 6 kHz. At the output you see a sine wave, not a square wave. What bandwidth is needed for a pulse with 10 ns edges?. If the instrument or the channel has less, the edge looks slower than it really is. Why does AM radio sound worse than FM? Because its channel is only 10 kHz wide and limits the audio to about 5 kHz, whereas FM has 200 kHz and transmits up to 15 kHz in stereo. FM is also much less sensitive to amplitude noise. What does a spectrum analyzer show that an oscilloscope does not? What frequencies the signal is composed of: harmonics, spurious signals, interference, occupied bandwidth and noise floor. The oscilloscope shows the shape, not the composition. What is the fundamental precaution when using a spectrum analyzer? Do not exceed the maximum input level —on the order of +30 dBm— or apply DC voltage to it. Measure with an attenuator, directional coupler or antenna, never by connecting a transmitter di"},{"t":"Vocabulary and characteristics","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#vocabulario","x":"01 Vocabulary and characteristics Term What it means Transducer Converts one physical quantity into another, usually electrical. Sensor The transducer plus its signal conditioning: it delivers a usable signal. Detector Sensor with an on/off output: there is a part or there isn't. Range Between which values it measures. Outside it, the reading means nothing. Resolution The smallest change it can distinguish. Accuracy How far it deviates from the true value. It is not the same as resolution. Repeatability Whether it measures the same thing under the same conditions. In automation it usually matters more than accuracy. Hysteresis The difference between the switch-on point and the switch-off point. It prevents chatter. Response time How long it takes to reflect the change. It limits the speed of the machine. Repeatability before accuracy A detector that always switches 2 mm early is not a problem: you correct the bracket position once and that's it. One that sometimes switches at 4 mm and sometimes at 6 mm is useless, even if on average it is perfect. In a machine, what you have to demand of a sensor is that it always do the same thing."},{"t":"Proximity detectors","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#presencia","x":"02 Proximity detectors Detector Detects Typical range Precautions Inductive Metals only 2 to 15 mm The rated range is for steel: with aluminum or bronze it drops to a third or a half. Immune to dirt and water. Capacitive Almost everything: metal, plastic, liquids, grains 3 to 25 mm Detects through non-metallic walls: useful for sensing the level inside a tank. Sensitive to moisture and dirt. Through-beam optical Any opaque object Up to 50 m Emitter and receiver face each other: it is the most reliable and has the longest range, but both sides have to be wired. Retroreflective optical Opaque objects Up to 10 m Uses a prismatic reflector. A very shiny object can fool it. Diffuse optical Objects, depending on their color 10 cm to 2 m All in one housing. The range depends on the color and gloss of the object: matte black barely reflects. Ultrasonic Any material, even transparent 10 cm to 6 m Color doesn't affect it. Wind, temperature and soft surfaces that absorb sound do. Magnetic (reed or Hall) Magnets mm to cm The classic position detector for the rod of a pneumatic cylinder. The inductive sensor's reduction factor When the datasheet says “Sn = 8 mm,” that is with a standard-size steel plate. With other metals you have to multiply by the correction factor: stainless steel ≈ 0.7, bronze and brass ≈ 0.4, aluminum ≈ 0.35, copper ≈ 0.3. An 8 mm detector on aluminum detects at less than 3 mm, and that is the most common cause of “the sensor doesn't see the part.”"},{"t":"How they are connected","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#conexion","x":"03 How they are connected PNP and NPN Almost all industrial detectors are three-wire: two for power (24 V and common) and one for the signal. PNP: when activated it drives the output positive. It is the standard in Europe and in most PLCs: the input is “sinking.” NPN: when activated it pulls the output to common. Usual in Asian equipment. They are not interchangeable: an NPN sensor on an input set up for PNP simply doesn't work, although nothing breaks. NO and NC Normally open conducts when it detects; normally closed conducts when it does not detect. In safety circuits NC is always used: if the cable is cut or the sensor breaks, the circuit is left open and the system reads it as “detects,” which is the safe condition: it stops. It is the same criterion as the stop pushbutton seen in electrical installations. Analog signals: why 4 to 20 mA It is a current, so the drop in the cable doesn't affect it: it can be run for hundreds of meters without error. Zero is at 4 mA, not at zero: if the current falls to 0 mA, the system knows that the cable was cut or the sensor died. This is called a live zero and it is its great advantage over 0-10 V. It is converted to voltage with a resistor: with 250 Ω, 4-20 mA becomes 1-5 V, ready for the A/D converter. 0-10 V is simpler and cheaper, but loses accuracy with distance and doesn't distinguish “real zero” from “cut cable.” Example · Converting a transmitter's signal A 0 to 10 bar pressure transmitter delivers 4-20 mA, read with 250 Ω and a"},{"t":"Position, speed and acceleration","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#posicion","x":"04 Position, speed and acceleration Sensor What it delivers Note Potentiometer Voltage proportional to position Cheap and absolute, but it wears out and has a limited range. Incremental encoder A, B and Z pulses It counts pulses: if power is cut, it loses its reference and has to return to the zero point. With both channels and all four edges the resolution is quadrupled. Absolute encoder One code per position It knows where it is as soon as it is switched on. More expensive; it communicates over a bus. Resolver Sinusoidal signals Very rugged: withstands temperature, vibration and dirt. Demanding industrial motors. Tachogenerator Voltage proportional to speed Analog and simple. Today it is usually replaced by counting the encoder pulses. MEMS accelerometer Acceleration on one to three axes Machine vibration, tilt, shock detection. It is the same sensor that cell phones have. Example · Resolution of an encoder A 1000 pulses per revolution encoder mounted on a shaft with a pinion that advances 50 mm per revolution: With a single channel: per pulse. Counting all four edges of A and B: 4000 counts per revolution → 0.0125 mm. Turning at 3000 rpm, the pulse frequency is; counting in quadrature, 200 kHz. The microcontroller's counter has to be able to handle that: it is the reason why many microcontrollers come with hardware encoder inputs."},{"t":"Actuators","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#actuadores","x":"05 Actuators Technology Force Control When to choose it Electric motors, servos, steppers Medium Excellent When you need to position accurately, regulate speed or log what happened. Solenoid Medium, short stroke On/off Valves, latches, strikers. Simple and fast. Pneumatic High On/off, or proportional with expensive valves Fast repetitive motions, environments with a spark hazard, cleanliness. Cheap to install if compressed air is already available. Hydraulic Very high Proportional Presses, lifts, heavy machinery. Pressures of hundreds of bar: dangerous and messy if there are leaks. Pneumatics: the essentials Single-acting cylinder: the air pushes in one direction and a spring returns it. Double-acting: the air acts in both directions and it is the most widely used. 5/2 solenoid valve: five ports and two positions, the typical valve for controlling a double-acting cylinder. Flow controls on the exhaust to adjust the speed: never on the inlet, or the motion comes out jerky. The force is pressure × area: a 32 mm diameter cylinder at 6 bar pushes, about 49 kgf. Each cylinder carries magnetic detectors at its ends: they are the inputs that tell the control the motion is finished."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Actual range of an inductive sensor Use a caliper to measure at what distance an inductive detector switches with a steel plate, an aluminum one and a bronze one, and with a part smaller than the standard one. Compare with the catalog figure and calculate the reduction factor of each material. Lab 2 · PNP, NPN, NO and NC Wire the four cases with an LED and a resistor, and use the multimeter to check what voltage appears on the signal wire in each state. Then connect an NPN sensor to an input configured for PNP and check that it doesn't work, without anything breaking. Lab 3 · The 4 to 20 mA loop Build the loop with a transmitter and a 250 Ω resistor. Measure the current and the voltage for several values of the quantity. Then add 100 m of cable (a reel) and measure again: the current doesn't change. Repeat the experiment with a 0-10 V signal and compare the error. Lab 4 · Encoder and direction of rotation With an incremental encoder and the two-channel oscilloscope, look at A and B turning one way and the other: the phase relationship reverses. Count pulses with a microcontroller and display position and direction. Cut and restore the power: the count is lost, and that is where you understand what homing (the reference search) is for."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The inductive sensor doesn't detect the part The part is aluminum or bronze, or smaller than the standard one: the actual range is much shorter. The sensor gives no signal even though its LED lights up PNP output connected to an NPN input or vice versa. The diffuse detector sees some parts and not others It depends on color and gloss. With dark parts you have to switch to through-beam or retroreflective. The capacitive sensor switches on by itself Moisture, dirt or condensation on the active face. Readjust the sensitivity and protect it. The analog signal varies when the sensor is moved farther away It is 0-10 V and the drop in the cable affects it. Switch to 4-20 mA. The system doesn't distinguish “zero” from “cut cable” Live zero is missing: with 4-20 mA, 0 mA is always a fault. The encoder loses position every time the power goes out It is incremental: you have to do homing, or use an absolute one. Two inductive detectors close together affect each other Mutual interference: respect the minimum distance or use shielded versions. The pneumatic cylinder arrives with a bang Flow controls badly placed: they go on the exhaust, not the inlet. And end-of-stroke cushions are missing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why does repeatability matter more than accuracy in automation? Because a constant offset is corrected just once by adjusting the position or the calibration, whereas a random scatter makes the machine behave differently in each cycle and no adjustment can fix it. What does an inductive sensor detect and how does it differ from a capacitive one? The inductive one detects only metals and is very immune to dirt. The capacitive one detects almost any material —including liquids and grains, through a non-metallic wall— but it is affected by moisture and dirt. An 8 mm inductive sensor detects aluminum. At what distance? With a reduction factor of approximately 0.35: less than 3 mm. The catalog value corresponds to a standard steel plate. What is the advantage of the live zero of the 4 to 20 mA signal? That 0 mA is not a valid value: if it shows up, it is a cut cable or a dead sensor. With 0-10 V, a real zero and a fault look exactly the same. With what resistor is 4-20 mA converted into 1-5 V? With 250 Ω: and. Why are safety sensors normally used normally closed? Because with a cut cable or a failed sensor, the circuit is left open and the system takes the safe condition: it stops. With NO, a fault would be mistaken for “all in order.” How does an incremental encoder determine the direction of rotation? With two channels shifted by 90°: if A changes before B, it turns one way; if it changes after, the other way. Counting all four edges also quadruples the resolu"},{"t":"Two standards living side by side","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#dos-normas","x":"01 Two standards living side by side There are two families of symbols and you run into both every day: European schematics and North American manuals. You do not have to pick one: you have to recognize both, because any project mixes datasheets from both origins. Component IEC 60617 / IRAM ANSI / IEEE 315 Resistor Empty rectangle Zigzag Inductor Semicircles or filled rectangle Turns (loops) Logic gates Rectangle with an internal symbol (&, ≥1, =1) Distinctive shapes (D, shield, crescent) Fuse Rectangle with a line running through it Curved stroke or zigzag Earth / ground Three decreasing strokes Same, but with a distinction between signal ground, chassis and earth In practice, almost everyone draws “American” gates Even though IEC is the current standard, the distinctive shapes of gates—the D of the AND, the shield of the OR—are still used everywhere, even in Europe, because they can be told apart at a glance even when the drawing is small or poorly printed. Electronic CAD programs ship with both libraries. What matters is not mixing them within the same drawing."},{"t":"The symbols you should recognize without thinking","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#catalogo","x":"02 The symbols you should recognize without thinking Details that tell diodes apart Rectifier: triangle with a straight bar. Zener: the bar has two angled “tabs.” It operates reverse biased. Schottky: the bar is shaped like a capital S. LED: two outgoing arrows. The photodiode has the arrows incoming: one emits and the other receives. Varicap diode: bar with a capacitor symbol. Details that tell transistors apart NPN: the emitter arrow points out of the transistor (Not Pointing iN toward the base). PNP: the arrow points in. JFET: gate with an arrow, continuous channel. MOSFET: gate separated by a gap (the oxide); channel in three segments if it is enhancement-mode and continuous if it is depletion-mode. Almost all power MOSFETs are drawn with their body diode, which physically exists and is sometimes put to use. The arrow mnemonic In every symbol with an arrow—diodes, transistors, thyristors—the arrow indicates the conventional direction of current when the device conducts. With that one idea you can deduce the polarity of almost any component without memorizing anything."},{"t":"Reference designators","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#designadores","x":"03 Reference designators Each component in a schematic carries a unique label: R12, C3, U1. It is not a personal convention: it is standardized, and it is what links the schematic to the bill of materials, to the board and to the silkscreen printed on it. Letter Component Letter Component R Resistor Q Transistor C Capacitor U Integrated circuit L Inductor J Female connector (jack) D Diode, LED P Male connector (plug) T Transformer SW / S Switch or pushbutton F Fuse K Relay or contactor Y Crystal or oscillator M Motor RV / VR Potentiometer or preset TP Test point Numbering rules that save hours A designator is unique across the whole board: there cannot be two R7s. Number from top to bottom and from left to right, in the order they are read. Almost all programs do it on their own with a renumbering command. In large designs, number by blocks: 1xx the power supply, 2xx the input, 3xx the output. Never renumber once the board is manufactured and documented: the silkscreen, the bill of materials and the assembly instructions would fall out of sync."},{"t":"How values are written","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#valores","x":"04 How values are written Next to the designator goes the value, and there too there are conventions that prevent costly confusion. Written Means Why 4k7 4.7 kΩ The letter replaces the decimal point: if the drawing is photocopied badly, the point is lost and the value changes tenfold. 2R2 2.2 Ω The R acts as the decimal point for values below one kilohm. 100n 100 nF For capacitors the unit is understood: always farads. 1u o 1µ 1 µF The u is used when the keyboard or the program does not accept µ. 10u/25V 10 µF, 25 V In electrolytics the voltage rating is as important as the capacitance. 1N4007 A type, not a value Semiconductors and integrated circuits are identified by their manufacturer part number. From the symbol to the real component The schematic says “R5 = 4k7.” On the bench you have to find the right resistor: With the color code: yellow-violet-red for 4700 Ω, plus the tolerance band. On SMD parts, the three digits 472 mean 47 followed by 2 zeros. In the four-digit code, 4701 is 470 with one zero. The prefixes and their equivalents are reviewed in the SI converter."},{"t":"Digital symbols","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#digitales","x":"05 Digital symbols They already appeared in combinational logic; here they matter as a drawing convention, and there are three details that change the meaning: The small circle A small circle on an input or output means negation. It turns the AND into a NAND and, on an input, indicates that the signal is active-low. The bar over the name RESET with a bar over it—or written /RESET or RESET# —indicates that the signal acts when it is at zero. Mistaking it leaves a circuit permanently in reset. The small triangle On a clock input it indicates that it acts on an edge. If it also has a circle, on the falling edge. On an output, three-state is shown with an inverted triangle (▽) and open collector or open drain with an underlined diamond (◇ with a line below). ICs are not drawn like the chip In a schematic, a microcontroller or a dual op amp is almost never drawn with its pins in physical order. They are split into functional blocks —U1A and U1B for the two halves of a dual op amp, or a separate power block with all the V CC and GND pins—and each pin is placed wherever the schematic is clearest. The pin number is written next to it, and it is the only thing that matters for routing. Anyone looking in the schematic for “pin 1 at the top left” is reading the wrong drawing."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · From the component to the symbol Put together a tray with twenty varied real components. Each student identifies them, draws their standard symbol, and writes down the designator that would correspond to each and its value. Everyone corrects together. It is the lab that fixes the vocabulary of the topic. Lab 2 · From the symbol to the circuit board With a scrap circuit board and its schematic (or that of an equivalent circuit), locate on the board five components marked in the schematic, using the silkscreen. Then measure them in circuit and verify that they match the indicated value. Lab 3 · Translating between standards Take a schematic in ANSI symbols from a datasheet and redraw it in IEC, or the other way around. Check that the circuit is exactly the same. It also serves to train the reading of imported documentation. Lab 4 · Your own library In the electronic CAD program you will use, create a new symbol from scratch—for example a sensor that is not in the library—with its pins correctly numbered and its default designator. It is the first step of the next topic."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The transistor was mounted with the emitter and collector swapped The symbol was read without looking at the arrow, or it was assumed that the order of the pins in the symbol is that of the package. It never is: check the datasheet. The electrolytic capacitor blew up Reversed polarity: in the symbol, the straight bar is the positive and the curved (or hatched) one the negative. The circuit stays in reset all the time An active-low signal treated as active-high. The bar over the name or the circle on the pin indicated it. The Zener does not regulate Mounted forward biased. The Zener operates reverse biased, and its symbol is recognized by the tabs on the bar. Two components with the same designator Copying and pasting a block without renumbering. The program detects it in the electrical rules check. The bill of materials does not match the board The designators were renumbered after the silkscreen was generated. A capacitor of the correct capacitance was bought and it does not fit or fails The working voltage and the type (ceramic, polyester, electrolytic) were not checked: the symbol does not tell them apart, the written value does."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electronic component symbols","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment How do you tell an NPN from a PNP in the symbol? By the emitter arrow: in the NPN it points out of the transistor and in the PNP it points in. The arrow marks the conventional direction of current. What does the small circle on a gate output mean? Negation. An AND with a circle on the output is a NAND. On an input, it indicates that the signal is active-low. What component is “C7 100n/63V”? The seventh capacitor in the circuit, of 100 nF and 63 V working voltage. From the value and the voltage, almost certainly polyester or ceramic. Why is it written 4k7 and not 4.7k? Because the decimal point is easily lost when photocopying, scanning or printing at low quality, and 4.7 kΩ would end up being read as 47 kΩ. The prefix letter takes the place of the point. What is the difference between the LED symbol and the photodiode symbol? The direction of the arrows: on the LED they point out (it emits light) and on the photodiode they point in (it receives it). What does a small triangle on the clock input of a flip-flop indicate? That the input acts on an edge and not on a level. If there is also a circle, the edge is falling. Can you rely on the symbol following the pin order of the package? No, and it almost never does. The symbol is drawn so that the schematic can be understood; the correspondence with the package is given by the pin number and, in the board design, by the footprint associated with the component. Which designator letter corresponds to a relay, a cr"},{"t":"What a simulator does inside","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#que-hace","x":"01 What a simulator does inside All circuit simulators descend from SPICE, written at Berkeley in 1973. The procedure has not changed: a system of equations is set up with Kirchhoff’s laws, solved numerically to find the voltages at all the nodes, and the calculation is repeated for each point in time or frequency. What goes in and what comes out In: the netlist —the same data as the schematic — plus a model for each component and a directive that says which analysis to run. Out: voltages and currents at each node, for each time or frequency point calculated. What does not go in: the length of the traces, the wiring capacitance, the real temperature, the tolerance of the components or the ambient noise. None of that exists for the simulator unless it is added by hand."},{"t":"Types of analysis","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#analisis","x":"02 Types of analysis Choosing the right analysis is half the work. Each one answers a different question. Analysis Question it answers Typical example Operating point (.op) At what voltages and currents does the circuit settle at rest? Checking the biasing of a transistor before looking at anything else. DC sweep (.dc) How does the output change if I sweep a source or a resistor? Plotting the characteristic curve of a diode or a transistor. Transient (.tran) What happens over time? Charging of a capacitor, start-up of an oscillator, waveform of a power supply. Frequency (.ac) How does it respond at each frequency? Response curve of a filter, bandwidth of an amplifier. Parametric (.step) What happens if this component has a different value? Sweeping R from 1 k to 100 k and viewing the ten overlaid curves. Monte Carlo / tolerances What happens with the real spread of component values? Checking that the circuit works with 5% resistors and 20% capacitors. Noise (.noise) How much noise does it generate itself? Design of the input stage of an instrument. The analysis almost nobody runs and you always should Monte Carlo. A circuit that works with nominal values can fail in production, where each resistor sits at some random point within its tolerance. Simulating a hundred random combinations shows whether the design has margin or works “by luck.” It is the difference between a prototype that works and a product that works."},{"t":"Models: that is where everything is","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#modelos","x":"03 Models: that is where everything is A resistor is modeled with one number. A transistor, with more than twenty parameters. The quality of the simulation does not depend on the program: it depends on the model. Manufacturer model Published alongside the datasheet, fitted to real measurements. It is the one to look for every time: almost all serious manufacturers offer it for download. Generic model The “NPN transistor” that comes with the program. It serves to understand a concept, not to predict the behavior of a specific circuit. Ideal model Perfect source, ideal op amp, switch with no resistance. Useful for isolating an idea; dangerous if you forget you are using it. What models do not include The inductance of the leads and of the traces. The capacitance between traces and to the ground plane. The ESR of real capacitors, which increases with age ( impedance measurements ). Heating: the model usually calculates at a fixed 27 °C unless told otherwise. Coupling between stages through the shared power supply. All of that has to be added by hand when it matters: a source model with its internal resistance, an inductance in series with each filter capacitor, a 0.1 Ω resistance representing the trace. A good simulation engineer is, above all, someone who knows which parasitics to add."},{"t":"Simulation versus reality","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#realidad","x":"04 Simulation versus reality Observed difference Where it comes from The oscillator frequency does not match Tolerance of R and C, and parasitic capacitances of the board and of the oscilloscope probe. The edge has overshoot and ringing Wiring inductance resonating with the input capacitance. It was not in the model. The real gain is lower The generic model had a higher β than the real transistor, or the loading of the instrument has an effect. The circuit oscillates and in the simulator it does not Feedback through the shared power supply or through coupling between traces: decoupling is missing. It heats up more than expected The simulation did not take temperature and its effects on the parameters into account. The regulator goes into oscillation The ESR of the real output capacitor is outside the range the IC requires. In the model, the capacitor was ideal. The right method Calculate first, even if only approximately. Whoever does not know what to expect cannot tell that the simulation is wrong. Simulate to verify the calculation, view waveforms and explore variants. Prototype and measure with real instruments. Compare the three results. If they differ, there is something that was not understood, and that is the most valuable part of the learning. A simulation result that nobody questioned is as unreliable as a measurement made with an uncalibrated instrument. Convergence problems When the simulator reports “analysis failed” or “singular matrix,” it is almost always one o"},{"t":"Tools and virtual measurements","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#herramientas","x":"05 Tools and virtual measurements Program Strength Note LTspice Full SPICE, free, fast and widely used Excellent for switching power supplies and analog work. Spartan interface, but it is the free standard. Falstad Browser-based simulator, with the current animated over the circuit Unbeatable for seeing what happens; it does not replace a serious SPICE. Proteus Simulates the circuit with the microcontroller running the program Widely used in technical education: it lets you test the firmware before having the board. Multisim Virtual instruments very similar to real ones Good for practicing measurements; paid license. Qucs-S / Ngspice Free, with a SPICE engine Open, cross-platform alternatives. Virtual instruments are ideal, and that is misleading The simulator’s oscilloscope has infinite bandwidth, infinite input impedance and does not load the circuit. The virtual multimeter is exact and has no burden voltage. Measuring in the simulator is, by definition, easier than measuring on the bench, and that is why lab practice cannot be replaced by simulation: what you learn by measuring is precisely how to deal with instruments that are not ideal, as seen in digital measuring instruments. Error margins: how to report a simulated result An honest simulation report states: which model was used for each active component, with what tolerances the analysis was run, at what temperature, and which parasitics were included. “The circuit gives 4.98 V” is not a result; “with 1% resistors and"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · The three analyses on an R-C circuit With and: run the operating point, a transient with a square-wave input and a frequency sweep. Verify that the time constant is, that the output reaches 63% in one τ, and that the cutoff in the sweep falls at 1592 Hz. The three results describe the same circuit. Lab 2 · Simulated versus measured Build that same R-C circuit on the bench and measure it with the generator and the oscilloscope. Overlay the two curves. Note the differences and look for the cause of each: capacitor tolerance, probe capacitance, generator output resistance. Lab 3 · Tolerances Run a Monte Carlo analysis varying R ±5% and C ±20% and observe how far the cutoff frequency shifts. Compare with what is measured on three different units of the same circuit built by different classmates. Lab 4 · Provoke a convergence failure Deliberately build a circuit with no ground and check the error. Then leave a node floating and fix it with a 1 MΩ resistor. It is the quickest way to learn to diagnose the most frequent simulator problem. Lab 5 · Compare with the published results Simulate the astable of the 555 with the same R and C as the site’s interactive simulator, and compare the three results: the formula, the SPICE simulation and the measurement on the built circuit. All three should agree within the tolerance of the components; if not, there is something to investigate."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause “Singular matrix” or failed analysis The ground node is missing, or there is a node with no DC path to ground. The transient takes forever Time step too small for the requested duration, or signals of very different frequencies in the same analysis. The circuit simulates perfectly and oscillates on the board Parasitics are missing: wiring inductance, decoupling, source resistance. The simulated gain does not match the measured one Generic model instead of the manufacturer’s model. The frequency curve comes out flat and meaningless An.ac analysis was run without a declared AC signal source, or with the circuit improperly biased. Everything works in simulation on the first try A reason for suspicion: almost certainly something ideal is being simulated. Review the models. The result changes a lot when the time step is changed The analysis did not really converge: adjust the tolerances and the maximum step."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What exactly does a SPICE-type simulator solve? The circuit’s Kirchhoff equations, numerically, for each point in time or frequency, using a mathematical model of each component. Which analysis is used to obtain a filter’s response curve? The frequency analysis (.ac): it sweeps the source frequency and plots the magnitude and phase of the output. What is a Monte Carlo analysis for? To see what happens with the real spread of component values: it runs many simulations with values drawn at random within their tolerances and shows the distribution of results. It is what distinguishes a design with margin from one that works by luck. Why can a circuit simulate well and oscillate on the board? Because on the board there are elements the model did not have: inductance and capacitance of the traces, impedance of the shared power supply, coupling between stages. The positive feedback that causes the oscillation was not in the simulated circuit. What is the most common cause of an analysis failing to converge? A missing ground node. SPICE needs a voltage reference; without it the system of equations has no unique solution. Why is the generic model of a transistor not enough? Because its parameters —gain, capacitances, saturation voltages— do not correspond to any real component. It serves to illustrate a concept, not to predict the behavior of the circuit you are going to build. The simulator gives 4.98 V. Is that a sufficient result? No. It must also state with whi"},{"t":"Three parameters, three families","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#idea","x":"01 Three parameters, three families A sinusoidal carrier is written. There are exactly three things that can be modified in it, and every modulation that exists comes from them. Amplitude · AM The carrier envelope follows the signal. Simple to generate and to detect; noise adds directly to the information. Frequency · FM The carrier is squeezed and stretched. The amplitude is constant, so amplitude noise can be clipped off: much better quality, at the cost of more bandwidth. Phase · PM The carrier is advanced or delayed. It is a close relative of FM —one is the derivative of the other— and it is the basis of all modern digital modulations."},{"t":"Amplitude modulation","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#am","x":"02 Amplitude modulation 🔬 Instrument simulator The four amplitude modulations built by hand on a laboratory bench: two generators, a multiplier, an adder and two filters connected with BNC cables. It produces double-sideband with suppressed carrier, AM by adding the carrier back, and the two single sidebands by filtering out one or the other. You compare them on two oscilloscopes and measure their bandwidth on the spectrum analyzer. › The second form is the one used in the lab: you measure the two extremes of the envelope on the oscilloscope and calculate. Example · Index and power A 100 W carrier modulated at 80 % by a 5 kHz tone: Bandwidth:, split into two sidebands around the carrier. Total power:. Of those 132 W, the information travels only in the sidebands: 32 W. 76 % of the power is spent on the carrier, which carries no information. Even with, the maximum without distortion, the sidebands take barely 33 %. That inefficiency is the big problem of AM, and what gave rise to its variants. Variant What it transmits BW Where it is used Full AM Carrier + 2 sidebands Broadcasting: a very cheap receiver, with a single detector diode. DSB-SC Only the 2 sidebands Saves the carrier power, but requires coherent detection. This is what a product modulator does. SSB A single sideband f m Amateur radio, marine, HF aviation: half the bandwidth and all the power useful. VSB One sideband and a remnant of the other Intermediate Analog television: a compromise between saving bandwidth and"},{"t":"Frequency modulation","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#fm","x":"03 Frequency modulation β is the modulation index and the second expression is Carson's rule, which gives the bandwidth actually occupied. Example · The two kinds of FM Broadcast FM (wideband): deviation, audio up to... That is why FM channels are spaced 200 kHz apart. Narrowband FM (handheld radios and utility services):, voice up to 3 kHz..: it fits in a 25 kHz channel with margin. More deviation gives a better signal-to-noise ratio and poorer use of the spectrum. That is the central trade-off of FM. Why FM is so immune to noise Atmospheric and industrial noise mostly affect the amplitude. Since in FM the amplitude carries no information, the receiver clips it with a limiter and the noise goes away. There is also the capture effect: given two signals on the same frequency, the receiver keeps the stronger one and the other disappears. In AM you would hear both superimposed. And pre-emphasis and de-emphasis are used: the highs are boosted when transmitting and attenuated when receiving, so the high-frequency noise drops even further."},{"t":"Comparison","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#comparacion","x":"04 Comparison AM FM SSB Bandwidth f m Power efficiency Low (≤ 33 %) High: constant amplitude Highest Noise immunity Poor Very good Medium Receiver complexity Minimal Medium High: the carrier has to be reinserted Range at low power Medium Medium The best Used in AM broadcasting, aviation FM broadcasting, handheld radios, TV audio HF: amateur radio, marine Why aviation still uses AM Precisely because of what in broadcasting is a defect: in AM, if two aircraft transmit at the same time, the controller hears both signals superimposed and knows there was a collision of transmissions. With FM, the capture effect would make one disappear completely and nobody would notice. In aviation safety, that is decisive."},{"t":"Digital modulations at a glance","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#digitales","x":"05 Digital modulations at a glance These are the same three families applied to a signal that takes only discrete values. They are covered in Year 7; here it is enough to recognize them. Acronym What varies Everyday example ASK / OOK Amplitude: the carrier is either present or not Gate remote controls, 433 MHz sensors. The telegraph and Morse code are its direct ancestors. FSK Two frequencies, one per symbol Old modems, radio beacons, LoRa (with a chirp variant) PSK The phase jumps between fixed values Satellite communications, WiFi in its robust modes QAM Amplitude and phase at the same time Digital TV, fast WiFi, cable modems: 64-QAM carries 6 bits per symbol"},{"t":"How demodulation works","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#demodulacion","x":"06 How demodulation works For AM Envelope detector: a diode, a capacitor and a resistor. The constant must be much greater than the carrier period and much smaller than that of the modulating signal. Product detector: multiplies by a local carrier. It is mandatory for DSB and SSB, and it is what an MC1496 does. For FM Discriminator or ratio detector: converts frequency variations into amplitude variations and then detects them. PLL: it locks onto the carrier and its correction voltage is the demodulated signal. It is the modern solution and the most stable. Before all that, always a limiter, which is where the noise immunity comes from. The superheterodyne receiver No receiver demodulates directly at the received frequency. All of them translate the signal to a fixed intermediate frequency —455 kHz in AM, 10.7 MHz in FM— by mixing it with a local oscillator, and do the filtering and demodulation there. The advantage is enormous: the most selective filter is designed just once, for a fixed frequency, and tuning is simply a matter of shifting the local oscillator. It is the architecture of practically every radio for a century."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Lab 1 · Measuring the modulation index With a generator that has AM modulation, observe the envelope on the oscilloscope and measure V max and V min. Calculate m with the formula and compare it with the value set on the generator. Raise the modulation until it goes past 1 and observe the overmodulation: the envelope “crosses over.” Lab 2 · Envelope detector Build the detector with a 1N4148, 10 nF and 10 kΩ, and recover the modulating signal from an AM signal. Change the capacitor to 1 nF and to 100 nF and observe the two defects: with too little filtering there is carrier left superimposed; with too much, the detector does not follow the peaks of the modulating signal and distortion appears. Lab 3 · AM versus FM with noise Modulate the same signal in AM and in FM and add noise to it with a generator. Listen to and look at the two demodulated outputs: AM degrades gradually, FM stays clean until it suddenly falls apart below the threshold. That behavior —the threshold effect — is also characteristic of FM. Lab 4 · Spectrum of an AM signal With a spectrum analyzer or an SDR receiver, look at a single-tone AM signal: the carrier appears and two symmetric lines separated by the frequency of the modulating signal. Change the tone and see how they move apart. Measure the relative height of the sidebands and compare it with the modulation index."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The AM sounds distorted and bothers the neighboring channels Overmodulation: m greater than 1. The envelope detector distorts the peaks constant too large: it cannot follow the modulating signal. The carrier is heard superimposed on the audio too small: not enough carrier filtering. The FM takes up more channel than expected Excessive deviation. The amplitude of the modulating signal has to be limited before the modulator. The SSB sounds like Donald Duck The carrier reinserted in the receiver is off: the BFO has to be adjusted to within tens of hertz. With two transmitters on the same frequency only one is heard FM capture effect. It is an advantage in broadcasting and a problem in safety services. The receiver picks up a station at two points on the dial Image frequency: not enough selectivity ahead of the superheterodyne mixer."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Modulation systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Which three parameters of a carrier can be modulated? Amplitude, frequency and phase. From them come AM, FM and PM, and their digital equivalents ASK, FSK and PSK. In an AM signal, a maximum peak of 8 V and a minimum of 2 V are measured. What is the index?, that is, 60 %. A 200 W carrier modulated at 100 %: what are the total power and the useful power?. The sidebands carry 100 W: 33 % of the total. The rest is spent on the carrier, which carries no information. What concrete advantage does SSB have over AM? It takes up half the bandwidth and all the power goes to information: with the same transmitter power, the useful range is much greater. FM with and: what are the index and the bandwidth?. according to Carson's rule. Why is FM more immune to noise than AM? Because the information is in the frequency and not in the amplitude: the receiver clips the amplitude with a limiter and removes with it almost all the noise, which is essentially amplitude noise. What is the capture effect? In FM, given two signals on the same frequency the receiver keeps the stronger one and the weaker one disappears. In AM you would hear both mixed. Why does aviation use AM and not FM? Because if two aircraft transmit simultaneously, in AM you hear both and the controller detects the overlap. With FM, the capture effect would hide one transmission completely. What does a superheterodyne receiver do, and why? It translates the received signal to a fixed intermediate frequency by mi"},{"t":"What a substation is and what types exist","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#tipos","x":"01 What a substation is and what types exist A substation is the set of equipment where voltage is changed or regulated and from which energy is distributed: transformers, switching and protective devices, busbars and the instruments that measure and watch over all of it. Substations are told apart by what they do: Step-up, at the power plant: raises the generator voltage so the energy can be transmitted (see Electrical energy ). Transformer substation (ET): steps down from high voltage (132 kV, for example) to medium voltage (33 or 13.2 kV) and feeds several feeders. Distribution transformer station (CT, from centro de transformación ): the last step, where 13.2 kV becomes 380/220 V for the neighbourhood, the building or the factory. Switching or sectionalizing: does not transform; it only connects, disconnects and reconfigures the grid to isolate a fault or to allow work. This page deals mostly with the CT, the one most often designed and built in a technical school, and its ideas hold for a large ET, which only adds more equipment. By construction, CTs come in four types: Type What it looks like Typical rating Where it is used Pros and cons Overhead (pole or platform) The transformer hangs from a pole or rests on a platform between two. Switching is done with fuse cut-outs and lightning is handled by surge arresters. A cable runs down to a low-voltage board. up to about 160 kVA on one pole; up to about 315 kVA on two Rural and suburban areas, new housing plots. Cheap and q"},{"t":"The single-line diagram and its equipment","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#unifilar","x":"02 The single-line diagram and its equipment A single-line diagram draws with one line what is really three phases. It does not show every cable but the order in which energy passes through the devices and what protects what. It is read from left to right, from the grid to the user. The figure shows a 400 kVA, 13.2/0.4 kV CT and four situations it goes through in its life. Equipment What it does What it can interrupt Disconnector Separates a section and leaves a visible isolating gap: you can see it is open. Nothing under load. It is operated with no current. Load-break switch Switches the CT in normal service. Often has fuses in series. Rated current, but not a short circuit. Medium-voltage fuse Protects the transformer and the grid from a short circuit. It blows and is replaced. Short circuits, once. Circuit breaker Makes and breaks under load and clears short circuits, controlled by relays. It is reset and reused. Load and short-circuit current. Current transformer (measuring CT) and voltage transformer (VT) Reduce current and voltage to standard values (for example 5 A or 1 A in the CT) that instruments and relays can measure. Does not interrupt: measures. Protective relay Receives the current-transformer signal, compares it with its setting and orders the breaker to open. ANSI/IEEE device numbers identify it: 50 (instantaneous overcurrent), 51 (time overcurrent), 87 (differential). Does not interrupt: gives the order. Surge arrester Diverts lightning and switching overvo"},{"t":"Distribution lines: overhead and underground","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#lineas","x":"03 Distribution lines: overhead and underground From the CT leave the low-voltage networks to the users, and to it arrive the medium-voltage ones. Both can run through the air, held up by poles, or underground, in insulated cables. The two solutions coexist in any city. Overhead lines Element Function and variants Conductors Carry the current. In rural medium voltage, bare aluminium conductor steel reinforced (ACSR): the aluminium conducts and the steel takes the tension. Where there are trees, covered or protected conductors are used. In low voltage, aerial bundled cable: three phases insulated with cross-linked polyethylene (XLPE), twisted around a bearing neutral. Insulators Separate the conductor from the support and withstand the voltage. Pin insulators where the line runs straight; suspension insulators (strings) where the conductor is dead-ended or turns. Made of porcelain, toughened glass or polymer (silicone). Sized by voltage and by creepage distance, which must grow near the coast, industry or dust. Structures Reinforced-concrete poles (the most used), treated-wood or steel poles; lattice towers at higher voltages. By function: suspension (tangent), dead-end, angle, terminal and branch structures. Designed for weight, wind and conductor tension. Crossarms, brackets and fittings Concrete, wood or galvanized steel: hold the insulators and set the phase spacing. Guy wires Steel cables that counter the lateral force on terminal, angle and dead-end poles. Earthing and a"},{"t":"Voltage drop along a line","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#caida","x":"04 Voltage drop along a line A conductor has resistance and, in AC, also reactance. When current flows, each section “keeps” part of the voltage, and what arrives at the end is lower than at the source. That difference is the voltage drop. It is not a detail: an induction motor develops a torque that depends on the square of the voltage, so with 10 % less voltage it delivers barely 81 % of its torque; lamps give less light and electronic equipment resets. For a three-phase line with the load concentrated at the end: R′ and X′: resistance and reactance per kilometre (Ω/km) · L: length (km) · I: line current (A) Reactance only matters in medium voltage and with thick conductors; for small-section low-voltage cables the simplified form is used (in a single-phase circuit the current goes out and back): Approximation for low voltage with small-reactance cable (X′ ≪ R′). Single-phase circuit: the current goes out and back (hence the 2). The admissible value is set by the regulations or by the utility for each type of network: the example uses 5 % as a design criterion, and internal installations have their own limits in AEA 90364. To correct an excessive drop there are four ways: increase the cross-section, shorten the run, raise the line voltage or reduce the current (for example by correcting the power factor, see AC power ). At the transformer, the tap can also be changed, normally in 2.5 % steps. Example · A 13.2 kV, 8 km line A three-phase 13.2 kV, 8 km line feeds a 1.5 MW loa"},{"t":"Planning the job: quality, productivity and critical path","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#proceso","x":"05 Planning the job: quality, productivity and critical path A substation or a line is built with crews, trucks, materials that arrive from outside and permits that depend on third parties. A good work process always goes through the same stages: survey and design, planning, execution with control, testing and commissioning and close-out, with the as-built drawings and the final report. Quality: doing well what was asked Quality is not decoration but a list of checks agreed before starting. An inspection and test plan sets when each thing is checked, with which instrument, what value is accepted and who signs it; some checks are hold points: without approval work does not continue (for example, depth and verticality of the poles before concreting). What does not comply is a nonconformity: it is recorded, corrected, and its cause is sought. Check When What is verified Receiving materials On arrival at site That they match the specification and IRAM standards, the nameplate and the factory test report. Setting out and foundations Before concreting or erecting Location, depth and verticality of the poles; design distances and heights. Cable insulation Before energizing Insulation resistance with a megohmmeter of suitable voltage, against the value in the cable specification. Earthing Before energizing Earth resistance, measured with an earth tester, less than or equal to the design value. Transformer and protection At commissioning Turns ratio, oil level and dielectric strength,"},{"t":"Efficiency, fire prevention and environmental care","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#eficiencia","x":"06 Efficiency, fire prevention and environmental care Efficiency: losses paid for every day A network is not perfect: part of the energy is lost as heat, in the transformer and in the conductors. The transformer losses are of two kinds (see Transformers ): the iron losses, which are always there while it is connected, even with no load, and the copper losses, which grow with the square of the current. That is why efficiency has a maximum, reached when the two are equal: x: fraction of rated power at which efficiency peaks · P₀: no-load (iron) loss · Pₖ: full-load (copper) loss Example · At what load is a 400 kVA transformer most efficient? A 400 kVA transformer has (catalogue data, indicative) P 0 = 0.93 kW of no-load loss and P k = 4.6 kW at rated load. With power factor 0.9: Load Output power Total losses Efficiency 20 % 72 kW 1.11 kW 98.48 % 45 % 162 kW 1.86 kW 98.86 % 100 % 360 kW 5.53 kW 98.49 % The difference in percentage looks small, but the transformer is connected all 8,760 hours of the year: choosing one too large “to be safe” makes it work far from its best point and extra no-load losses are paid; choosing one too small overheats it and shortens its life. The criterion is to size for the real demand and leave a reasonable reserve. The other efficiency levers are those of the previous section’s example — adequate cross-sections, short runs and higher voltage— and keeping the power factor high so that less current flows. In the work process, efficiency also means pl"},{"t":"In the workshop and on site","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#taller","x":"07 In the workshop and on site Lab 1 · Reading the single-line diagram of a distribution transformer station Materials: single-line diagram of a real CT (from the school, a cooperative or the utility) and, if a guided visit is authorized, the matching CT for observation only, from where the person in charge indicates. Steps: 1) identify and name every symbol (disconnector, breaker, CT, relay, arrester, busbars); 2) note the rating, the turns ratio and the protection of each outgoing feeder; 3) calculate the rated current on both sides and the short-circuit current on the low-voltage side; 4) write on a sheet the sequence for leaving the CT dead. What is measured: nothing with instruments. Acceptance criterion: the calculated currents match the diagram or the nameplate within 5 % and the sequence follows the order breaker–disconnector–verification–earthing. Lab 2 · Measuring a voltage drop Materials: 50 m of two-core 2.5 mm² cable, fully unrolled, a 1,000 W resistive load (heater or iron), a socket with residual-current and overcurrent protection, a voltmeter and a clamp meter. Steps: 1) with the load connected, measure the voltage at the origin of the cable and at the far end; 2) measure the current; 3) calculate the resistance of the whole (out and back, 100 m) and the expected drop: Acceptance criterion: the measured drop differs from the calculated one by less than 15 % (hot copper has slightly more resistance than at 20 °C) and the voltage at the far end is above 95 % of "},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Opening a disconnector under load A sustained arc that can spread; risk of severe burns. The circuit breaker is opened first. Choosing the low-voltage breaker by rated current only If its breaking capacity is lower than the short-circuit current, it is destroyed clearing the first fault. Calculating the voltage drop with the current of a single phase or the wrong voltage (phase instead of line) The result is off by a factor of √3 and a conductor that fails looks fine. Forgetting the “2” (out and back) in a single-phase circuit The calculated drop is half the real one. Planning with all the tasks chained one after another The duration comes out too long and it is not seen which tasks can be done in parallel. Speeding up tasks with float to recover the delay Not a single day is gained: what rules is the critical path. Starting the job without having ordered the long-lead material The transformer, as in the example, ends up setting the duration of the whole job. Assuming an old transformer has no PCBs Hazardous waste mishandled, fines and lasting environmental damage. The oil is analysed. Throwing water at a burning transformer or board Electrocution of the person fighting the fire and spread of the fire. The power is cut and the correct extinguisher is used. Handing over the job without as-built drawings The next person to work there does not know what is buried or how it is connected."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Substations and distribution lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/subestaciones-lineas#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the difference between a disconnector and a circuit breaker? Which one is opened first to de-energize? The breaker interrupts under load and short-circuit current; the disconnector cannot break current and serves to leave a visible isolating gap. To de-energize, the breaker is opened first and then the disconnector; to energize, the reverse. After opening, the absence of voltage is verified and the equipment is earthed. What is the rated current on the 0.4 kV side of a 160 kVA transformer? With that value the main low-voltage protection and the cross-section of the outgoing conductors are chosen. Name four elements of an overhead line and the function of each. Conductors (carry the current), insulators (separate the conductor from the support), poles or structures (hold everything up, with mechanical design) and guy wires (counter lateral forces). Also: crossarms and fittings, arresters and earthing. A 380 V three-phase line feeds 40 kW at cos φ = 0.9 over 150 m, with a cable of R′ = 0.87 Ω/km and X′ = 0.08 Ω/km. What is the voltage drop? First the current: Then the drop, with sin φ = 0.436: It is 3.8 %: it meets a 5 % limit, and would not meet a 3 % one. In the example job, what happens if the transformer delivery slips one day? And if the earthing slips one day? With the transformer one day late (D = 8 d), task E starts on day 8 and the whole job moves one day: The earthing, however, has 3 days of float: a one-day delay does not change the duratio"},{"t":"The fixed telephone network","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#fija","x":"01 The fixed telephone network Element Function Subscriber pair Two copper wires from the house to the exchange. It carries the voice and the power: the exchange supplies 48 V, which is why the landline phone kept working during a power outage. Signaling Going off-hook closes the loop and requests dial tone; DTMF sends each digit as the sum of two tones, one for the row and one for the column; the ringer signal is 75 V of alternating current at 25 Hz. Exchange It switches the call. From electromechanical to digital, and today to servers that handle voice over IP. Bandwidth The telephone channel occupies 300 to 3400 Hz. It is enough to understand speech and to recognize who is speaking, but not for music. Digitization Voice is sampled at 8 kHz with 8 bits per sample: 64 kbit/s per channel. It is the basis of the whole digital network, and from it come the 30-channel multiplexes. Sampling at 8 kHz a signal limited to 3400 Hz is a direct application of the sampling theorem —see signals and Fourier analysis — and the 8-bit quantization is the same one studied in A/D and D/A converters."},{"t":"The idea of the cell","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#celda","x":"02 The idea of the cell A single powerful transmitter covering an entire city can only handle as many simultaneous calls as it has channels. By lowering the power and installing many small antennas, the same group of frequencies can be used again a few blocks away without interference. Consequences of the cellular scheme Adjustable capacity: where more traffic is needed, smaller cells are used. In the center of a city there are antennas every few blocks; on the highway, every several kilometers. Low power: a cell phone transmits between 0.1 and 2 W, and generally much less thanks to power control. Mobility: the network follows the user. The handover passes the call to the neighboring cell; roaming allows using another operator's network. Sectorization: each site usually has three 120° panel antennas, and each sector is a different cell: capacity is tripled with the same tower. Network elements Terminal with its SIM, which stores the subscriber's identity and keys. The device has its own identifier, the IMEI. Base station: antennas, radios, power supply and backup. It is the part that can be seen from the street. Controller: manages several base stations, the handovers and the radio resources. Core network: switches calls and data, authenticates, bills and connects with the other networks."},{"t":"How they share the air","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#acceso","x":"03 How they share the air Within a cell, many users have to coexist in the same spectrum. Multiple access techniques solve that sharing, and each generation used a different one. Method How it divides Where it was used FDMA Each user gets a frequency of their own for the whole call. Analog 1G. Simple and very inefficient. TDMA Several users share one frequency, each in its own time slot. Eight per carrier in GSM. 2G / GSM. CDMA Everyone transmits at the same time in the same band, each with a different code that the receiver uses to separate it. 3G / UMTS. OFDMA The spectrum is divided into thousands of subcarriers and blocks of subcarriers and time are assigned to each user, according to what they need. 4G / LTE and 5G. It is also the technique of modern WiFi. Why OFDMA won Because it allocates resources in blocks and in real time: someone downloading a file gets many subcarriers, someone who only has a messaging app open gets a few. Also, by splitting the channel into narrow subcarriers, each one sees an almost flat channel, and that makes it much simpler to correct the distortions of multipath."},{"t":"From 1G to 5G","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#generaciones","x":"04 From 1G to 5G Gen. What it introduced Typical speed 1G Analog voice with frequency modulation. No encryption: anyone with a receiver could listen in. — 2G Digital voice, encryption, SMS, the SIM card. Later GPRS and EDGE added data. 9.6 to 384 kbit/s 3G Data considered from the design stage: mobile internet, video calls. 0.4 to 42 Mbit/s 4G All IP, including voice. OFDMA and multiple antennas (MIMO). 10 to 300 Mbit/s 5G More capacity, very low latency and device density: also designed for industry and sensors. 100 Mbit/s to 1 Gbit/s The radio side, in concrete terms Bands in Argentina: 700 MHz, 850 MHz, 900 MHz, 1900 MHz, 2100 MHz, 2600 MHz and 3.5 GHz for 5G. Low bands penetrate buildings better and cover farther; high bands carry more data but reach less far. Power control: the network orders the mobile to transmit the minimum necessary. Near the antenna it transmits microwatts; at the cell edge, close to the maximum. This saves battery, reduces interference and increases capacity. Duplexing: FDD uses two frequencies, one for each direction; TDD uses the same one alternating in time. MIMO: several antennas at each end, transmitting different streams on the same frequency. It is the main reason for the jump in speed in 4G and 5G. Less signal, more battery used It sounds backwards but it is exact: in an area of poor coverage, power control demands that the mobile transmit at maximum and retry more times. That is why the phone heats up and the battery drains quickly right w"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Lab 1 · Mapping coverage With an app that shows the signal level in dBm and the cell the device is connected to, walk a route noting level and position. Plot it. You can see the level jumps when changing cells, and you can identify where the antenna serving each stretch is. Lab 2 · Handover live During a call, cover a long distance noting the cell identifier. Record at which points the handover occurs and what signal level there was at that moment. It is the direct way to see the mechanism that makes it possible to talk while moving. Lab 3 · Penetration and shielding Measure the signal level outside, inside the classroom, in a basement and inside an elevator. Compare a low band with a high one if the device allows forcing them. The drop inside metal structures shows the Faraday cage effect with your own numbers. Lab 4 · DTMF Record the keypad tones of a telephone and analyze them with a spectrum program. Identify the two tones of each digit and build the complete table of rows and columns. It is the most direct application of the spectral analysis seen in Signal generation and processing."},{"t":"Common mistakes and misconceptions","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes and misconceptions Misconception What actually happens “The signal bars indicate the data rate” They indicate signal level. With an excellent signal and a congested cell, the speed can be awful. “The more antennas nearby, the more radiation I receive” The opposite: with the antenna nearby the mobile transmits much less, and it is the mobile that is held against the head. “5G and WiFi's 5 GHz are the same thing” They are unrelated: one is a generation of cellular telephony, the other a frequency band. “The IMEI and the number are in the SIM” The number and the keys are in the SIM; the IMEI identifies the device. “With more power in the phone there would be more coverage” The link is also limited in the other direction and by interference between cells: raising the power would degrade the network. “The cell is a hexagon” The hexagon is a drawing model. Real coverage depends on the terrain, the buildings and the antenna height."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Telephony and cellular telephony","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the central idea of the cellular system? Reusing frequencies: dividing the territory into low-power cells and using the same group of channels again in non-neighboring cells. That way a few channels serve millions of users. What is the handover? The transfer of a call or data session from one cell to the neighboring one when the user moves, without the service being interrupted. Difference between handover and roaming. Handover is a change of cell within the same network. Roaming is using the network of another operator, typically in another country. Why is the telephone channel limited to 3400 Hz? Because it is enough for speech to be intelligible and recognizable, and limiting the bandwidth allows many more channels to be multiplexed over the same medium. Where do the 64 kbit/s of a digital voice channel come from? From sampling at 8000 samples per second with 8 bits each:. How do TDMA and CDMA differ? TDMA divides time: each user has their slot on the same frequency. CDMA lets everyone transmit at once in the same band, separating them by codes. Why is OFDMA more efficient? Because it dynamically allocates blocks of subcarriers and time according to what each user needs, and because narrow subcarriers simplify the handling of multipath. What advantage do low bands have over high ones? They penetrate buildings better and cover more area with the same power. In exchange, they offer less data capacity. What is power control for? So that each mobile "},{"t":"Scanning and interlacing","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#exploracion","x":"01 Scanning and interlacing Parameter 625-line system 525-line system Lines per frame 625 525 Frames per second 25 30 Fields per second 50 60 Line frequency 15,625 Hz 15,734 Hz Line duration 64 µs 63.5 µs Used in PAL, Europe and part of South America; PAL-N in Argentina, Uruguay and Paraguay NTSC, North America and Japan Why interlacing was invented At 25 frames per second motion looks smooth, but the screen flickers in a very annoying way. Doubling the frames to 50 would cure the flicker and double the bandwidth required, which at the time was not feasible. Interlacing achieves both: each frame is divided into two fields, one with the odd lines and one with the even lines. The screen is refreshed 50 times per second —the flicker disappears— but only 25 complete images travel through the channel. It is one of the most elegant engineering tricks in the history of communications. Example · Where the 15,625 Hz comes from. That is exactly the high-pitched whine given off by the flyback (line output) transformer of CRT TV sets, at the edge of audibility: many people heard it and never knew where it came from. And of the 625 lines, about 576 carry picture: the rest are used up in the vertical retrace, time that was later put to use for teletext and other data."},{"t":"The composite video signal","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#compuesto","x":"02 The composite video signal Component What it carries Luminance (Y) The brightness. It is the signal that black-and-white TV sets used to see, which is why color had to be compatible. Chrominance (C) The color, modulated onto a 4.43 MHz subcarrier in PAL (3.58 MHz in PAL-N, the one used in Argentina). Amplitude gives the saturation and phase gives the hue. Horizontal sync A pulse at the start of each line that tells the receiver where it begins. Vertical sync A special sequence that marks the start of each field. Color burst A few cycles of the subcarrier on the back porch: it is the phase reference without which the colors come out shifted. Black and white level They define the scale: the sync pulses sit below black, in the “blacker than black” region. Compatibility, the constraint that defined everything When color arrived, there were already millions of black-and-white TV sets. The solution was to fit the chrominance inside the same channel, on a subcarrier chosen to interleave with the luminance spectrum and interfere as little as possible. A black-and-white receiver sees that subcarrier as a fine pattern and ignores it; a color receiver demodulates it. The whole PAL system is a consequence of that constraint. PAL and NTSC In NTSC, a phase error in transmission directly changes the hue: faces look green or purple, which is why TV sets came with a tint control. PAL inverts the phase of one of the components on alternate lines, so that the error on one line is canceled by"},{"t":"Digital television","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#digital","x":"03 Digital television Stage What it does 1 Digitization: the image is converted into pixels and the signal into numbers. Uncompressed, a standard-definition picture requires on the order of 270 Mbit/s. 2 Compression: MPEG-2 or H.264 reduce that rate about a hundredfold by taking advantage of the fact that almost nothing changes between consecutive frames. 3 Multiplexing: several video, audio and data programs are combined into a single transport stream. 4 Channel coding: error correction is added to withstand multipath and noise. 5 COFDM modulation: thousands of narrow carriers, each with its own QAM, spread across the same 6 MHz channel. Why COFDM solves ghosting In analog television, a signal that arrived bouncing off a building produced a displaced “ghost” image. In COFDM, the thousands of carriers are so narrow that each symbol lasts much longer than the delay of the reflection, and in addition a guard interval is added between symbols. The result is that the echo, instead of causing trouble, adds energy. This makes possible something impossible in analog: single-frequency networks, in which all the repeaters in a region transmit on the same channel. The digital cliff Analog television degraded gradually: first a little snow, then more, until it became unwatchable. Digital works perfectly up to a point and then cuts out abruptly, with a frozen or blocky picture. This completely changes the installation work: it is not enough to “see that it looks good.” You have to measur"},{"t":"Closed-circuit television","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#cctv","x":"04 Closed-circuit television What defines an installation Resolution and field of view: the figure that matters is how many pixels fall on the object. To identify a person you need on the order of 250 pixels per meter; to merely detect presence, much fewer. Lighting: almost all image problems come from light, not from the camera. Backlighting, reflections and darkness are solved by placing the camera well, not by raising the resolution. Storage: bit rate per camera × number of cameras × retention days. It is what sets the size of the disk and is often underestimated. Power: over the same network cable (PoE) it simplifies the installation enormously, but you have to check the total power of the equipment that supplies it. Legal framework and privacy Video surveillance is regulated. You must post signs that the area is being filmed, limit the view to your own property —not the sidewalk or the neighbor’s window—, set a retention period and restrict who has access to the recordings. It is part of the technical job: an installation that points at other people’s spaces is a legal problem for the installer and the owner, as well as a lack of consideration."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Lab 1 · Viewing composite video With a bar generator and an oscilloscope, observe a complete line of video: identify the sync pulse, the porch, the color burst and the levels of each bar. Measure the duration of each part and compare it with the standard. Lab 2 · Measuring a digital installation With a field strength meter, survey the level, signal-to-noise ratio, MER and error rate of the digital channels at different points in the school. Determine how much margin there is to the cliff at each outlet, and document it. Lab 3 · Distribution Set up a distribution network with taps and splitters, calculate the losses and check that the level at the worst outlet stays within the receiver’s range. Check what happens when an output is left unterminated. Lab 4 · Camera and field of view Mount a camera and calculate the number of pixels per meter at different distances. Verify experimentally from what distance a person can be identified and beyond what distance they can only be detected."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause Looks perfect and sometimes breaks up into blocks Insufficient margin: digital gives no warning before cutting out. You have to measure MER and error rate. Wrong colors in analog Problem with the color burst or a phase error, characteristic of NTSC. Ghost image in analog Reflected signal. In digital, COFDM turns it into useful energy. High level and poor quality Interference from another channel or saturation of the installation’s amplifier. Only the high channels fail Cable loss, which grows with frequency, or poorly chosen taps. Unterminated distribution outputs Mismatch and reflections that degrade all the outlets. Camera facing the window Backlighting: you see a black silhouette. It is a placement problem, not a camera problem. Disk full within a week The bit rate per camera and the retention days were underestimated."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Analog and digital television","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is interlacing for? To refresh the screen 50 times per second —and eliminate flicker— while sending only 25 complete images, without doubling the bandwidth. Where does the 15,625 Hz line frequency come from? From. It is the high-pitched whine given off by CRT TV sets. What are luminance and chrominance? Luminance carries the brightness —it was the complete signal in black and white— and chrominance carries the color, modulated onto a subcarrier within the same channel. What is the color burst for? It is the phase reference of the subcarrier. Without it the receiver cannot demodulate the color correctly and the hues come out shifted. What advantage does PAL have over NTSC? It inverts the phase on alternate lines, so that a phase error is canceled between lines: instead of changing the hue, it produces a slight loss of saturation. What does COFDM do with reflected signals? Thanks to narrow carriers and the guard interval, the echo arrives within the same symbol and adds energy instead of producing ghosts. What are single-frequency networks? Networks in which all the repeaters transmit on the same channel. This is possible thanks to COFDM, because the signals from several transmitters add up instead of interfering. What is the digital cliff and what does it mean for the installation? Reception is perfect up to a point and then cuts out abruptly. It means that it is not enough to look at the picture: you have to measure the margin with MER and error rate. "},{"t":"The statement","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#teorema","x":"01 The statement The sampling frequency must be at least twice the highest component present in the signal. If this is not met, every component above f s /2 appears mirrored at a lower frequency. Application Useful band Sampling Why that value Telephony 300 to 3400 Hz 8 kHz A little more than twice 3400 Hz, leaving margin for the filter. Digital audio 20 Hz to 20 kHz 44.1 kHz Covers human hearing with margin for the input filter. Instrumentation depends on the process 5 to 20 times Reconstructing is not enough: the waveform has to be seen clearly. Digital oscilloscope bandwidth 5 to 10 times So the edges can be seen without relying on interpolation. Twice is the theoretical minimum, not the working value Sampling at exactly twice the frequency only works with mathematically perfect signals and ideal filters. In practice a margin is taken: telephony samples at 8 kHz for a 3.4 kHz band, and audio at 44.1 kHz for 20 kHz. That margin is what gives the anti-aliasing filter a range of frequencies over which to attenuate."},{"t":"Aliasing","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#aliasing","x":"02 Aliasing Worked example · A tone that appears where it isn't A signal containing a 5 kHz tone is sampled at 8 kHz, with no input filter. The Nyquist limit is. The 5 kHz tone exceeds it. Alias frequency:. In the reconstructed signal a 3 kHz tone appears that never existed, inside the usable band and therefore impossible to filter out. It is the same phenomenon by which, in a film, the wheels of a car seem to turn backward: the camera “samples” at 24 or 30 frames per second and the wheel turns faster than that. The anti-aliasing filter always goes before the converter And it is analog, because once the signal is sampled the damage is irreversible: no digital processing can separate the alias from the legitimate signal. Its cutoff frequency is placed below f s /2, and its slope has to be steep enough that at f s /2 the attenuation is greater than the dynamic range of the converter. That requirement is demanding: an eighth-order filter is not unusual. That is why oversampling is used, which puts the useful band far from f s /2 and allows a simple analog filter, leaving the fine filtering to the digital domain, where it is easy."},{"t":"Sample-and-hold and reconstruction","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#reconstruccion","x":"03 Sample-and-hold and reconstruction On the conversion side The sample-and-hold circuit freezes the value while the converter works. Without it, a signal that changes during conversion produces a meaningless result. The aperture time —the time it takes to freeze— and the jitter —the variation of the sampling instant— limit accuracy at high frequency. Droop is the slow discharge of the hold capacitor: it must be negligible during the conversion. On the reconstruction side The D/A converter outputs steps, not a curve: it holds each value until the next sample. Those steps contain high-frequency components that were not in the original. The reconstruction filter removes them and returns the smooth curve. The hold also introduces a slight attenuation of high frequencies, which in demanding systems is compensated for. Jitter: the error you don't see coming If the samples are not taken at exactly equally spaced instants, the captured value corresponds to the wrong moment, and that amounts to an amplitude error. The faster the signal varies, the larger that error. In audio and digital radio, sampling clock jitter can limit performance long before the number of bits of the converter does."},{"t":"Pulse modulation and multiplexing","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#pulsos","x":"04 Pulse modulation and multiplexing Acronym What it modulates in the pulse Use PAM The amplitude of each pulse follows the value of the sample. It is the intermediate step of every sampling: what exists before quantization. PWM The width of the pulse. The information is in the duration. Power control, servos, switching power supplies, brightness control. PPM The position of the pulse within its interval. Optical links and radio control. Good immunity to amplitude noise. PCM Each sample is quantized and encoded in bits. All of telephony and digital audio. Developed in the next topic. Time-division multiplexing Between one sample and the next there is free time, and that time is used to interleave the samples of other channels. It is the idea that allowed a single copper pair to carry thirty conversations. In the European system, the E1 frame has 32 time slots of 8 bits each, repeated 8000 times per second:. Of those 32 slots, 30 carry voice and two are reserved for synchronization and signaling. Example · How many channels fit A digital link of 8.448 Mbit/s carrying 64 kbit/s telephone channels, with 5% of the capacity reserved for synchronization and control. Usable capacity:. Channels: as a theoretical maximum. The real system that works at that rate —the E2 — carries 120 channels: four E1 frames of 30 channels each. The difference is the synchronization and justification bits that multiplexing needs in order to combine four frames whose clocks are not exactly equal."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Lab 1 · Making an alias With a generator and a sound card or an A/D converter, sample tones of increasing frequency without an input filter. Record the frequency from which the measured tone starts to go down instead of up, and verify the alias formula at several points. Lab 2 · The anti-aliasing filter Repeat the previous lab with an active low-pass filter inserted, with a cutoff below half the sampling frequency. Check that the aliases disappear and measure how much attenuation is needed at f s /2 for them to go unnoticed. Lab 3 · Reconstruction Convert a sampled signal back to analog and observe the steps on the oscilloscope. Add the reconstruction filter and compare. Measure the high-frequency content before and after with a spectrum analyzer. Lab 4 · Time-division multiplexing With a microcontroller, sample four slow signals and transmit them interleaved over a single serial channel, with a synchronization word at the start of each frame. On the other side, separate them and reconstruct them. It is a complete TDM frame made by hand."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Sampling without an anti-aliasing filter Components that never existed appear, inside the useful band and impossible to remove afterwards. Sampling at exactly twice the frequency There is no margin for the filter: in practice considerably more is needed. Filtering after the converter Too late: the aliasing has already happened and is irreversible. Believing that 2 times is enough to see the waveform Nyquist guarantees reconstruction, not a legible display. To observe, 5 to 10 times is needed. Ignoring clock jitter It limits accuracy at high frequency long before the converter resolution does. No sample-and-hold The signal changes during conversion and the result does not correspond to any instant. Forgetting the reconstruction filter The D/A steps radiate and interfere, and the signal is not the original."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does the sampling theorem say? That a band-limited signal is completely determined by its samples if the sampling frequency is at least twice its highest component. A 6 kHz tone sampled at 8 kHz: where does it appear? At, inside the useful band and with no way of telling it from a legitimate signal of that frequency. Why does telephony sample at 8 kHz? Because its band reaches 3400 Hz: twice that would be 6800 Hz, and 8 kHz leaves a reasonable margin for the anti-aliasing filter to work. Where does the anti-aliasing filter go and why? Before the converter, and it is analog. Once the signal has been sampled, the aliasing has already happened and no later processing can undo it. What is the advantage of oversampling? It puts the useful band far from f s /2, so a simple analog filter is enough and the fine filtering is done in the digital domain, where it is easy. What is the sample-and-hold circuit for? To freeze the value of the signal while the converter works. Without it, a signal that changes during conversion gives a result that does not correspond to any instant. What is jitter and what effect does it produce? The variation of the sampling instant. It amounts to an amplitude error, larger the faster the signal varies, and it can limit performance before the number of bits does. Why is a filter needed after the D/A converter? Because the D/A outputs steps, which contain high-frequency components that did not exist in the original. The reconstruction"},{"t":"What it is and when it is justified","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#que-son","x":"01 What it is and when it is justified A live-working job (in Argentina, trabajo con tensión, TCT) is one done on an installation that is still energized, with the worker either in contact with live parts or inside the distance across which an arc could reach them. It is the exception to the five golden rules: the general rule is to work dead by cutting, locking out, verifying and earthing; live working is accepted only when cutting is not possible or would create a greater risk. Situation Why the supply is not cut Example Continuity of supply The outage would leave many customers or an essential service without power, and the regulator penalizes interruptions (see power quality ). A tap on a medium-voltage line that feeds a neighborhood and a hospital. A process that cannot stop Stopping the process costs more, or is dangerous or irreversible. A casting furnace, a cold room, a fire-pump system. The task requires voltage What is to be measured or adjusted exists only while the installation is energized. Clamp-meter current readings, thermography, hunting an intermittent fault, setting a protection in service. Cutting creates another risk The switching operation itself is dangerous, or de-energizing a safety system is worse. A board that feeds ventilation or gas extraction. Convenience is not a reason “It is faster” or “I do not want to bother production” does not justify live working. The decision is made in writing, with the reason, before starting, and by whoever is respons"},{"t":"The Argentine legal framework","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#marco","x":"02 The Argentine legal framework There is no single live-working standard: there is a chain, from the general law to each company's procedure. For the hierarchy between law, decree, regulation and technical standard, see Regulations for electrical installations; here we look at what each link contributes to a live job. Standard What it contributes to live working Law 19,587 on Occupational Health and Safety The framework law: the employer must provide safe conditions, training, protective equipment and written procedures; the worker must follow them and report unsafe conditions. Decree 351/79 Its regulation. It sets the safety conditions for electrical installations and work, and is the basis from which the specific live-working rules start. SRT Resolution 592/2004 “Regulation for carrying out live work on electrical installations” ( Reglamento para la ejecución de trabajos con tensión en instalaciones eléctricas ), from the Superintendency of Occupational Risks. It is the specific rule: it requires trained and authorized personnel, written procedures, suitable equipment and working conditions. Its current text is consulted on the SRT website. Law 24,557 on Occupational Risks The ART system: prevention, accident coverage and the employer's duty to report and control risks. AEA (Argentine Electrotechnical Association) Technical regulations and guides for installations and networks. AEA 90364 governs installations in buildings; for networks and transformer stations, the 95000 s"},{"t":"The three methods and the safety zones","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#metodos","x":"03 The three methods and the safety zones The methods differ in where the insulation is that protects the worker. In all three, the worker is never in contact with two parts at different potential at the same time. Method How the worker is protected Typical equipment Typical use At a distance (insulated stick) Stays at a distance and works with long insulating-handle tools. He is at earth potential. Insulating sticks with interchangeable heads, detectors and test sets. Medium and high voltage. At low voltage, switching and cutting that cannot be done by hand. In contact (gloves) Touches the live part, but wearing insulating gloves and sleeves, and insulated from earth and the other phases with blankets and covers. He is at earth potential, with insulation interposed. Gloves with leather protectors, sleeves, blankets, rigid covers, insulated tools, insulated platform or basket. Low voltage, where it is done most. At medium voltage, according to the procedure and regulations. At potential (bare-hand) Is brought to the same potential as the conductor: he is lifted insulated from earth and bonded with a conductive suit. Between him and the conductor there is no voltage difference. Long insulating boom or basket, conductive suit, equipotential bonding. High and extra-high voltage, on transmission lines. “In contact” does not mean “unprotected” In the glove method the gloves protect the hand, but the rest of the body stays close to other live parts. That is why everything within re"},{"t":"Personnel, procedure and work permit","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#personal","x":"04 Personnel, procedure and work permit Authorization of personnel. A diploma or experience is not enough: to do live work a person needs specific training, theoretical and practical, in the method to be used; to have been assessed; to have proven medical fitness for the post; and to hold a written authorization from the employer, limited to certain methods and voltage levels. The authorization is renewed with retraining and is withdrawn if the person stops following the procedure. The number of hours, the contents and the periodicity are set by the regulations in force. Role What they do Work leader The only person responsible for execution. Checks the permit, the equipment and the weather, gives the briefing, orders each step and can stop the work. One leader per task. Operators Carry out the work according to the procedure. Nobody works alone: at least one other trained person who can act in an accident. Safety observer Watches the distances and the surroundings from the ground, without any task that could distract him. Network or plant operator The person in charge of the installation. Authorizes the work, disables automatic reclosing and restores nothing without the work leader's closing notice. The written procedure. Each type of task has its own procedure, written beforehand and reviewed by someone technically competent. At a minimum it contains: The scope: installation, voltage level, method and task it covers. The personnel required and the authorization each one mus"},{"t":"Insulating tools and equipment, and their tests","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#equipos","x":"05 Insulating tools and equipment, and their tests What protects the worker in live work is the equipment, and equipment protects only if it is intact, clean, dry and tested on time. Each item carries an individual identification, and its purchase date, tests and incidents are recorded. Equipment What it is for What is checked Rubber insulating gloves, with leather protectors Insulation for the hand in the glove method. The protector shields the glove from cuts and punctures. Visual inspection and air test before each use; periodic dielectric test; store without folding, away from heat, light and oils. Insulating sleeves, blankets and rigid covers Cover the conductors and live parts that are within reach and are not touched. No cuts, deformations or dirt; periodic test. Sticks and insulating-handle tools Work at a distance. They replace the worker's arm. Clean and dry; dielectric test of the insulating section; not used with cracks or damaged coating. Insulating platforms, baskets and booms Lift the worker and insulate him from earth. Dielectric test of the insulating boom and the aerial lift; cleaning and condition of the coating. Voltage detectors Confirm the presence or absence of voltage. Tested before and after each use on a known source. Type and category suited to the voltage. Helmet, face shield and arc-rated clothing Protect against the arc even when the method is correctly applied. Helmet for electrical use, face shield and garments with the arc rating given by the "},{"t":"The general overhaul of a machine","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#recorrida","x":"06 The general overhaul of a machine The general overhaul ( recorrida general ) is the intervention in which a machine is taken out of service, completely dismantled, checked part by part, repaired as needed, and then reassembled and tested. It is done with the machine dead and locked out, with the five golden rules applied on the supply side: here the work is the opposite of live working. It is scheduled when service hours, the trend of predictive maintenance or a serious failure indicate it (see Maintenance of electrical components and Maintenance management ). Stage What is done What is recorded 1 · Inspection The nameplate is read, the history is reviewed and the symptoms are measured with the machine still assembled: current per phase, temperature, vibration, noise and grease leaks. Nameplate data, reason for the intervention, entry values. 2 · Dismantling Disconnect and mark terminals and cables. Remove end shields, rotor and bearings with a puller, marking position and orientation. Shaft and air-gap clearances are measured before the rotor is removed. Photos, marks, measured clearances, order of the parts. 3 · Diagnosis Cleaning and inspection of each part: winding, core, rotor, shaft, bearing seats, frame. Insulation and winding resistance are measured (see Motor and transformer tests ). With that it is decided what to repair and whether repair makes sense. Measured values, damage found, list of parts and jobs. 4 · Repair Replace bearings and seals, correct seats and "},{"t":"In the workshop","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#taller","x":"07 In the workshop Practice 1 · Inspect and test an insulating glove Materials: a pair of insulating gloves with leather protectors (the school's own, with their label), paper towels, water and neutral soap, record sheet. Steps: 1) read the class and test date on the label and record both; 2) inspect the glove by eye, outside and inside; 3) do the air test on each glove; 4) wash them with water and neutral soap and dry them; 5) store them without folding. Acceptance criterion: valid test date, no cuts, cracks, blisters or deformations, and the inflated glove does not lose air. Any fault puts it out of service, even the first time. Practice 2 · Write a procedure and a permit Task: in groups of three, write the procedure and work permit for a simulated task on the training board: “measure the current of one phase with a clamp meter on an energized board”. Minimum content: scope, people and roles (work leader and observer), equipment with dates, steps with their hazards, conditions to suspend, and actions in an accident. Another group reviews the procedure and tries to find gaps. Acceptance criterion: no step without its hazard, no role without a name, and the reviewing group cannot find a situation the procedure does not resolve. Practice 3 · General overhaul of a 1.5 kW motor Materials: an out-of-service three-phase motor, bearing puller, wrenches, megohmmeter, ohmmeter or bridge, caliper, grease, new bearings, record sheet. Everything dead, with the motor disconnected. Steps:"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Doing live work “because it is faster” People are exposed to a risk that could have been avoided with a scheduled outage. Doing live work without authorization or a written permit If there is an accident, the employer's liability and the worker's own are worsened. Estimating the safety distance by eye The arc strikes before the body touches anything. The regulations set the values. Using gloves without inspecting them or with an expired test A punctured glove lets current through. The test label is not decoration. Continuing in rain or a thunderstorm “because there is little left” Moisture lowers the insulation of equipment and supports; lightning gives no warning. Working alone or without an observer No one can cut the supply or rescue the person who stayed exposed. Not disabling automatic reclosing The protection can close again onto a fault while people are working. Reassembling a motor without first measuring insulation and clearances There is nothing to compare with at the end, and a machine that fails again is handed over. Repairing a motor without comparing with replacement Money is spent on a machine that consumes more and will fail again; see Maintenance management. Ignoring a growing vibration because “the motor still runs” A faulty bearing ends up seizing the shaft or burning the winding."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Live working","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/trabajos-con-tension#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment When is live working justified and when is it not? It is justified when cutting is not possible or creates a greater risk: continuity of an essential service, a process that cannot be stopped, a task that can only be done with voltage (a measurement), or a cut that is dangerous in itself. It is not justified by speed or convenience. The rule is to work dead. Which Argentine standards regulate live working? Law 19,587 and its Decree 351/79 (health and safety), SRT Resolution 592/2004 (the specific regulation for live work), Law 24,557 on Occupational Risks, the AEA regulations and the utility's procedures, under the oversight of ENRE or ERSeP. What are the three live-working methods and where is the insulation in each? At a distance: the insulation is the stick and the distance. In contact: the gloves and sleeves, plus the blankets and the support insulated from earth. At potential: the insulating boom that separates the worker from earth, while the conductive suit keeps him at the same potential as the conductor. Why can safety distances not be given “from memory”, and who sets them? Because they depend on voltage, altitude, humidity and the overvoltages the network can produce, and the regulations set them for each case. They are read in the standard or the procedure; estimating by eye is dangerous because the arc strikes without anyone touching anything. Can a class 1 glove be used on a 13.2 kV line phase to phase? No. The voltage to earth is kV, higher t"},{"t":"The principle","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#principio","x":"01 The principle Everything follows from what we saw in electrodynamics and induction: a varying current produces a varying magnetic flux, and that flux, passing through another winding, induces a voltage in it. The iron core is there so that almost all of the primary's flux passes through the secondary. The turns ratio a. Watch the order: the currents are inverted with respect to the voltages, and that is the key to everything that follows. It only works with AC What induces a voltage is the change in flux. With DC the flux is constant and nothing is induced: the only current is whatever the wire resistance allows, and the winding burns out. Connecting a mains transformer to a battery destroys it, and it is a mistake you only make once. Example · A 220 V to 12 V transformer Ratio. If the primary has 1100 turns, the secondary has turns. With a load drawing 5 A from the secondary, the primary current is. The power: on the load side, and on the mains side. The voltage goes down and the current goes up in the same proportion."},{"t":"The real transformer: where energy is lost","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#real","x":"02 The real transformer: where energy is lost An ideal transformer would transfer 100 % of the power. A real one loses power in two different places, and telling them apart is what lets you diagnose a fault or calculate an efficiency. Core (iron) losses · P Fe Hysteresis: the energy it takes to reorient the magnetic domains on every cycle. It is reduced with grain-oriented silicon-steel laminations. Eddy currents: currents induced inside the core itself. They are reduced by laminating the core and insulating the laminations from one another: that is why cores are a stack of thin sheets and not a solid block. They depend on the voltage and frequency, not on the load: they exist even with the secondary open. They are what makes a transformer draw power—and hum—with nothing connected. Copper losses · P Cu Joule heating in the winding resistance:. They depend on the square of the load: at half load they are a quarter of what they are at full load. They set the thermal limit of the transformer, and they are what you feel when you touch it after it has been working for a while. The regulation compares the no-load voltage with the full-load voltage: how much the transformer “sags” when loaded. In a small one it can be 10 to 20 %; in a distribution transformer, 2 to 4 %. The two classic tests Open-circuit test: the primary is supplied at rated voltage with the secondary open. Almost no useful current flows, so the wattmeter reads practically just the iron losses. It also gives the ma"},{"t":"Designing a small transformer","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#calculo","x":"03 Designing a small transformer It is an approximate calculation, but good enough to wind a laboratory transformer, and the procedure is still used in the workshop. S is the core cross-section in cm² and P the power in VA; the relation holds for 50 Hz with a flux density of about 1 T. J is the allowable current density, 3 A/mm² in small transformers. Worked example · 220 V to 12 V, 60 VA transformer Core cross-section:. Choose a set of E-I laminations whose center leg times the stack thickness gives approximately that value. Turns per volt:. Primary: turns. Secondary: turns, plus 5 % to compensate for the drop under load → 61 turns. Currents:;. Secondary wire: → (AWG 15). Primary wire: → (AWG 28). Then you have to check that the winding fits in the window of the lamination: if it does not, increase the core cross-section (fewer turns) or accept a higher current density at the cost of a higher temperature. Why a transformer hums Because of magnetostriction: the laminations change dimension slightly with the flux, a hundred times per second. If they are loose, they vibrate and the hum is amplified. A hum that suddenly gets louder usually points to loose laminations, overvoltage—the core goes into saturation—or a shorted turn, which will also heat it up quickly."},{"t":"Impedance transformation","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#impedancia","x":"04 Impedance transformation This is the transformer's third function, the least obvious and the most used in electronics: seen from the primary, whatever is on the secondary appears multiplied by. It is used to match a source to its load and transfer the maximum power, which is the central problem of output stages and transmission lines. Example · Matching a loudspeaker to an output stage A vacuum-tube stage needs to see 5000 Ω at its plate and the loudspeaker is 8 Ω: With 2500 turns on the primary, the secondary has 100. The same idea explains the 70 V line transformer of public-address systems, the balun of an antenna and the reason why in RF people always talk about 50 Ω: if the load is not what the line expects, part of the energy is reflected back, as seen in impedance measurements."},{"t":"Types you need to know","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#tipos","x":"05 Types you need to know Type What is special about it Where it appears Power Two separate windings, laminated E-I or core-type (legged) core Power supplies, distribution, substations 1:1 isolation Does not change the voltage: provides galvanic isolation Essential for measuring mains-connected circuits with an oscilloscope Autotransformer A single tapped winding: smaller and cheaper 110/220 V step-up converters, motor starting. Does not isolate: primary and secondary share a conductor Toroidal Ring-shaped core: less leakage, less hum, less stray field High-quality audio, instrumentation Current transformer (CT) The primary is the conductor itself; it delivers a proportional current (5 A typical) Metering and protection in switchboards; the same principle as the clamp meter Voltage transformer (VT) Steps the voltage down to a measurable value (110 V typical) Metering at medium and high voltage High-frequency Ferrite core, not laminated steel Switching power supplies: at 100 kHz the core can be hundreds of times smaller Never open the secondary of a current transformer With the primary in service, if the secondary of a CT is opened, the current that was compensating the flux disappears: the core saturates and voltages of thousands of volts appear across the open terminals. It is one of the most dangerous operations in a switchboard. Before disconnecting an instrument fed by a CT you must short-circuit the secondary using the terminal block provided for that purpose. Why switch"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Turns ratio With a multi-tap laboratory transformer, measure the input voltage and the voltage at each output with no load, and calculate the ratios. Compare them with the turns ratio if the manufacturer states it. Then wind 10 turns of wire around the core and measure the voltage that appears: dividing gives you the volts per turn value experimentally. Lab 2 · The two tests Open-circuit test: supply at rated voltage with the secondary open and measure V, I and P. Short-circuit test: with the secondary short-circuited, raise the primary voltage gradually with a variac until rated current is reached, and measure. With those data, calculate the efficiency at full load and at half load, and plot the curve. Lab 3 · Regulation Measure the secondary voltage with no load and with rated load (lamps or resistors). Calculate the regulation. Repeat with a higher-power transformer and compare: the larger one regulates better. That is why an oversized transformer gives more stable voltages. Lab 4 · Impedance matching With an audio output transformer, measure the resistance seen by the generator with the secondary loaded with 8 Ω and with the secondary open. Compare the measured value with. Then drive a loudspeaker with and without the transformer from a high-impedance stage and compare the volume: the difference is the matching. Working with an isolation transformer Any oscilloscope measurement on a mains-connected circuit must be made with an isolation transformer. "},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause Gets very hot with no load Shorted turn, poorly insulated laminations or a supply voltage above the rated value (saturation). The output voltage drops a lot when the load is connected Poor regulation: undersized transformer or wire that is too thin. Hums more than usual Loose laminations, overvoltage or an incipient winding fault. The primary fuse blows when you switch it on Inrush current: when energized at the worst instant, the flux adds to the remanent flux and saturates the core. Solved with a slow-blow fuse or an inrush-limiting NTC thermistor. Gives the correct voltage but delivers no current You are measuring with no load. A transformer with shorted turns or insufficient power reads fine with no load and collapses under load. The 12 V secondary measures 13.5 V This is normal: small transformers are wound with 5 to 10 % extra turns to compensate for the drop under load. Burned out when connected to DC Without a change in flux there is no inductive reactance: only the wire resistance remains, and it is very low. An autotransformer “does not isolate” and there was an accident Correct: it shares a conductor between input and output. To isolate you need one with two separate windings. Spark when disconnecting an instrument from a CT The secondary of the current transformer was opened. It must be short-circuited first."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Transformers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment A 220 V to 24 V transformer has 1100 turns on the primary: how many does the secondary have?. turns. If the secondary delivers 4 A, what current does the primary of the previous example draw?. Check by power: and. How do iron losses differ from copper losses? The iron losses (hysteresis and eddy currents) depend on the voltage and frequency and are practically constant: they exist even at no load. The copper losses are and grow with the square of the load. At what load is the efficiency highest? When the copper losses equal the iron losses. With and at full load, that happens at of the rated load. What is the short-circuit test for, and why does it not destroy the transformer? To measure the copper losses and the short-circuit impedance. It does not destroy it because only 3 to 6 % of the rated voltage is applied: just what is needed for rated current to flow. Why is the core made of laminations and not solid iron? To cut down the eddy currents. In a solid block, the varying flux induces internal currents that heat it uselessly. Laminating and insulating the sheets greatly reduces that loss. You want to match a 4 Ω load to a 900 Ω source: what turns ratio is needed?. That is, 15 turns on the primary for every one on the secondary. What is the difference between an autotransformer and an isolation transformer? The autotransformer has a single winding with a tap: input and output share a conductor and there is no galvanic isolation. The isolation transformer "},{"t":"How it is built","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#construccion","x":"01 How it is built A bipolar transistor (BJT, Bipolar Junction Transistor ) is two PN junctions sharing a very thin central region. Depending on the order of the materials there are two types: NPN and PNP."},{"t":"Transistor action","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#funcionamiento","x":"02 Transistor action For an NPN to work in its useful region, two conditions are needed at the same time: The base-emitter junction forward biased ( ). The base-collector junction reverse biased (the collector more positive than the base). Once both are met, the following happens. The heavily doped emitter injects a flood of electrons into the base. Since the base is extremely thin and lightly doped, only 1% of those electrons find a hole to recombine with; the remaining 99% cross the base and are captured by the collector's field. Result: a very small current flows through the base, and a current a hundred times larger flows through the collector — and the small one controls the large one. Kirchhoff's current law applied to the transistor. The current entering through the base plus the current entering through the collector all leaves through the emitter (in an NPN). The idea to hold on to The bipolar transistor is a current-controlled current source. It does not amplify energy out of nothing: it takes energy from the power supply and lets more or less through depending on what the base tells it. It is a faucet, and the base is the handle."},{"t":"DC current gain","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#ganancia","x":"03 DC current gain The ratio between the collector current and the base current is the gain β (beta), which appears in datasheets as h FE. β is typically 100 to 300. α is always slightly less than 1 (0.99 for ). β is not a reliable figure This is the most important point of the whole section. In the datasheet of a BC547, β is listed as 110 to 800: a range of 7 to 1. It also changes with temperature and with collector current. That is why no well-designed circuit depends on the value of β. The entire topic of biasing that follows exists precisely for this reason. Worked example A transistor with has. Calculate I C, I E and α. As you can see, with an error of less than 1%. That approximation is used constantly and greatly simplifies the calculations."},{"t":"The three operating regions","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#zonas","x":"04 The three operating regions Region B-E junction B-C junction V CE Behavior Use Cutoff Reverse (or < 0.6 V) Reverse I C ≈ 0. Open switch. Digital Active Forward (0.7 V) Reverse between 0.2 and V CC. Amplifies. Analog Saturation Forward Forward I C is set by the load, not by β. Closed switch. Digital There is a fourth region, the reverse region (emitter and collector interchanged), which is hardly ever used: the gain falls below 5 because the structure is not symmetrical. How to tell which region it is in, by measuring With the transistor operating, measure V CE: → it is in cutoff. → it is saturated. V CE intermediate (ideally ) → it is in the active region."},{"t":"Characteristic curves","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#curvas","x":"05 Characteristic curves A transistor is not described by a single curve but by a family of them: I C as a function of V CE, one curve for each value of I B. Two regions can be distinguished: Initial knee (V CE < 0.2 V): the current rises very quickly. This is the saturation region. Flat section: I C hardly changes even as V CE rises. This is the active region, where the transistor behaves as a current source. The slight slope of the flat section is due to the Early effect (base-width modulation) and is the reason why the output resistance of the transistor is not infinite."},{"t":"Load line and Q-point","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#recta","x":"06 Load line and Q-point The curves describe the transistor; the load line describes the external circuit. Where they cross is the actual operating point, called the Q-point (from quiescent, at rest). Applying Kirchhoff's law to the collector-emitter loop of a circuit with collector resistor R C: DC load line equation. This is the equation of a straight line, and to draw it its two end points are enough: Cutoff end. The transistor does not conduct and the entire supply voltage drops across it. Saturation end. The transistor conducts as much as the load allows. Where to put the Q-point It depends on what the transistor is used for: Amplifier: in the middle of the line ( ). That way the signal can swing up and down equally without clipping. This is the maximum symmetrical swing. Switch: jumping between the two end points (cutoff and saturation), without staying in the middle. At the end points it dissipates almost no power. Power dissipation The power the transistor dissipates is. In cutoff and in saturation: in both cases the power is negligible. The midpoint of the line is precisely the point of maximum dissipation, and that is why a class A amplifier heats up even with no signal."},{"t":"The transistor as a switch","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#llave","x":"07 The transistor as a switch This is the most immediate application and the one that connects with Digital Electronics I: the output of a logic gate cannot drive a relay, a motor or a lamp, but it can drive the base of a transistor that can. Worked example · Calculating R B You want to saturate a BC547 (guaranteed minimum β = 110) to drive a 12 V relay that draws 30 mA. The control signal comes from a 5 V digital output. 1. Minimum base current to reach saturation: 2. Apply an overdrive factor of 3 to 5 to ensure saturation even with the worst-case β and at low temperature. With a factor of 4:. 3. The base resistor: Adopt 3.9 kΩ ( standard value ) or 4.7 kΩ if a little less margin is acceptable. 4. Dissipation check: saturated, and. A BC547 (500 mW) is more than enough. Inductive loads Relays, motors, solenoids and coils store energy in their magnetic field. When the current is cut off abruptly, that energy is released as a voltage spike of hundreds of volts ( ). The flyback diode gives that current a path and holds it at 0.7 V. Without it, the transistor breaks down on the first switching."},{"t":"Biasing","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#polarizacion","x":"08 Biasing Biasing means setting the Q-point and, above all, making it stay there even when the temperature or the transistor changes. This is what separates a circuit that works from one that works only sometimes. Fixed-bias (fixed-base) biasing A single resistor between V CC and the base. It is the simplest and the worst. The problem is obvious: I C is directly proportional to β. If the transistor is replaced by another of the same type but with instead of 100, the collector current triples and the Q-point goes into saturation. The circuit stops amplifying. And since β also increases with temperature, the circuit drifts on its own while operating. Voltage-divider biasing This is the one used in practice. Two resistors set the base voltage and an emitter resistor introduces negative feedback. The calculation has four steps, and β appears in none of them: Calculation of the Q-point with a voltage divider. Valid if the divider current is at least 10 times I B, a condition that is met by choosing. Worked example · The Q-point and its stability,,,,. Stability test: if the transistor is replaced by one with a β three times higher, the calculation does not change at all, because β does not appear in any formula. With fixed bias, the same substitution would triple I C. That is the whole point of the voltage divider. Why R E stabilizes It is a negative-feedback loop: if I C tends to rise because of temperature, rises. Since V B is fixed by the divider, decreases, and that reduces I "},{"t":"Regulated power supplies with transistors","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#fuente","x":"09 Regulated power supplies with transistors The Zener regulator is only good for small currents. By adding a transistor in an emitter follower configuration, the available current is multiplied by β. The output is. The Zener only has to deliver, that is, a few milliamperes, while the transistor handles the entire load current. With, a Zener that could handle 50 mA now allows 5 A — limited, of course, by the transistor's power dissipation: With 18 V in, 12 V out and 1 A, the transistor dissipates 6 W: it definitely needs a heat sink. 🔋 Regulated power supply with the LM317 The integrated version of this same circuit, with built-in thermal protection and current limiting. It walks through the four stages with the real waveforms. ›"},{"t":"Packages, identification and power dissipation","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#encapsulados","x":"10 Packages, identification and power dissipation Transistor Type I C max. V CEO P D Typical β Package BC547 NPN 100 mA 45 V 500 mW 110-800 TO-92 BC557 PNP 100 mA 45 V 500 mW 110-800 TO-92 (complement of the 547) 2N2222 NPN 800 mA 40 V 500 mW 100-300 TO-92 / TO-18 BD139 NPN 1.5 A 80 V 12.5 W 40-160 TO-126, with heat sink TIP31C NPN 3 A 100 V 40 W 10-50 TO-220 TIP120 NPN Darlington 5 A 60 V 65 W ≥ 1000 TO-220 The official datasheets are at onsemi.com (Texas Instruments does not make generic BJTs). The pinout is not universal: a BC547 in a TO-92 package, viewed from the front with the flat face toward the viewer, has C-B-E from left to right; a 2N2222 in the same package has E-B-C. Mixing them up is the most common mistake in the shop. Power dissipation and heat sinks The P D in the datasheet is valid at a case temperature of 25 °C, which almost never happens. In practice a safety factor is applied: do not exceed half of the rated power, and fit a heat sink with silicone thermal grease as soon as you go past 1 W. Watch out: in many TO-220 packages the metal tab is connected to the collector, so if two transistors are bolted to the same heat sink you must use an insulating mica pad and a plastic shoulder washer."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#laboratorio","x":"11 In the lab Lab 1 · Identifying the leads and type with a multimeter A transistor is “two diodes back to back.” On the diode range: Look for the lead that, with the red probe held fixed, reads against the other two: that is the base and the transistor is an NPN. If that happens with the black probe held fixed, it is a PNP. Between the two remaining leads the reading must be OL in both directions. If it conducts, the transistor is shorted. Of those two, the emitter reads a few hundredths of a volt more than the collector against the base (0.72 vs 0.68 V, approximately). If the multimeter has an h FE function, you can confirm by trying both orientations: the one that gives the higher gain is the correct one. Lab 2 · Measuring β Build the circuit with from 12 V to the base and from 12 V to the collector, emitter to ground. Measure the drop across R B and across R C, calculate I B and I C, and obtain β. Repeat with three transistors of the same type and compare: the spread among them is the lesson of the exercise. Lab 3 · Cutoff and saturation A transistor with an LED in the collector (with its resistor) and a pushbutton that takes the base to 5 V through 4.7 kΩ. Measure V CE with the pushbutton released (it should read ) and pressed (it should read ). Calculate the power dissipated in both cases and check that it is minimal. Lab 4 · Q-point stability Build two circuits: one with fixed bias and one with a voltage divider, adjusted for the same initial Q-point. Replace the trans"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#errores","x":"12 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The transistor never conducts Leads reversed (emitter and collector swapped), or V BE below 0.6 V. It always conducts, even with no signal The base resistor is missing, or the divider is miscalculated and left it saturated. It gets very hot with no load Q-point in the middle of the line with high current. This is normal in class A, but it is worth checking the dissipation. The transistor burns out when the relay is switched off The flyback diode is missing. It works with one transistor but not with another identical one The circuit depends on β: fixed-bias biasing. You need to switch to a voltage divider. The output signal is clipped at the top or bottom Q-point off-center: the signal runs into saturation or cutoff before completing the cycle. It works cold and fails when it warms up Thermal drift. R E is missing or too small."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Bipolar transistors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares#autoevaluacion","x":"13 Self-assessment Why does the base of a transistor have to be thin and lightly doped? So that the carriers injected by the emitter find nothing to recombine with and cross over to the collector. If the base were thick or heavily doped, most of them would recombine, I B would be large and β small: there would be no transistor action. Can a transistor be used with the emitter and collector interchanged? It works, but very poorly: the gain falls below 5 and the reverse voltage that the base-emitter junction can withstand is only 5 or 6 V. The three regions have different doping levels and sizes, so the transistor is not symmetrical. A BC547 with has. What are I C and I E ?.. In practice, is assumed. What are the two end points of the load line if and ? Cutoff: with. Saturation: with V CE = 0. For maximum swing, the Q-point goes at with. Why is fixed-bias biasing bad? Because depends directly on β, which varies by up to 7 to 1 between units of the same model and also increases with temperature. The Q-point shifts when the transistor is replaced or when it heats up, and the circuit stops working. Explain in one sentence how R E stabilizes the circuit. If I C rises, V E rises; since V B is fixed by the divider, drops and the current falls back down. It is negative feedback. Why is the base overdriven when the transistor is used as a switch? To guarantee saturation with the worst possible β and at any temperature. 3 to 5 times the theoretical minimum I B is used. If the transistor"},{"t":"Why vary the frequency","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#por-que","x":"01 Why vary the frequency In AC motors it was established that the synchronous speed is. With 50 Hz and four poles, that is 1500 rpm, and there is no way to change it: the mains is in charge. Method Range Problem Valve or damper Whole flow range The motor keeps drawing the same power: the outlet is throttled and the energy is thrown away. Pulleys and gearboxes Fixed It can't be adjusted while running. Pole changing Two or three speeds Special motors only, in steps. Variable-frequency drive Continuous, from 0 to over 50 Hz Equipment cost and harmonics on the mains. Example · Why it pays for itself on a pump In a centrifugal pump, the flow is proportional to the speed, but the power goes with the cube of the speed. At 80 % speed, the power consumed is. At 50 %, it is. Throttling with a valve, on the other hand, barely lowers the consumption. That is why on pumps and fans the drive pays for itself within months, and it is the main reason they are everywhere."},{"t":"What is inside","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#partes","x":"02 What is inside Stage What it does Practical detail Rectifier Converts the mains AC into DC Three-phase diode bridge: on 380 V it gives about 537 V DC. DC bus Stores energy in large capacitors They stay charged above 500 V for minutes after disconnection. It is the greatest hazard of the equipment. Inverter Six IGBTs that build the output AC by PWM 2 to 16 kHz carrier ( power supplies ). Control Microcontroller or DSP that computes the modulation Holds the parameters, the protections and the communications. Braking Resistor or return to the mains When braking, the motor generates and raises the bus voltage: that energy has to be burned off or returned."},{"t":"The volts-per-hertz law","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#vhz","x":"03 The volts-per-hertz law It is the central concept of the topic. If the frequency is lowered without lowering the voltage, the magnetic flux of the motor grows until it saturates the iron: the current shoots up and the motor burns out. For 380 V and 50 Hz, the ratio is 7.6 volts per hertz. 25 Hz corresponds to 190 V; 10 Hz, to 76 V. Below rated: constant torque V and f rise proportionally, the flux is maintained and the motor can deliver its rated torque at any speed. It is the usual working region. At very low frequency a voltage boost is needed to compensate for the drop across the winding resistance, which at 2 or 3 Hz eats up almost all the voltage. Above rated: constant power The voltage cannot exceed the mains voltage, so V/f drops, the flux drops and the torque falls in inverse proportion to the frequency. At 100 Hz the motor gives half the torque. The mechanical limit must also be checked: bearings, balancing and the motor's own ventilation. Motor cooling also drops with speed The fan of a self-ventilated motor is mounted on its own shaft: at half speed it ventilates much less, yet the motor can keep delivering the same torque and the same current. That is why prolonged operation at low speed with rated torque calls for independent forced ventilation or an oversized motor. It is one of the most common causes of burnt-out motors in installations with drives."},{"t":"Types of control","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#control","x":"04 Types of control Control How it works When it is used Scalar V/f Applies the V/f curve without measuring anything from the motor Pumps, fans, conveyors: loads that don't demand torque at zero speed. It is the simplest and cheapest. Sensorless vector Estimates the flux position from the measured currents When torque is needed at low speed and good dynamic response: elevators, extruders. Closed-loop vector Uses an encoder on the shaft Positioning and exact torque at zero speed: machine tools, cranes. The parameters that must always be entered Motor nameplate data: voltage, current, frequency, speed, cos φ. Without this the drive cannot protect it. Acceleration and deceleration ramps: if they are too short, the drive trips on overcurrent or on DC bus overvoltage. Minimum and maximum frequency. Current limit and thermal protection. Command source: keypad, terminal block, communication. Carrier frequency: the higher it is, the quieter the motor and the more heat in the drive."},{"t":"Installation: what ruins a drive","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#instalacion","x":"05 Installation: what ruins a drive Never between the drive and the motor No switching device —contactor, switch, compensation capacitors— is placed between the drive output and the motor. Opening that circuit while the drive is delivering current destroys the output stage. If isolation is needed, it is done with the drive stopped and with an auxiliary contact that signals the drive. Shielded cable and grounds The PWM output has nanosecond edges: it radiates a great deal. The cable to the motor must be shielded, with the shield grounded at both ends and over 360°, not with a “pigtail.” Without that, faults appear in sensors, in communications and even in neighboring equipment. Other precautions Bearing currents: the switching induces voltages on the shaft that discharge through the bearing and pit it. With long cables or large motors, insulated bearings or shaft grounding brushes are used. Harmonics toward the mains: the input rectifier draws current in peaks. It is corrected with a line or DC-bus reactor, and in large installations with filters ( AC power ). Enclosure ventilation: the drive dissipates 2 to 4 % of the power it handles. A 15 kW unit can be giving off 500 W inside the cabinet. Distance to the motor: long cables produce reflections that double the voltage at the motor terminals. Beyond a few tens of meters an output filter is needed. Before opening a drive Cut the power, wait the time the manufacturer indicates —typically 5 to 15 minutes — and verify with an ins"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Parameterize and start With a training drive and a small motor, enter the nameplate data and the ramps, and start from the keypad. Use a tachometer to measure the speed at 10, 25, 50 and 60 Hz and compare it with. Note the slip at each point. Lab 2 · The V/f curve, measured Use a True RMS multimeter to measure the output voltage at different frequencies and build the table of V versus f. Verify that the ratio stays constant up to the rated frequency and that after that the voltage flattens out. Plot it. Lab 3 · Ramps and protections Shorten the acceleration ramp until the drive trips on overcurrent, and the braking ramp until it trips on DC bus overvoltage. Note the limit values and explain each trip. It is the best way to understand what each parameter protects. Lab 4 · Carrier and noise Change the carrier frequency between 2 and 12 kHz and listen to the motor: the characteristic whine changes pitch until it becomes inaudible. Measure the drive's temperature in each case: silence is paid for with heat."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause Trips on overcurrent at start-up Ramp too short for the inertia, or current limit set wrong. Trips on overvoltage when braking The motor returns energy and the bus voltage rises. Lengthen the ramp or add a braking resistor. The motor heats up at low speed Insufficient self-ventilation. Forced ventilation is needed. No strength at low frequency Voltage boost missing, or the application requires vector control. Faults in sensors and communications when the motor starts Output cable unshielded or shield badly connected. Bearings pitted after a few months Shaft currents induced by the switching. The drive was destroyed when an output contactor opened Never disconnect between drive and motor while the equipment is running. The motor vibrates and makes odd noise Low carrier, or mechanical resonance at a certain frequency: solved with “skip frequencies” in the parameter setup."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Variable-speed drives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why does the voltage have to be lowered when the frequency is lowered? Because the magnetic flux is proportional to V/f: if f drops and V doesn't, the flux grows, the iron saturates and the magnetizing current shoots up, burning out the motor. A 380 V, 50 Hz motor runs at 30 Hz. What voltage should the drive apply? The ratio is, so —plus the boost that compensates for the resistive drop—. What happens above the rated frequency? The voltage cannot rise any further, so V/f falls: the flux drops and the available torque decreases. The motor enters the constant-power region and its mechanical limit must also be checked. Why does a pump with a drive save so much energy? Because the power of a centrifugal pump varies with the cube of the speed: at 80 % speed it consumes 51 %, and at 50 %, barely 12.5 %. Throttling with a valve does not lower the consumption. What are the three stages of a drive? Rectifier (mains to DC), DC bus with its capacitors, and inverter of six IGBTs that builds the output AC by PWM. Why isn't a contactor placed between the drive and the motor? Because opening it while the drive is delivering current destroys the output stage: an inductive current is abruptly interrupted. If isolation is needed, it is done with the drive stopped. What is the difference between V/f control and vector control? V/f applies a curve without measuring the state of the motor: it is simple and sufficient for pumps and fans. Vector control estimates or measures the "},{"t":"From the phenomenon to the number","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#cadena","x":"01 From the phenomenon to the number To measure a temperature, a force or a pressure, a sensor converts the quantity into an electrical signal: a resistance that changes, a voltage of microvolts, a current. That signal can almost never be connected directly to the converter. Signal conditioning is everything between the two: exciting the sensor, amplifying, filtering, isolating, compensating and linearizing. The errors of that chain are usually larger than those of the sensor itself. Sensors and actuators are presented in the technician program, in sensors and actuators."},{"t":"Temperature sensors","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#temperatura","x":"02 Temperature sensors Sensor Principle Typical range and accuracy Conditioning Pt100 (RTD) Platinum resistance, 100 Ω at 0 °C, rises 0.385 Ω/°C −200 to 600 °C, ±0.3 °C (class B at 0 °C) Excitation current, three or four wires Thermocouple Seebeck voltage between two metals: about 41 µV/°C for type K −200 to 1250 °C (K), ±2 °C Cold-junction compensation and high amplification NTC thermistor Resistance that falls sharply with temperature −40 to 150 °C, very sensitive Divider and Steinhart-Hart linearization Integrated (LM35 and similar) Junction voltage of transistors, already calibrated −55 to 150 °C, ±0.5 °C Almost none: 10 mV/°C The resistance of a Pt100 follows the Callendar-Van Dusen equation, which for positive temperatures is: \\[ R(T) = R_0\\left(1 + A\\,T + B\\,T^2\\right) \\] With \\( R_0 = 100\\ \\Omega \\), \\( A = 3.9083 \\times 10^{-3} \\) and \\( B = -5.775 \\times 10^{-7} \\) according to IEC 60751. At 100 °C it gives 138.51 Ω. Assuming a linear relationship with 0.385%/°C gives zero error at 0 and 100 °C, but almost 9 °C at 300 °C."},{"t":"Strain gauges and bridges","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#puentes","x":"03 Strain gauges and bridges A strain gauge is a resistor that stretches with the part it is bonded to. Its relative change is proportional to the strain \\( \\varepsilon \\), with a gauge factor close to 2. Strains are in thousandths, so the change in resistance is tiny, and it is measured in a bridge: \\[ \\frac{\\Delta R}{R} = GF\\cdot\\varepsilon \\] \\[ V_o \\approx V_{exc}\\,\\frac{GF\\,\\varepsilon}{4} \\] For a quarter bridge, with a single active gauge. With 1000 microstrain, a factor of 2 and 5 V of excitation, the output is 2.5 mV. With four active gauges, four times more, and the effect of temperature cancels. Load cells and piezoresistive pressure sensors are full bridges specified in millivolts per volt of excitation: a 2 mV/V cell excited with 10 V gives 20 mV at full load. That is why they are measured ratiometrically: the converter uses the same excitation voltage as its reference, and its variations cancel."},{"t":"The instrumentation amplifier","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#ina","x":"04 The instrumentation amplifier The output of a bridge is a difference of millivolts sitting on a common voltage of several volts. An ordinary operational amplifier would also amplify that common voltage and the errors of its resistors. The three-op-amp instrumentation amplifier has a high-impedance differential input, a gain set with a single resistor and a common-mode rejection of 100 dB or more: \\[ G = 1 + \\frac{2 R}{R_G} \\] \\( R \\) are the internal resistors of the first stage. In integrated circuits the formula is given directly, for example \\( G = 1 + 50\\ \\text{k}\\Omega/R_G \\). Before the converter goes an anti-aliasing low-pass filter, and in industrial environments, an isolator that breaks the ground connection between the sensor and the system."},{"t":"Excitation, cables and self-heating","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#excitacion","x":"05 Excitation, cables and self-heating A two-wire Pt100 adds the resistance of the cables to that of the sensor: 10 m of thin cable has almost 1 Ω per conductor, and 1.7 Ω is equivalent to 4.5 °C of error. The three-wire connection compensates the cables in a bridge, assuming they are equal; the four-wire one carries the current through two and measures the voltage through the other two, with no current, and eliminates the error completely. The excitation current heats the sensor, which then measures a little more than the ambient. With a self-heating coefficient of 0.4 °C/mW, 1 mA in a Pt100 produces 0.06 °C of error; 5 mA, 1.4 °C. More current gives more signal, but not a better measurement. Lab · the chain of a Pt100 A Pt100 10 m from the equipment, with cable of 0.84 Ω per conductor, excited with a constant current. An instrumentation amplifier takes the 0 to 300 °C range to the 4.096 V of a 12-bit ADC. The graph shows the error of each source over the whole range; the dot, the chosen temperature. Two-wire Three-wire Four-wire 0.5 mA excitation 1 mA excitation 5 mA excitation Conversion with Callendar-Van Dusen Linear conversion (0.385%/°C) Actual temperature ="},{"t":"Other non-electrical parameters","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#otros","x":"06 Other non-electrical parameters Quantity Usual sensor Output Force and weight Load cell with strain gauges Bridge of 1 to 3 mV/V Pressure Piezoresistive diaphragm Bridge, or 4 to 20 mA already conditioned Position Potentiometer, LVDT, optical encoder Voltage, modulated AC or pulses Illumination Photodiode Current, with a transimpedance amplifier Humidity Capacitive Capacitance, or digital over I²C In industry, many transmitters deliver a current of 4 to 20 mA: the current is not lost in long cables, and the zero at 4 mA makes it possible to detect a cut cable, which would give 0 mA."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#laboratorio","x":"07 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Pt100 with two, three and four wires Measure a Pt100 in ice water and in boiling water with the three connections, through 10 m of cable. Calculate the error of each. Activity 2 · A scale Condition a load cell with an instrumentation amplifier and a 24-bit ADC, calibrate with two known weights and measure the repeatability. Activity 3 · Cold junction Measure with a type K thermocouple and an amplifier without compensation, and then with a cold-junction compensation IC. Compare with a reference thermometer."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Measuring a two-wire Pt100 with long cables. Each ohm of cable is 2.6 °C. Raising the excitation to get more signal. Self-heating grows. Forgetting the cold junction of a thermocouple. The reading is shifted by the temperature of the terminals. Amplifying a bridge with a simple op amp. The common-mode voltage and the mismatched resistors add errors larger than the signal."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What resistance does a Pt100 have at 50 °C? \\( 100\\,(1 + 0.195 - 0.00144) \\approx 119.40\\ \\Omega \\). What output does a quarter bridge give with a gauge factor of 2, at 500 µε and 10 V of excitation? \\( 10 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 500 \\cdot 10^{-6} / 4 = 2.5 \\) mV. What gain resistor gives \\( G = 101 \\) with the formula \\( 1 + 50\\ \\text{k}\\Omega / R_G \\)? 500 Ω. Why do industrial transmitters use 4 to 20 mA and not 0 to 20 mA? To distinguish the minimum of the scale from a cut cable, and so that the transmitter can be powered from the 4 mA of the loop."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Signal conditioning and sensors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Ramón Pallás Areny. Sensores y acondicionadores de señal (in Spanish). 4th ed., Marcombo, 2003. The reference in Spanish: each type of sensor with its conditioning circuit. Jacob Fraden. Handbook of Modern Sensors. 5th ed., Springer, 2016. The physics of all sensors and their interfaces. Walt Kester (ed.). Sensor Signal Conditioning. Analog Devices, 1999. Bridges, instrumentation amplifiers and temperature conditioning with real circuits."},{"t":"The expert witness, the judge’s technical eyes","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#perito","x":"01 The expert witness, the judge’s technical eyes Many lawsuits depend on a technical question: whether a fire started in a poorly protected switchboard, whether a medical device failed because of its design or because of misuse, whether an installation meets what was contracted. The judge knows law, not electronics. When the decision requires special knowledge of a science or a technique, expert evidence is ordered, and a professional in the field studies the question and explains it in a report. The expert is an auxiliary of the court: not working for either of the parties, but for the tribunal, and their duty is impartiality. At the national level, expert evidence is regulated in Articles 457 to 478 of the Código Procesal Civil y Comercial de la Nación (National Code of Civil and Commercial Procedure); each province has its own procedural code, with very similar rules."},{"t":"Court-appointed expert and party technical consultant","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#roles","x":"02 Court-appointed expert and party technical consultant Court-appointed expert (perito de oficio) Party technical consultant (consultor técnico de parte) Who appoints them The judge, by lot from a list of registered professionals Each party, freely Whom they serve The tribunal: they must be impartial The party that proposes them: they defend its technical position What they do Studies, measures, tests and submits the expert report Attends the operations, monitors the expert’s work and may submit their own report Who pays Fees set by the judge, generally paid by the party that loses the case The party that hired them To be a court-appointed expert you must register on the lists kept by the courts of each jurisdiction, with a degree and a license in the specialty."},{"t":"How an expert works","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#proceso","x":"03 How an expert works Accepting the appointment: the designated expert accepts before the tribunal within the time limit set by the code, and may ask for an advance to cover the expenses of the expert examination. Studying the case file: reads the complaint, the answer and, above all, the expert questions (puntos de pericia): the specific questions that the parties and the judge put to the expert. Expert operations: inspections, measurements, tests, with prior notice to the parties and their consultants so that they can attend. Report: within the set deadline, with an explanation of the technical operations carried out and the scientific principles on which the conclusions are based. Clarifications and challenges: the parties may request clarifications or object to the report, and the expert must respond. The judge is not obliged to follow the expert opinion: it is weighed according to the rules of sound critical judgment (sana crítica). But a well-founded report is very hard to set aside, and in practice it decides many trials."},{"t":"The expert report","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#informe","x":"04 The expert report Part What it contains Heading Case caption, court, identification and license number of the expert Purpose The expert questions, transcribed as they were proposed Background What emerges from the case file and the documentation consulted Methodology and operations What was inspected, with what instruments, calibrated when, and against which standards it was compared Answers One for each expert question, clear, well-founded and not going beyond what was asked Conclusions and annexes Summary, photographs, measurements, drawings What an expert does not do An expert does not give opinions on legal questions —whether there was fault or who must pay is the judge’s job—, does not answer what was not asked and does not state what could not be verified. An opinion that knowingly departs from the truth is the crime of false testimony, which Article 275 of the Criminal Code expressly extends to experts."},{"t":"Arbitration","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#arbitraje","x":"05 Arbitration The parties to a contract can agree that their disputes will be resolved not by a judge but by arbitrators chosen by them: this is the arbitration agreement, regulated in Articles 1649 to 1665 of the Civil and Commercial Code. For technical disputes it has clear advantages: the arbitrators can be specialists, the procedure is faster and confidential, and the decision, the award (laudo), is binding and is enforced like a court judgment. Type How it decides Arbitration in law (arbitraje de derecho) Applying legal rules, like a judge. It is the type presumed if nothing was agreed Amiable compositeurs (amigables componedores) According to equity, to the best of their knowledge and judgment, if the parties expressly authorized it Not everything can be arbitrated: the Code excludes, among others, family matters, consumer relations and labor matters. An engineer can take part as an arbitrator on a technical panel, as an expert within an arbitration or as a party to a contract with an arbitration clause. Before a trial, moreover, many jurisdictions require mandatory mediation, in which a mediator helps the parties to reach an agreement without deciding for them."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Case What the expert is asked Fire in a shop Whether the origin was electrical, at what point, and whether the installation met the regulations Claim over defective equipment Whether the failure is one of design, manufacture or use Dispute over a structured cabling job Whether what was installed meets the specification and the certification standards Fraud with an energy meter Whether the device was tampered with and how Computer forensics The content and integrity of digital devices and records"},{"t":"Activities","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#laboratorio","x":"07 Activities Activity 1 · Expert questions For a fire attributed to a power strip, write five expert questions that each party could propose. Activity 2 · An expert report Write the expert report for a simulated case, using the structure on this page and answering point by point. Activity 3 · Registering as an expert Find out what requirements the courts of your jurisdiction set for registering on the list of electronic engineering experts."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Giving an opinion on who is legally right. The expert answers technical questions. Carrying out the operations without notifying the parties. They can challenge the expert examination because they could not monitor it. Acting as a court-appointed expert while having a tie to one of the parties. The expert must recuse themselves. Agreeing to amiable-compositeur arbitration without meaning to. If it is not expressly authorized, the arbitration is in law."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the difference between a court-appointed expert and a technical consultant? The court-appointed expert is designated by the judge and must be impartial; the technical consultant is proposed by a party and defends its position. Is the judge obliged to follow the expert report? No: it is weighed according to the rules of sound critical judgment. What does an expert who knowingly departs from the truth risk? Committing false testimony (Article 275 of the Criminal Code). How does an amiable compositeur decide? According to equity, if the parties expressly authorized it."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Expert witness work and arbitration","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Lino Enrique Palacio. Manual de derecho procesal civil. Abeledo Perrot (in Spanish). The chapter on expert evidence, with the complete procedure. Código Procesal Civil y Comercial de la Nación (National Code of Civil and Commercial Procedure). Articles 457 to 478. InfoLEG (in Spanish). The rules on expert examination at the national level. Roque J. Caivano. Arbitraje. Ad-Hoc (in Spanish). The reference work on arbitration in Argentina."},{"t":"Why match","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#porque","x":"01 Why match An RF power transistor presents a few ohms at its output; an antenna, 50 Ω; a low-noise amplifier delivers its best noise figure with a particular source impedance. Connecting them directly wastes power and, on lines, produces the standing waves of transmission lines. A lossless matching network transforms one impedance into another at one frequency: \\[ P_{L,\\max} = \\frac{|V_s|^2}{8 R_s} \\quad \\text{if} \\quad Z_L = Z_s^* \\] Maximum power transfer is obtained with the conjugate of the source as the load. In a power amplifier, on the other hand, the load chosen is the one that gives the required power and efficiency, which is not the conjugate: the network transforms the antenna into that load."},{"t":"The L network","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#redl","x":"02 The L network The simplest network has two reactances: one in series on the side of the lower resistance and another in parallel on the side of the higher one. The ratio between the resistances sets its quality factor, and the reactances follow from it: \\[ Q = \\sqrt{\\frac{R_{high}}{R_{low}} - 1} \\] \\[ X_{series} = Q\\, R_{low}, \\quad X_{parallel} = \\frac{R_{high}}{Q} \\] With a series inductor and a parallel capacitor, the network is low-pass and also attenuates harmonics; with the components swapped, high-pass. To take 5 Ω to 50 Ω at 100 MHz, Q = 3: 23.9 nH in series and 95.5 pF in parallel. The Q is not free: it is set by the transformation ratio, and with it the bandwidth of the match, which is approximately inversely proportional to Q. A large transformation in a single step is narrowband. Lab · an L network between 50 Ω and a load A low-pass L network that matches a resistive load to a 50 Ω source at 100 MHz. The curve is the return loss seen from the source; the dotted line, 10 dB, the usual threshold of an acceptable match. Load resistance R L ="},{"t":"π and T networks, multiple sections and transformers","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#otras","x":"03 π and T networks, multiple sections and transformers With three elements, the π and T networks make it possible to choose the Q independently of the transformation ratio: the path goes through a virtual intermediate resistance. They serve to narrow the band, for example to filter harmonics at the output of a transmitter. To widen the band the opposite is done: several L networks in cascade with intermediate resistances in geometric progression, each with a lower Q. At higher frequencies, the networks are built with line sections and stubs, and designed on the Smith chart. Broadband transformers, with a ferrite core or a transmission-line type, give fixed ratios over several octaves."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Design and measure Design an L network that matches a 12 Ω resistance to 50 Ω at 50 MHz, build it with SMD components and measure the return loss with a network analyzer. Activity 2 · Two sections Redesign the same match with two L networks and an intermediate resistance of √(12·50) ≈ 24.5 Ω, and compare the bandwidth. Activity 3 · Smith chart Plot on the Smith chart the path of the impedance as the components are added."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the parasitics of the components. At hundreds of megahertz a capacitor has inductance and an inductor, capacitance. Expecting broadband from a large transformation in one step. The high Q prevents it; several sections are needed. Matching a power amplifier to the conjugate. It is matched to the load that gives the required power and efficiency. Ignoring inductor losses. With a high network Q, the circulating current dissipates in the winding resistance."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What Q does an L network that matches 10 Ω to 50 Ω have? \\( \\sqrt{5 - 1} = 2 \\). What reactances does it need in series and in parallel? 20 Ω in series on the 10 Ω side and 25 Ω in parallel on the 50 Ω side. What advantage does a low-pass L network have at the output of a transmitter? Besides matching, it attenuates harmonics. How is the bandwidth of a large-ratio match widened? With several sections in cascade and intermediate resistances in geometric progression."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Impedance matching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Chris Bowick. RF Circuit Design. 2nd ed., Newnes, 2008. L, π and T networks with numerical design examples. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2012. Matching with lumped elements, stubs and quarter-wave transformers. Les Besser and Rowan Gilmore. Practical RF Circuit Design for Modern Wireless Systems. Artech House, 2003. Impedance matching in amplifiers and the use of the Smith chart."},{"t":"Scope and work breakdown","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#alcance","x":"01 Scope and work breakdown Planning starts by defining the scope: what the project delivers and what is left out. Then it is divided into ever smaller parts, down to tasks that one person can estimate and control: this is the work breakdown structure. For each task, its estimated duration is noted, along with the resources it uses —people, instruments, money— and which other tasks it depends on: the prototype cannot be assembled before the board exists, nor tested before it is assembled."},{"t":"Gantt and critical path","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#gantt","x":"02 Gantt and critical path The Gantt chart, which was seen in the history of industrial organization, shows each task as a bar over time. The critical path method (CPM) calculates, with a forward pass, the earliest start of each task and the total duration; with a backward pass, the latest start that does not delay the end. The difference is the slack. The tasks with no slack form the critical path: any delay in them delays the whole project, and any effort to shorten it has to go there."},{"t":"Estimating with uncertainty: PERT","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#pert","x":"03 Estimating with uncertainty: PERT Durations are estimates. The PERT method asks for three per task —optimistic \\( a \\), most likely \\( m \\) and pessimistic \\( b \\)— and calculates an expected time and a variance. The project duration is approximated with a normal distribution, with the sum of the means and of the variances along the critical path: \\[ t_e = \\frac{a + 4m + b}{6}, \\quad \\sigma^2 = \\left(\\frac{b - a}{6}\\right)^2 \\] \\[ P(T \\le D) = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{D - T_e}{\\sigma_T}\\right) \\] \\( T_e \\) is the expected duration of the critical path, \\( \\sigma_T \\) the square root of the sum of its variances and \\( \\Phi \\) the cumulative normal distribution. It is an approximation: if there are near-critical paths, the actual probability of meeting the deadline is somewhat lower. Lab · critical path of a final project Ten tasks, in weeks (a, m, b): A specification (1, 2, 3); B hardware design (2, 4, 8), after A; C firmware (2, 5, 10), after A; D purchasing (1, m, 8), after B; E board fabrication (1, 2, 4), after B; F assembly (1, 1, 2), after D and E; G integration (2, 4, 9), after C and F; H testing (1, 2, 4) and I report (1, 2, 3), after G; J presentation (0.5, 1, 1.5), after H and I. In red, the critical path; in light gray, the slack of each task. Most likely duration of purchasing m = Delivery deadline D ="},{"t":"Resources, risks and monitoring","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#seguimiento","x":"04 Resources, risks and monitoring A plan without assigned resources is wishful thinking: if the same person designs the hardware and writes the firmware, those tasks are not parallel even if the chart says so. The risks identified in the technical analysis translate into time reserves and early actions, such as ordering long-lead components earlier. During the project, progress is made by milestones —approved specification, board working, integrated prototype, tests complete— and what has been done is compared with what was planned in order to correct in time. Agile methodologies propose short cycles with partial deliveries, useful above all in software. Responsibility for deadlines and for the quality of the work is part of professional practice."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Breakdown Build the work breakdown structure of the final project down to tasks of one or two weeks. Activity 2 · Gantt and critical path Enter the tasks, the dependencies and the three estimates into a project management tool and identify the critical path. Activity 3 · Monitoring Every two weeks, record the actual progress and compare it with the plan; decide what to do about the deviations."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Estimating only the most likely case. Tasks slip more than they come in early; the pessimistic estimate matters. Forgetting component lead times. They are often on the critical path. Speeding up tasks that have slack. They do not shorten the project; the effort goes to the critical path. Planning without assigning people. Two parallel tasks of the same person are not parallel."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the expected time of a task with a = 2, m = 4 and b = 8? \\( (2 + 16 + 8)/6 \\approx 4.33 \\) weeks. With the initial values of the lab, how long does the project take and what is the critical path? About 18.7 weeks, through A, B, D, F, G, H and J. What is the probability of finishing in 20 weeks? Around 74 %. What is the slack of a task? How much it can slip without delaying the end of the project."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Project planning and management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Project Management Institute. A Guide to the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK Guide). PMI. (Spanish edition: “Guía de los fundamentos para la dirección de proyectos (Guía del PMBOK)”.) The reference framework for project management. Harold Kerzner. Project Management: A Systems Approach to Planning, Scheduling, and Controlling. Wiley. Project planning, scheduling and control in detail. Frederick P. Brooks. The Mythical Man-Month. Addison-Wesley, 1995 anniversary edition. Why adding people to a late project makes it later."},{"t":"What an algorithm is","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#algoritmo","x":"01 What an algorithm is A cooking recipe, the instructions for assembling a piece of furniture and the procedure for measuring a resistance with a multimeter have something in common: they are steps that, followed to the letter, lead to a result. An algorithm is exactly that, with one more requirement: it must be possible for someone who does not understand the problem to follow it, such as a computer. Donald Knuth asks for five properties: Property What it requires A recipe violates it if… Finiteness It ends after a finite number of steps it says “stir until done” Definiteness Each step is precise and unambiguous it says “a pinch of salt” Input It has zero or more well-specified starting data it does not say how many servings it makes Output It produces at least one result related to the input — Effectiveness Each step can be done with pencil and paper in a finite time it asks for “just the right point” An algorithm and a program are not the same thing The algorithm is the idea, independent of the language. The program is an algorithm written in a language that a machine can execute. Euclid’s algorithm is 2300 years old; its programs, a few decades. First you think of the algorithm, then you write the program, and the most expensive mistakes are the ones made before touching the keyboard."},{"t":"A method for solving problems","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#metodo","x":"02 A method for solving problems In 1945 George Pólya proposed four phases for solving mathematics problems that fit programming exactly: Understand the problem. What are the data? What is being asked? What conditions apply? What unusual cases may appear? Devise a plan. Does it resemble a problem already solved? Can it be split into smaller problems? Carry out the plan, checking each step: in programming, coding. Examine the solution. Test it with known cases and with the unusual cases from phase 1. Example: equivalent resistance in parallel 1 · Understand. Data: N resistance values. Result: the equivalent resistance of the parallel combination. Condition: \\( \\frac{1}{R_{\\operatorname{eq}}} = \\sum_{i = 1}^N \\frac{1}{R_i} \\). Unusual cases: a 0 Ω resistor is a short circuit and makes \\( R_{eq} = 0 \\) (and the formula would divide by zero); a negative value is a data-entry error; \\( N = 0 \\) makes no sense. 2 · Plan. It is an accumulation: add up the conductances in an accumulator and invert at the end. 3 · Carry out. Write the pseudocode, then the program. 4 · Examine. Two equal 100 Ω resistors must give 50 Ω; 100 Ω and 0 Ω, 0 Ω; a single resistor, its own value. The unusual cases of phase 1 are the ones that get forgotten later. Writing them down before programming is half of testing."},{"t":"Tools for representing algorithms","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#representacion","x":"03 Tools for representing algorithms Before writing code it is advisable to express the algorithm in a notation that does not distract with details of the language. There are three classic ones: Pseudocode Structured natural language with keywords ( If, While, For ). It is the closest to code and the fastest to write. Flowchart Standardized symbols joined by arrows (ISO 5807). It shows the possible paths at a glance, but it gets tangled in long algorithms. Nassi-Shneiderman Also called a Chapin chart. Nested boxes with no arrows: by construction it only allows the permitted control structures."},{"t":"Three structures are enough for everything","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#estructuras","x":"04 Three structures are enough for everything In 1966 Corrado Böhm and Giuseppe Jacopini proved that any algorithm can be written by combining just three structures, each with one entry and one exit: Sequence One step after another. Selection Choosing a path according to a condition: If … then … else …, or Case for several cases. Iteration Repeating while a condition holds: While, Repeat … until, For. Since each structure has a single entry and a single exit, they can be nested like boxes, and each one can be reasoned about separately. That is structured programming, and it is the basis of the next topic. Iteration When it evaluates Minimum iterations Used when… While Before each iteration 0 no iterations may be needed at all Repeat … until After each iteration 1 something must be done at least once, such as reading a value and validating it For Before, with a counter 0 it is known in advance how many iterations there will be"},{"t":"The desk check","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#prueba","x":"05 The desk check Before trusting an algorithm you have to run it by hand, with a pencil, recording in a table the value of each variable every time it changes. This is the desk check, and it is the tool that finds the most errors per minute invested. The machine below does it step by step: choose an algorithm, enter the data and step through it. Lab · automatic desk check Greatest common divisor (Euclid) Decimal to binary (successive divisions) Maximum of a list Next step Run to the end Reset Desk-check table Why Euclid’s algorithm terminates That an algorithm terminates has to be proved. In Euclid’s, the remainder r is always smaller than b, so b decreases on every iteration and is a non-negative integer: it cannot decrease forever. And it is correct because \\( \\gcd(a,\\ b) = \\gcd(b,\\ a \\bmod b) \\): that equality holds on every iteration. A property that is preserved on every iteration is called a loop invariant."},{"t":"Stepwise refinement","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#refinamiento","x":"06 Stepwise refinement Niklaus Wirth proposed in 1971 designing from the top down: first write the solution in coarse steps and then replace each coarse step with its detail, until everything is executable. Each coarse step ends up being a function. // Level 1: the whole problem Read the resistance values Compute the equivalent resistance Display the result // Level 2: “Compute the equivalent resistance” g ← 0 For i ← 1 to N do If R[i] = 0 then Req ← 0; stop // there is a short circuit g ← g + 1 / R[i] EndFor Req ← 1 / g The advantage is not only order: at each level you can verify that the plan is correct without having written the detail yet."},{"t":"Correct is not enough: efficient too","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#eficiencia","x":"07 Correct is not enough: efficient too Two correct algorithms can take very different amounts of time. Efficiency is measured by counting how many operations they perform as a function of the input size n, regardless of the machine. Searching for a value in a list of n elements by scanning it takes, in the worst case, n comparisons. If the list is sorted, binary search looks at the middle element, discards the half that cannot contain it and repeats: in the worst case it makes \\( \\lfloor \\log_2 n \\rfloor + 1 \\) comparisons. Elements n Sequential search Binary search 10 10 4 1,000 1,000 10 1,000,000 1,000,000 20 The first is said to be of order n, O( n ), and the second of logarithmic order, O(log n ). With a million data items, a faster computer does not make up for choosing the wrong algorithm."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Exercise 1 · From problem to flowchart For an electronics problem —the nearest E12 standard value to a calculated resistance, or the power dissipated by each resistor in a divider— carry out Pólya’s four phases in writing, the flowchart and the desk check with three sets of data, one of them an unusual case. Exercise 2 · Counting the iterations Add an iteration counter to Euclid’s algorithm and test it with two consecutive Fibonacci numbers (89 and 55, 144 and 89). Compare with other pairs of the same size: the Fibonacci pairs are the worst case. Exercise 3 · With tools Write the pseudocode in PSeInt, which executes it and draws the flowchart by itself, and check that the output matches the desk check."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Starting to program without having understood the problem. It shows when the program “works” but solves something else. The off-by-one error (also called a fencepost error ): the loop runs one iteration too many or too few. It is detected in the desk check by looking at the first and last iterations. A loop that never ends because the condition variable does not change inside it. Testing only the easy case. You must test zero, one, the empty case and the extremes. Incorrectly negated conditions. The opposite of “a > 0 and b > 0” is “a ≤ 0 or b ≤ 0” (De Morgan’s laws)."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Why is “stir until it browns” not an algorithmic step? Because it violates definiteness: it does not say how to decide that it has browned. It does not guarantee finiteness either. What are the three control structures of Böhm and Jacopini? Sequence, selection and iteration. What is the difference between While and Repeat … until ? While evaluates the condition first and may never execute the body; Repeat evaluates afterward and executes it at least once. Do the desk check of Euclid’s algorithm with \\( a = 48 \\) and \\( b = 18 \\). \\( (48,\\ 18) \\to r = 12 \\to (18,\\ 12) \\to r = 6 \\to (12,\\ 6) \\to r = 0 \\to (6,\\ 0) \\). Result: 6. What is the maximum number of comparisons a binary search makes in 4096 sorted elements? \\( \\lfloor \\log_2 4096 \\rfloor + 1 = 12 + 1 = 13 \\). What is a loop invariant? A property that is true before entering the loop and holds after every iteration. On exit, together with the exit condition, it proves that the result is correct."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Luis Joyanes Aguilar. Fundamentos de programación. Algoritmos, estructura de datos y objetos. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008 (in Spanish). The reference text in Spanish for pseudocode, flowcharts and Nassi-Shneiderman diagrams. George Pólya. How to Solve It. 1945. Spanish edition: Trillas ( Cómo plantear y resolver problemas ). Short and still relevant: the four-phase method with dozens of examples. Niklaus Wirth. Algorithms + Data Structures = Programs. Spanish edition (“Algoritmos + estructuras de datos = programas”), Ediciones del Castillo, 1980. By the creator of Pascal and of stepwise refinement. The title is a whole declaration. Thomas H. Cormen, Charles E. Leiserson, Ronald L. Rivest and Clifford Stein. Introduction to Algorithms. 4th ed., MIT Press, 2022. For when you want to get serious about invariants, correctness and efficiency analysis."},{"t":"One frequency disguised as another","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#que","x":"01 One frequency disguised as another When the sampling frequency is not high enough, the replicas of the spectrum get into the useful band and a high signal appears in the place of a low one. This is called an alias, and the frequency at which it shows up comes from a simple calculation: \\[ f_{\\text{alias}} = \\big|\\,f - k\\,f_s\\,\\big|, \\qquad k \\text{ the integer that leaves the result between } 0 \\text{ and } f_s/2 \\] The frequency is “folded” into the band, as if the axis were bent at \\( f_s/2 \\) and at every multiple of \\( f_s \\). The problem is not that the signal gets distorted: it is that there is no way to tell afterward. Once sampled, the alias signal is indistinguishable from a real signal at that low frequency. There is no subsequent processing that can separate it, because the samples are exactly the same. Lab · the impostor sinusoid A sinusoid sampled at a fixed \\( f_s \\). Raise its frequency beyond \\( f_s/2 \\) and watch how the same samples are explained by a different, much slower sinusoid. Show the alias sinusoid Show the folding map Signal frequency = 3.0 Hz · fs = 10 Hz"},{"t":"Where you see it without looking for it","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#donde","x":"02 Where you see it without looking for it Situation What is observed Why The wheels of a wagon in the movies They turn backward or stand still 24 frames per second sample the rotation of the spokes A striped shirt on television Bands of color that do not exist appear The spatial pattern exceeds the resolution of the sensor An oscilloscope with a slow timebase A fast sinusoid looks slow and with a pulsing amplitude The sampling rate dropped below twice the frequency An image shrunk without filtering Textures with strange patterns, the moiré effect Decimating without a prior anti-aliasing filter Strobe light on a motor The shaft appears stopped It is aliasing used on purpose The last row is worth remembering: aliasing is not only a defect. A stroboscope and a sequential-sampling oscilloscope use it on purpose to observe signals much faster than their electronics, sampling at a frequency barely different from that of the signal so that the alias falls in a band that is comfortable to measure."},{"t":"The anti-aliasing filter is not optional","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#filtro","x":"03 The anti-aliasing filter is not optional The sampling theorem requires the signal to be bandlimited, and no real signal is: there is always noise and high-frequency components. That is why an analog low-pass goes before the converter, and it has to be analog: once sampled, the damage is already done. \\[ \\text{required attenuation at } f_s - f_{\\text{useful}} \\;\\ge\\; \\text{dynamic range of the converter} \\] A 16-bit converter —96 dB— is of little use if at the frequency that will fold onto the useful band the filter attenuates only 30 dB. This is where the usual tension appears: the closer the useful band is to \\( f_s/2 \\), the sharper the filter has to be, and a sharp filter is high-order, expensive and has nasty group delay. Oversampling, which solves the problem from the other side The elegant way out is to sample much faster than necessary. If the useful band is 20 kHz and you sample at 5 MHz, what folds onto the band is above 4.9 MHz, and a first-order analog filter already attenuates it more than enough. Then you decimate digitally, where the filter can be sharp and linear-phase at no analog cost. That is the idea behind sigma-delta converters, which sample at tens of megahertz with a single bit and do all the fine work in the digital domain. That is why a modern audio codec needs barely an RC at the input where the converters of the eighties had eighth-order filters."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Folding by hand With \\( f_s = 8 \\) kHz, calculate the frequency at which signals of 3, 5, 9, 11 and 16 kHz appear. Verify that 3 and 5 kHz land in the same place, and explain why they cannot be distinguished. Activity 2 · Causing it Using a microcontroller, sample a 1.1 kHz sinusoid at 1 kS/s and plot the samples. Check that a 100 Hz sinusoid is seen, and that raising it to 1.2 kHz gives a 200 Hz one. Activity 3 · Measuring the required attenuation For a 12-bit converter with a 10 kHz useful band and \\( f_s = 48 \\) kHz, determine at what frequency the folding starts and how much attenuation is needed there. Choose the order of the anti-aliasing filter."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing you can filter afterward. Once sampled, the alias is indistinguishable from a legitimate signal. Putting the anti-aliasing filter in the digital domain. It has to be analog, before the converter. Forgetting wideband noise. Even if the useful signal is slow, the noise folds anyway. Trusting that the converter “filters something”. A sample-and-hold is not an anti-aliasing filter. Decimating without filtering. Lowering the sampling frequency also generates aliasing, and there the filter can indeed be digital."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment With \\( f_s = 8 \\) kHz, how does a 9 kHz signal appear? As 1 kHz: \\( |9 - 8| = 1 \\). And an 11 kHz one? As 3 kHz: \\( |11 - 8| = 3 \\). It is confused with a real 3 kHz signal. Why does the anti-aliasing filter have to be analog? Because the folding happens at the moment of sampling. Afterward there is nothing left to separate. Why do the wagon wheels turn backward in the movies? Because 24 frames per second sample the rotation of the spokes, and the alias can come out negative: it appears to rotate backward. What advantage does oversampling give? It moves the folding band away, so the analog filter can be much simpler and the fine work is done digitally. Is aliasing good for anything? Yes: the stroboscope and the sequential-sampling oscilloscope use it on purpose to observe signals faster than their own electronics."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Aliasing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1997. Aliasing derived from the overlap of replicas, with graphical examples. Walt Kester (ed.). Data Conversion Handbook. Analog Devices / Newnes, 2005. Real anti-aliasing filter design, oversampling and sigma-delta architectures. Richard G. Lyons. Understanding Digital Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. Folding explained with figures you understand at first glance, and bandpass sampling."},{"t":"The differential pair","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#par","x":"01 The differential pair Two identical transistors with their emitters joined and fed by a current source \\( I_{EE} \\) form the most important circuit in analog electronics. The source fixes the sum of the two currents; all the input can do is split it between the two sides according to the voltage difference between the bases, \\( v_{id} = v_{B1} - v_{B2} \\). With the exponential relationship of each transistor, the split is exact: \\[ i_{C1} = \\frac{I_{EE}}{1 + e^{-v_{id}/V_T}} \\qquad i_{C2} = \\frac{I_{EE}}{1 + e^{v_{id}/V_T}} \\] \\[ i_{C1} - i_{C2} = I_{EE} \\tanh \\frac{v_{id}}{2 V_T} \\] If both bases rise together, the split does not change: the pair ignores the common voltage and responds only to the difference. That property makes it ideal as the input of an operational amplifier, a comparator or an instrument: it amplifies the signal of interest and rejects the noise or the DC that arrives equally at both inputs. It uses the current sources and active loads from the previous unit."},{"t":"Small signal: differential and common-mode gain","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#debil","x":"02 Small signal: differential and common-mode gain For small \\( v_{id} \\), the hyperbolic tangent is a straight line and each transistor has \\( g_m = I_{EE}/(2 V_T) \\). With collector resistors \\( R_C \\): Quantity Differential output Output from a single collector Differential gain \\( A_d \\) \\( g_m R_C \\) \\( g_m R_C / 2 \\) Common-mode gain \\( A_{cm} \\) Zero if everything is symmetric \\( \\approx -R_C / (2 R_{EE}) \\) Differential input resistance \\( 2\\, r_\\pi \\) \\( R_{EE} \\) is the output resistance of the tail current source. The ratio between differential gain and common-mode gain is the common-mode rejection ratio: \\[ CMRR = \\left| \\frac{A_d}{A_{cm}} \\right| \\approx g_m R_{EE} \\quad \\text{(single-ended output)} \\] With a tail resistance of 10 kΩ and 1 mA, barely 193 (46 dB). With a 1 MΩ current source, 19,300 (86 dB): that is why the tail is always a current source. With a mirror as the active load, the output from a single collector recovers the full differential gain: \\( A_d = g_m (r_{o2} \\parallel r_{o4}) \\), the input structure of almost all operational amplifiers."},{"t":"Large signal: the pair saturates gracefully","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#fuerte","x":"03 Large signal: the pair saturates gracefully The hyperbolic tangent flattens out: with \\( v_{id} = 2 V_T \\approx 52 \\) mV the pair already delivers 76 % of its maximum current, and with about 100 mV practically all the current flows through one side only. Unlike the common emitter, the pair neither clips abruptly nor saturates: it limits smoothly, always between \\( \\pm I_{EE} \\). Being an odd function, the hyperbolic tangent generates no even harmonics: the second harmonic of the common emitter disappears and the third remains: \\[ HD_3 \\approx \\frac{1}{48} \\left( \\frac{\\hat V}{V_T} \\right)^2 \\ HD_3 \\approx \\frac{1}{48} \\left( \\frac{\\hat V}{V_T} \\right)^2 \\frac{1}{(1 + g_m R_E)^3} \\quad \\text{with degeneration} \\] With a 10 mV peak, 0.31 %; with 50 mV, almost 8 %. An emitter resistor with \\( g_m R_E \\approx 2 \\) divides it by about 25. Lab · the transfer characteristic of the differential pair The difference of collector currents as a function of the differential input voltage, calculated with the exponential of each transistor and an optional emitter resistor. The band marks the swing of the chosen signal; its gain and its distortion are calculated. Tail current \\( I_{EE} \\) = Emitter resistance, per side \\( R_E \\) = Differential amplitude (peak) = Tail current source resistance \\( R_{EE} \\) ="},{"t":"Mismatch and offset voltage","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#offset","x":"04 Mismatch and offset voltage If the two transistors are not identical, with zero input the currents do not split in half and the output is not zero. The differential voltage that must be applied to balance them is the offset voltage. A 1 % difference in the saturation current of the transistors produces \\( V_T \\ln 1.01 \\approx 0.26 \\) mV; a 1 % difference in the load resistors, the same again. That is why pairs are built with the transistors close together, in symmetric layouts, as you will see in the unit on integrated circuit design."},{"t":"The MOS differential pair","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#mos","x":"05 The MOS differential pair With MOS transistors the idea is the same, but the transfer characteristic follows the square law instead of the exponential. The linear range is no longer set by \\( V_T \\) but by the overdrive voltage: the pair stops splitting when \\( |v_{id}| \\) reaches \\( \\sqrt{2}\\, V_{ov} \\), a few hundred millivolts. It is more linear and has zero input current, at the cost of less gain and more offset."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Plotting the transfer characteristic With a pair of matched transistors, a 1 mA tail source and 4.7 kΩ resistors, sweep the differential voltage from −150 to +150 mV and measure the difference of collector voltages. Fit it with a hyperbolic tangent. Activity 2 · Common-mode rejection Apply the same signal to both bases and measure the output with a 10 kΩ tail resistor and then with a current source. Calculate the rejection in each case. Activity 3 · Measuring the offset With the inputs grounded, measure the differential output voltage and refer it to the input. Compare loose transistors with a matched pair."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Using a resistor as the tail. The common-mode rejection ends up poor. Exceeding the linear range. Without degeneration, beyond about 20 mV the distortion grows quickly. Building the pair from transistors of different batches. The offset grows. Confusing differential output with single-ended output. The gain is half with a single collector and resistors."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the \\( g_m \\) of each transistor with \\( I_{EE} = 2 \\) mA? \\( 1 \\text{ mA} / 25.85 \\text{ mV} \\approx 38.7 \\) mS. Why does the differential pair not produce a second harmonic? Because its transfer characteristic, a hyperbolic tangent, is an odd function. What CMRR is obtained with \\( g_m = 20 \\) mS and a 500 kΩ tail? \\( 20 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 5 \\times 10^5 = 10\\,000 \\), 80 dB. What fraction of \\( I_{EE} \\) is steered with \\( v_{id} = 2 V_T \\)? \\( \\tanh 1 \\approx 0.76 \\): the current difference is 76 % of \\( I_{EE} \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The differential amplifier","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. The bipolar and MOS differential pair, with the large-signal transfer characteristic and the offset. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. The differential amplifier with an active load, step by step. Barrie Gilbert. A Precise Four-Quadrant Multiplier with Subnanosecond Response. IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits, 1968. The differential pair taken to the extreme: the multiplier that uses its hyperbolic tangent on purpose."},{"t":"Why more than one stage","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#por-que","x":"01 Why more than one stage A single stage rarely meets everything that is asked of an amplifier: high gain, high input resistance so as not to load the source, and low output resistance to drive the load. Each configuration is good at one thing, so they are combined in cascade, each doing its own job: Stage Function Typical configuration Input Not loading the source, rejecting the common mode, low noise Differential pair, follower, FET Gain Providing almost all the voltage amplification Common emitter or common source with an active load Output Delivering current to a low-resistance load Emitter follower, complementary pair This is exactly the structure of an integrated operational amplifier: a differential pair, a gain stage and an output stage."},{"t":"The gain of a cascade","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#ganancia","x":"02 The gain of a cascade The total gain is the product of the gains of each stage, but measured with the real load that the next one places on it. Between two stages a divider is formed between the output resistance of one and the input resistance of the other: \\[ A_v = A_1 \\frac{R_{in,2}}{R_{out,1} + R_{in,2}}\\; A_2 \\frac{R_{in,3}}{R_{out,2} + R_{in,3}}\\; A_3 \\cdots \\] In decibels, the gains add and the loading losses subtract. The typical mistake is to calculate each stage unloaded and multiply: a stage with a gain of 100 and a 10 kΩ output, loaded by a 10 kΩ input, delivers only 50. A follower inserted between the two solves the problem, at the cost of one more stage."},{"t":"How the stages are connected","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#acoplamiento","x":"03 How the stages are connected Coupling Advantage Problem Capacitive Each stage is biased separately DC does not pass; each capacitor adds a low-frequency cutoff Direct Amplifies down to DC; no large capacitors: it is the only option in an integrated circuit The DC levels of one stage set those of the next, and drift is amplified Transformer Matches impedances and isolates Heavy, expensive and band-limited: today only in radio frequency and isolation"},{"t":"Classic combinations","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#combinaciones","x":"04 Classic combinations Darlington: two transistors where the emitter of the first feeds the base of the second. They behave like a single one with a β of around \\( \\beta_1 \\beta_2 \\), about 10,000, and twice the base-emitter voltage. It is used in output stages and in power switches. Cascode: a common emitter followed by a common base. The common base keeps the collector voltage of the first fixed, so the first has almost no voltage gain and therefore barely suffers from the Miller effect: the collector-base capacitance, which in a common emitter with a gain of 100 is seen multiplied by 101 from the input, is multiplied by barely 2 in the cascode. Result: the same \\( g_m R_C \\) with much more bandwidth. Common emitter followed by common collector: the gain of the first and the low output resistance of the second."},{"t":"The bandwidth shrinks","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#ancho-banda","x":"05 The bandwidth shrinks Each stage has its own high-frequency cutoff. When they are cascaded, the cutoffs multiply and the overall response falls off sooner than that of each one. For \\( n \\) identical stages with a pole at \\( f_H \\): \\[ f_{H,\\text{total}} = f_H \\sqrt{2^{1/n} - 1} \\qquad f_{L,\\text{total}} = \\frac{f_L}{\\sqrt{2^{1/n} - 1}} \\] With two stages, the bandwidth drops to 64 %; with three, to 51 %; with four, to 43.5 %. The low-frequency cutoff rises in the same proportion. That is why it is not a good idea to build an enormous gain with many identical stages: each added stage gives gain but eats up bandwidth. The design balances the number of stages, the gain of each one and its own bandwidth. Lab · cascaded stages \\( n \\) identical stages, each with an open-loop gain, a high-frequency pole and a loading loss at each junction (given by the ratio between the input of the next stage and the output of the previous one). The graph compares the response of a single stage with that of the cascade. Number of stages \\( n \\) = Unloaded gain of each stage = Pole of each stage \\( f_H \\) = Ratio \\( R_{in,\\text{next}} / R_{out} \\) at each junction ="},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Two stages and a follower Build two common emitters coupled by a capacitor and measure the total gain. Insert an emitter follower between them and compare with the product of the unloaded gains. Activity 2 · Measuring the Miller effect Measure the high-frequency cutoff of a common emitter with a 10 kΩ source resistance, and then that of a cascode with the same input transistor and the same load. Activity 3 · Bandwidth shrinkage With two identical stages, measure the bandwidth of each one and that of the cascade, and compare it with the 64 % predicted by the formula."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Multiplying unloaded gains. The loading of each stage on the previous one must be included. Assuming the total bandwidth is that of one stage. With several stages it is smaller. Direct coupling without checking the DC levels. One stage can end up saturated by the voltage of the previous one. Forgetting the Miller effect. A high-gain stage multiplies its collector-base capacitance as seen from the input."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment A stage with a gain of 50 and a 5 kΩ output drives a 20 kΩ input. What does it deliver? \\( 50 \\times 20/25 = 40 \\). What bandwidth do three identical stages of 1 MHz each have? \\( 1 \\text{ MHz} \\times \\sqrt{2^{1/3} - 1} \\approx 510 \\) kHz. Why does the cascode have more bandwidth than the common emitter? Because the input transistor has almost no voltage gain and its collector-base capacitance is not multiplied by the Miller effect. What is the approximate β of a Darlington made of two transistors with β = 80? About 6400."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Multistage amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. Multistage amplifiers and frequency response, with the Miller effect and the cascode. Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. Output stages and the transistor combinations of an integrated amplifier. Jacob Millman and Arvin Grabel. Microelectronics. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1987. The classic text of the course, very complete on coupled multistage amplifiers and their frequency response."},{"t":"The op amp as a feedback amplifier","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#ideal","x":"01 The op amp as a feedback amplifier An operational amplifier is a differential amplifier with enormous gain, from \\( 10^5 \\) to \\( 10^7 \\), with a high-impedance input and a low-impedance output. It only makes sense inside a feedback loop: the loop gain is so large that the gain of the circuit is set by the external resistors. With negative feedback and the output not saturated, the voltage between the inputs is the output divided by the gain, practically zero: this is the virtual short circuit. \\[ A_v^{\\mathrm{inv}} = -\\frac{R_2}{R_1} \\] \\[ A_v^{\\mathrm{non\\,inv}} = 1 + \\frac{R_2}{R_1} \\] With finite gain \\( A \\), the actual gain is the ideal one divided by \\( 1 + 1/(A\\beta) \\), with \\( \\beta = R_1/(R_1+R_2) \\): a gain-of-100 amplifier with an op amp of \\( A = 10^5 \\) has a 0.1 % error. An introduction to the component, with the basic circuits, is in the interactive course on operational amplifiers on this site."},{"t":"Linear applications","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#lineales","x":"02 Linear applications Circuit Transfer function Remarks Inverting summer \\( -R_f \\sum v_i/R_i \\) The inputs do not interact: the junction is a virtual ground Integrator \\( -\\frac{1}{RC}\\int v_i\\,dt \\) Needs a resistor in parallel with C, or it saturates with the offset Differentiator \\( -RC\\, dv_i/dt \\) Amplifies noise; limited with a series resistor Current-to-voltage converter \\( -R\\, i_i \\) For photodiodes: the virtual ground keeps the diode at 0 V Instrumentation amplifier \\( (1 + 2R/R_G)(v_+ - v_-) \\) Three op amps; the gain is set with a single resistor The instrumentation amplifier is the most important linear application for sensors: very high input impedance, differential gain adjustable with \\( R_G \\) and a common-mode rejection that does not depend on the matching of the resistors of the first stage. Active filters, another central linear application, were covered in analog filters."},{"t":"What the ideal model does not tell","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#noideal","x":"03 What the ideal model does not tell At DC, the real op amp has an input offset voltage \\( V_{OS} \\) between its inputs, from microvolts to millivolts, and bias currents \\( I_B \\) that flow through the external resistors. Both appear at the output multiplied by the noise gain \\( 1/\\beta \\): \\[ V_{o,\\mathrm{error}} = \\left(1 + \\frac{R_2}{R_1}\\right) V_{OS} + R_2\\, I_{OS} \\] The formula assumes the compensating resistor \\( R_1 \\parallel R_2 \\) at the noninverting input, which cancels the common part of the bias currents and leaves only their difference \\( I_{OS} \\). At AC, the gain falls with frequency, the output cannot change faster than the slew rate and it cannot reach the supply voltages except in rail-to-rail op amps. These limits are studied in the units on frequency response and stability."},{"t":"Nonlinear applications","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#nolineales","x":"04 Nonlinear applications Without negative feedback, the op amp is a comparator: its output saturates to one side or the other depending on which input is larger. With a noisy input that crosses the threshold slowly, the noise makes it cross several times and the output bounces. The solution is positive feedback: the Schmitt trigger has two thresholds, and once the output changes, the threshold moves away from the input: \\[ V_{T\\pm} = \\pm \\frac{R_1}{R_2}\\, V_{sat} \\] For the noninverting configuration, with \\( R_1 \\) from the input and \\( R_2 \\) from the output to the noninverting input. The difference between thresholds, the hysteresis, has to exceed the peak amplitude of the noise. Lab · comparator and Schmitt trigger Two periods of a 1 V sine wave with Gaussian noise, applied to a simple comparator (red) and to a Schmitt trigger with thresholds at ±V h (blue). The ideal output changes three times. The noise is always the same pseudorandom sequence, scaled. Noise, RMS value = Hysteresis threshold V h = Other nonlinear applications combine the op amp with diodes. The precision rectifier puts the diode inside the loop, which divides its threshold voltage by the gain: it rectifies signals of millivolts. The peak detector adds a capacitor that holds the maximum, and the logarithmic amplifier uses the exponential of a transistor in the loop to compress a dynamic range of several decades."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Offset and bias Measure the output of a gain-of-1000 inverting amplifier with the input grounded, with and without the compensating resistor, and deduce \\( V_{OS} \\) and \\( I_{OS} \\). Compare with the datasheet. Activity 2 · Instrumentation amplifier Build an instrumentation amplifier with three op amps for a strain-gauge bridge and measure its common-mode rejection by applying the same signal to both inputs. Activity 3 · Schmitt Add noise from a generator to a slow sine wave and observe the output of a comparator and of a Schmitt trigger. Adjust the hysteresis until the bouncing disappears."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Applying the virtual short circuit to a comparator. It only holds with negative feedback and the output not saturated. Using an op amp as a fast comparator. It recovers from saturation slowly; comparators are designed for that. Integrating without a parallel resistor. The offset gets integrated and drives the output into saturation within seconds. Giving too much hysteresis. It eliminates the bouncing, but delays the switching and can keep small signals from triggering it."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What gain error does a gain-of-1000 noninverting amplifier have with an op amp of A = 10⁵? \\( A\\beta = 100 \\): the gain comes out 1 % low. A gain-of-100 inverting amplifier uses an op amp with V OS = 2 mV. What output voltage does it have with the input grounded? \\( 101 \\cdot 2 \\) mV ≈ 0.2 V. An instrumentation amplifier with R = 25 kΩ needs a gain of 101. What is the value of R G ? \\( 1 + 50\\,\\mathrm{k}/R_G = 101 \\): R G = 500 Ω. A Schmitt trigger with a ±12 V output needs thresholds of ±0.6 V. What R 2 /R 1 ratio does it take? 20."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Sergio Franco. Design with Operational Amplifiers and Analog Integrated Circuits. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2015. The practical reference: linear and nonlinear applications with their errors. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. Op amps from the workbench, with hundreds of annotated circuits. Robert F. Coughlin and Frederick F. Driscoll. Operational Amplifiers and Linear Integrated Circuits. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Amplificadores operacionales y circuitos integrados lineales”), Prentice Hall, 1999. A text in Spanish with many worked applications."},{"t":"What changes with power","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#clases","x":"01 What changes with power In a signal amplifier, gain and noise matter. In a power amplifier, which drives a loudspeaker or a motor, efficiency comes to the front, because whatever does not reach the load is dissipated as heat in the transistors, along with large-signal distortion. The classes of operation are distinguished by the fraction of the cycle during which each transistor conducts: Class Conduction Maximum efficiency Characteristic A The whole cycle 25 % with a resistive load, 50 % with a transformer Minimum distortion, dissipates even with no signal B Half a cycle each transistor 78.5 % No quiescent consumption, crossover distortion AB Somewhat more than half a cycle Almost like B The quiescent current eliminates the crossover C Less than half a cycle Close to 100 % in the limit Only with a tuned load, at radio frequency D Switching Over 90 % in practice Pulse-width modulation and an output filter The introduction to the classes with the circuits is in power amplifiers, in the technician program. Here we calculate efficiency, dissipation and distortion."},{"t":"Class B: efficiency and dissipation","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#claseb","x":"02 Class B: efficiency and dissipation In the complementary pair of followers, each transistor conducts one half-cycle. With ±\\( V_{CC} \\) supplies, a load \\( R_L \\) and a sinusoidal output of amplitude \\( V_p \\), the supply delivers an average current of \\( V_p/(\\pi R_L) \\) per branch: \\[ P_L = \\frac{V_p^2}{2 R_L} \\] \\[ P_{CC} = \\frac{2 V_{CC} V_p}{\\pi R_L} \\] \\[ \\eta = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\frac{V_p}{V_{CC}} \\] The efficiency reaches 78.5 % only if the output reaches the supply. With half the amplitude, it is 39 %. The dissipation in the transistors is the difference between what the supply delivers and what the load receives, and it is not maximum at full power but at an intermediate amplitude. Differentiating: \\[ P_{D,\\max} = \\frac{V_{CC}^2}{\\pi^2 R_L} \\ \\text{per transistor, at} \\ V_p = \\frac{2 V_{CC}}{\\pi} \\] With ±15 V across 8 Ω, each transistor dissipates up to 2.85 W when the output is 9.5 V peak, even though the maximum power in the load is 14 W."},{"t":"Crossover distortion and class AB","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#cruce","x":"03 Crossover distortion and class AB A bipolar follower needs about 0.6 V between base and emitter to conduct. In class B, while the input is between −0.6 V and +0.6 V neither transistor conducts and the output stays at zero: this is crossover distortion, which affects small signals the most, precisely the ones the ear perceives in the most detail. Class AB separates the bases with a bias voltage, two diodes or a \\( V_{BE} \\) multiplier, so that a quiescent current \\( I_Q \\) flows through the transistors and one of them is always conducting. Lab · the complementary pair in class B and AB Two complementary followers with the exponential model \\( i_C = I_S e^{v_{BE}/V_T} \\) (\\( I_S = 10^{-12} \\) A), ±15 V supplies and an 8 Ω loudspeaker. Above, the input (gray) and the output (blue); below, the currents of the two transistors. The output is solved exactly at each instant. Bias voltage between bases = Input amplitude = The quiescent current brings a risk: the base–emitter voltage of a bipolar transistor falls about 2 mV per degree. If the transistors heat up and the bias is fixed, the current rises, they heat up more and are destroyed: this is thermal runaway. It is avoided with emitter resistors of a few tenths of an ohm and by mounting the \\( V_{BE} \\) multiplier on the same heat sink, so that its voltage falls together with that of the output transistors."},{"t":"Thermal design","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#termico","x":"04 Thermal design Heat flows from the junction to the ambient through thermal resistances in series, from junction to case, from case to heat sink and from heat sink to air. With the dissipated power \\( P_D \\): \\[ T_j = T_a + P_D\\,(\\theta_{jc} + \\theta_{cs} + \\theta_{sa}) \\] To dissipate 2.85 W with the air at 40 °C, a design junction temperature of 125 °C, \\( \\theta_{jc} \\) = 1.5 °C/W and 0.5 °C/W for a mica washer with grease, the heat sink must have at most 27.8 °C/W per transistor. Besides the thermal limit, datasheets give the safe operating area: at high voltage and moderate current, a bipolar transistor can fail through second breakdown even though the power is within the limit. Reactive loads, such as a loudspeaker, put voltage and current out of phase and take the operating point into that region."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Crossover Build an unbiased complementary pair, observe the crossover distortion with a small signal and measure the distortion with an analyzer. Add two diodes between the bases and repeat. Activity 2 · Efficiency Measure the average current of the supplies and the power in the load for several amplitudes, and compare the efficiency with \\( \\pi V_p/(4 V_{CC}) \\). Activity 3 · Thermal runaway With a class AB amplifier without emitter resistors, measure the quiescent current while the heat sink is heated with a hair dryer. Repeat with 0.22 Ω resistors."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Sizing the heat sink for the maximum output power. The maximum dissipation occurs at about two thirds of the maximum amplitude. Measuring distortion only at full power. Crossover distortion is worse with small signals. Biasing class AB with a fixed voltage. Without thermal compensation, the quiescent current runs away. Forgetting the safe operating area with reactive loads. A loudspeaker is not a resistor."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What efficiency does a class B amplifier have with ±20 V and 12 V peak at the output? \\( 0.785 \\cdot 12/20 \\approx 47 \\) %. How much does each transistor of a class B amplifier with ±24 V across 4 Ω dissipate at most? \\( 24^2/(\\pi^2 \\cdot 4) \\approx 14.6 \\) W. Why is crossover distortion worse with small signals? Because the ±0.6 V dead zone is a larger fraction of the signal. A transistor dissipates 10 W with θ jc = 1 °C/W and θ cs = 0.5 °C/W. With air at 30 °C and a junction at 130 °C, what heat sink is needed? \\( 100/10 - 1.5 = 8.5 \\) °C/W."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Douglas Self. Audio Power Amplifier Design. 6th ed., Focal Press, 2013. The reference book on crossover distortion, biasing and thermal stability. Bob Cordell. Designing Audio Power Amplifiers. 2nd ed., Routledge, 2019. The complete design, from the input pair to the safe operating area. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 8th ed., Oxford University Press, 2020. Class A, B and AB output stages with their efficiency and dissipation analysis."},{"t":"Power, efficiency and linearity","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#objetivo","x":"01 Power, efficiency and linearity The power amplifier is the stage that consumes the most in a transmitter, and what it does not deliver to the antenna it turns into heat. Three figures describe it: the output power, the drain efficiency (or collector efficiency), \\( \\eta = P_{out}/P_{DC} \\), and the linearity, which decides whether it can amplify a variable-envelope signal without distorting it. The same ideas as in low-frequency amplifiers apply here, with one advantage: the load is a resonant circuit, which lets only the fundamental through even if the transistor current is highly distorted."},{"t":"Classes A, AB, B and C: the conduction angle","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#angulo","x":"02 Classes A, AB, B and C: the conduction angle In the linear classes the transistor works as a current source, and the classes are distinguished by the conduction angle α, the part of the cycle in which it conducts: 360° in class A, 180° in class B, in between in AB and less than 180° in C. The current is a segment of a sinusoid, and the resonant circuit takes its fundamental. With the same peak current and the output voltage swung up to the supply: \\[ \\eta = \\frac{\\alpha - \\sin\\alpha}{2\\big(2\\sin(\\alpha/2) - \\alpha\\cos(\\alpha/2)\\big)} \\] It gives 50 % in class A, 78.5 % in class B and tends to 100 % in class C with very small angles. But the fundamental current, and with it the power, falls with the angle: with 90° the efficiency is 94 % and the power 2 dB lower than in class A, and with smaller angles more and more drive is needed. Lab · the conduction angle An amplifier with a resonant load and a fixed peak current. Above, the transistor current over one cycle and its fundamental; below, the efficiency (blue) and the power relative to class A (green) as a function of the angle. Conduction angle α ="},{"t":"Switching classes: D, E and F","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#conmutados","x":"03 Switching classes: D, E and F If the transistor works as a switch, in theory it does not dissipate: when it conducts there is no voltage, and when there is voltage it does not conduct. Class D uses two transistors that switch a square wave into a filter. Class E, by Sokal, uses a single transistor with a load network that makes the voltage reach zero just when the switch closes, eliminating the switching loss with the output capacitance. Class F controls the harmonics of the voltage to square it up. Practical efficiencies exceed 80 %, but these classes are not amplitude-linear: they serve for FM, FSK and other constant-envelope modulations, or with techniques that separate the envelope, such as envelope tracking and the Doherty amplifier."},{"t":"Linearity and back-off","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#linealidad","x":"04 Linearity and back-off Cellular and Wi-Fi modulations have a variable envelope and a peak-to-average power ratio of several decibels, as seen in complex envelope. So as not to clip the peaks, the amplifier operates with the average power several dB below compression, and in class AB the efficiency falls with amplitude. The transmitters unit quantifies that cost. The distortion that remains is corrected with digital predistortion: the processor applies to the signal the nonlinearity inverse to that of the amplifier."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Class C Build a class C amplifier of a few watts in the HF band with a MOSFET, measure the supply current and the power in a 50 Ω load, and compute the efficiency for several bias points. Activity 2 · Harmonics Measure the harmonics at the output with and without the low-pass filter, and compare them with the spurious emission limits. Activity 3 · Compression Sweep the input power of a class AB amplifier and measure the compression point and the efficiency as a function of output power."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using class C with variable-envelope modulations. It destroys the amplitude information. Forgetting the harmonic filter. A class C or D amplifier emits strong harmonics that invade other bands. Measuring efficiency only at full power. In real operation the amplifier works backed off and is much less efficient. Mismatching the load without protection. With the antenna disconnected, the reflected wave can destroy the transistor."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What theoretical efficiency does an amplifier with a 180° conduction angle have? 78.5 %: it is class B. How much does a 100 W amplifier with 60 % efficiency dissipate? \\( 100/0.6 - 100 \\approx 67 \\) W. Why can class E have high efficiency even at high frequencies? Because the load network brings the voltage to zero before closing the switch, and the energy of the output capacitance is not lost. What modulations allow nonlinear amplifiers? Constant-envelope ones, such as FM and FSK."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"RF power amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Steve C. Cripps. RF Power Amplifiers for Wireless Communications. 2nd ed., Artech House, 2006. The analysis of conduction angle and the high-efficiency classes. Herbert L. Krauss, Charles W. Bostian and Frederick H. Raab. Solid State Radio Engineering. Wiley, 1980. A classic on solid-state power amplifiers. Nathan O. Sokal and Alan D. Sokal. Class E—A New Class of High-Efficiency Tuned Single-Ended Switching Power Amplifiers. IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits, 10(3), 1975. The original paper on class E."},{"t":"The loop gain","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#lazo","x":"01 The loop gain A feedback amplifier takes a sample of its output, multiplies it by a factor \\( \\beta \\) and subtracts it from the input. If the amplifier without feedback has gain \\( A \\), the closed-loop gain is: \\[ A_f = \\frac{A}{1 + A\\beta} = \\frac{A}{1 + T} \\] \\( T = A\\beta \\) is the loop gain: how much a signal is amplified as it travels around the complete loop. If \\( T \\gg 1 \\), \\( A_f \\approx 1/\\beta \\) and the gain is set by the feedback network, which is built with precise, stable resistors. Feedback trades gain for other qualities. The most direct trade is desensitivity: a relative variation of \\( A \\) reaches the feedback gain divided by \\( 1 + T \\): \\[ \\frac{dA_f}{A_f} = \\frac{1}{1 + T}\\, \\frac{dA}{A} \\] With A = 10⁵ and β = 0.01 (T = 1000), a 50 % change in the amplifier gain, due to temperature or spread, moves the feedback gain by barely 0.1 %. The multistage amplifiers of Applied Electronics I have large but imprecise gains; feedback turns them into small, exact gains."},{"t":"The four topologies","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#topologias","x":"02 The four topologies The sample of the output can be a voltage (shunt connection across the output) or a current (series connection), and the feedback signal can be subtracted as a voltage (in series with the input) or as a current (in parallel). Each combination stabilizes a different transfer function and modifies the impedances by the factor \\( 1 + T \\): Topology (input–output) Stabilizes Input Output Series–shunt Voltage gain \\( R_i(1+T) \\) \\( R_o/(1+T) \\) Shunt–series Current gain \\( R_i/(1+T) \\) \\( R_o(1+T) \\) Series–series Transconductance \\( R_i(1+T) \\) \\( R_o(1+T) \\) Shunt–shunt Transresistance \\( R_i/(1+T) \\) \\( R_o/(1+T) \\) The rule is simple: a series connection multiplies the impedance by \\( 1 + T \\) and a shunt connection divides it. The noninverting amplifier with an op amp is series–shunt: very high input impedance and almost zero output impedance. The common-emitter stage with an unbypassed emitter resistor is series–series, and with a collector-to-base resistor, shunt–shunt. In real circuits the feedback network loads the amplifier. The rigorous analysis first separates the amplifier loaded by the network, calculates \\( A \\) and \\( \\beta \\) of that version, and only then applies the formulas. Another, more direct way is to calculate \\( T \\) by breaking the loop at a point and to apply Blackman's theorem for the impedances."},{"t":"Distortion, noise and bandwidth","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#distorsion","x":"03 Distortion, noise and bandwidth Feedback continuously compares the output with the input: if the amplifier distorts, the error appears at the amplifier input and is corrected. The distortion components generated inside the loop end up divided, approximately, by \\( 1 + T \\), when compared at equal output amplitude. The same happens with bandwidth: with a dominant pole at \\( f_H \\), \\[ f_{Hf} = f_H\\,(1 + T_0) \\] \\[ A_f\\, f_{Hf} = A_0\\, f_H \\] The gain–bandwidth product is conserved: the gain that is given up is converted into bandwidth. This is the basis of the frequency analysis in the following units. Lab · feedback reduces distortion An amplifier of gain \\( A \\) with an asymmetric hyperbolic-tangent transfer function, which saturates at +7 V and −13 V. With and without feedback, the input is adjusted to obtain the same output amplitude, and the waveforms (above) and the harmonics (below, in dB relative to the fundamental) are compared. The feedback output is calculated by solving the loop at each instant. A = 100 A = 1000 A = 10,000 Feedback factor β = Output amplitude = There is something that feedback does not improve: the noise that enters along with the signal, or that the first stage generates. That noise is amplified just like the signal. The signal-to-noise ratio is decided at the input stage, before the loop."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Desensitivity Build a two-stage transistor amplifier, and measure its gain without feedback and with a series-voltage feedback network. Swap the transistor for another with a different β and compare the variations. Activity 2 · Impedances Measure the input and output impedances with and without feedback and verify the factor \\( 1 + T \\). Activity 3 · Distortion With a spectrum analyzer or the FFT of an oscilloscope, measure the harmonic distortion with and without feedback, adjusting the input for the same output."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Comparing distortion at equal input. With feedback the output is much smaller; the valid comparison is at equal output. Forgetting the loading of the feedback network. Without including it, \\( A \\) and \\( \\beta \\) come out wrong and so do the impedances. Expecting feedback to lower the input noise. It only reduces what is generated inside the loop, after the input. Confusing negative feedback with positive. If the sign is reversed, \\( 1 + T \\) can vanish and the circuit oscillates."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment An amplifier with gain 2000 is fed back with β = 0.05. What gain does it have? \\( 2000/(1 + 100) \\approx 19.8 \\). In the same circuit, if the amplifier gain drops to 1000, how much does the feedback gain change? It becomes \\( 1000/51 = 19.6 \\): a 1 % change for a 50 % one. An amplifier with a 1 kΩ output resistance is fed back in series–shunt with T = 99. What output resistance does it have? 10 Ω. An amplifier with gain 10⁴ has a pole at 100 Hz. What bandwidth does it have with gain 100? About 10 kHz: the gain–bandwidth product, 1 MHz, is conserved."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Feedback amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 8th ed., Oxford University Press, 2020. The four topologies with the method of separating the amplifier loaded by the network. Paul R. Gray, Paul J. Hurst, Stephen H. Lewis and Robert G. Meyer. Analysis and Design of Analog Integrated Circuits. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. Feedback with Blackman's theorem and loop-gain analysis. Jacob Millman and Arvin Grabel. Microelectronics. 6th ed. (Spanish edition, “Microelectrónica”), Hispano Europea, 1993. A classic treatment of feedback amplifiers, available in Spanish."},{"t":"Describing an RF amplifier: the S parameters","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#sparam","x":"01 Describing an RF amplifier: the S parameters At radio frequency, voltages and currents cannot be measured with open or short circuits: transistors oscillate and cables have lengths comparable to the wavelength. That is why devices are described with scattering parameters, which relate the incident and reflected waves at each port with all ports terminated in 50 Ω. They are the RF version of the two-port networks of Circuit Theory II: Parameter Meaning S 11 Input reflection coefficient S 21 Forward gain, from input to output S 12 Reverse transmission: the internal feedback of the transistor S 22 Output reflection coefficient They are measured with a vector network analyzer and manufacturers publish them as a function of frequency and bias. With them the input and output matching networks are designed."},{"t":"Stability: the Rollett factor","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#estabilidad","x":"02 Stability: the Rollett factor Because of the internal feedback \\( S_{12} \\), a transistor can present negative resistance at one port for certain loads at the other, and oscillate. It is unconditionally stable if it does not oscillate with any passive load, a condition that is checked with two numbers: \\[ K = \\frac{1 - |S_{11}|^2 - |S_{22}|^2 + |\\Delta|^2}{2\\,|S_{12}S_{21}|} > 1 \\] \\[ |\\Delta| = |S_{11}S_{22} - S_{12}S_{21}| If they are met, the input and the output can be matched at the same time, and the gain obtained is the maximum available gain, \\( MAG = |S_{21}/S_{12}|\\,(K - \\sqrt{K^2 - 1}) \\). If not, there are forbidden loads, which show up as stability circles on the Smith chart, and the limit is the maximum stable gain \\( MSG = |S_{21}/S_{12}| \\). Lab · stability and gain of an RF transistor A transistor with S 11 = 0.6∠−160°, S 21 = 3∠80°, S 12 with phase 45° and S 22 = 0.5∠−60°. The magnitude of the internal feedback S 12 is varied. The blue curve is K as a function of |S 12 |; the green one, the maximum available gain where it exists. |S 12 | = |S 21 | ="},{"t":"Tuned and low-noise amplifiers","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#sintonizados","x":"03 Tuned and low-noise amplifiers A tuned amplifier uses resonant circuits as its load, to amplify only one band and reject the others: they are the intermediate-frequency stages of receivers. Its bandwidth is set by the loaded Q of the tanks, and with several synchronous stages the band narrows, as seen in multistage amplifiers. The collector–base capacitance, which in the S parameters is \\( S_{12} \\), can make them oscillate; it is cancelled with a neutralization or avoided with a cascode. In a low-noise amplifier (LNA), the first stage of a receiver, the input is not matched for maximum gain but for the source impedance that gives the minimum noise figure, \\( \\Gamma_{opt} \\), which the manufacturer publishes. The two do not coincide, and the design is a compromise that is resolved with constant-gain and constant-noise circles on the Smith chart. The noise figure and its measurement were covered in amplifier measurements."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · S parameters Measure with a vector network analyzer the S parameters of a transistor mounted on its evaluation board, and compute K and the maximum gain as a function of frequency. Activity 2 · Oscillation Find a frequency where K < 1, present at the input a load inside the instability circle and observe the oscillation with a spectrum analyzer. Activity 3 · Stabilizing Add a resistor in series with the base or in parallel with the output and measure how K rises and how much the gain drops."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Checking stability only at the operating frequency. The transistor can oscillate far from the band, where it has more gain. Matching an LNA for maximum gain. Noise figure is lost, and that is what matters in the first stage. Using K without looking at |Δ|. Both conditions are needed. Forgetting the board parasitics. They change the S parameters relative to those in the datasheet."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What does S 21 = 3∠80° mean? A wave voltage gain of 3 times, 9.5 dB, with 80° of phase, between 50 Ω ports. What is the maximum stable gain? \\( |S_{21}/S_{12}| \\): the limit when the transistor is potentially unstable, attainable by stabilizing it with resistors. Why is an LNA not matched to the conjugate of its input? Because the minimum noise figure is obtained with another source impedance, Γ opt. What does neutralization do? It cancels the internal collector–base feedback with an external feedback of opposite sign."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Guillermo Gonzalez. Microwave Transistor Amplifiers: Analysis and Design. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 1997. Design with S parameters: stability, gain and noise. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2012. S parameters and the design of microwave amplifiers. Keysight Technologies. S-Parameter Design. Application Note 154, Keysight. A practical introduction to amplifier design with S parameters."},{"t":"Thinking in frequency","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#fourier","x":"01 Thinking in frequency In communications almost everything is reasoned in the frequency domain: what part of the spectrum a signal occupies, what a channel lets through, where an interference falls. The tools are those of Signals and Systems —the series and the Fourier transform, their properties, the spectra and Parseval's relation—, applied to concrete questions: how much bandwidth a signal needs and what a channel does to it. One principle runs through the whole subject: the duration and the bandwidth of a signal are inversely related. A pulse of duration \\( T \\) occupies a bandwidth on the order of \\( 1/T \\). Transmitting faster requires more spectrum."},{"t":"How wide is a bandwidth?","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#ancho","x":"02 How wide is a bandwidth? Except in ideal cases, the spectrum of a real signal does not end at any frequency: it decays, but never reaches zero. That is why there are several definitions of bandwidth, and one has to say which one is being used: Definition What it measures −3 dB Up to where the power spectral density falls to half Null-to-null Between the first zeros of the spectrum on either side of the carrier. In baseband, up to the first zero: for a rectangular pulse, \\( 1/T \\); the modulated signal occupies \\( 2/T \\) null-to-null Fractional power (99 %) The band that contains 99 % of the energy: the one used by regulatory bodies Noise equivalent That of an ideal rectangular filter that passes the same noise power \\[ E = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |x(t)|^2\\, dt = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |X(f)|^2\\, df \\] By Parseval, energy can be counted in time or in frequency. The fraction contained in a band is the integral of \\( |X(f)|^2 \\) over that band divided by the total energy. Lab · the pulse shape and its bandwidth Three pulses of the same duration \\( T \\): rectangular, triangular and raised cosine. The plot shows their energy spectral density in dB, normalized; the shaded strip is the chosen band. Below, the fraction of the energy of each pulse that falls inside it. Bandwidth B·T ="},{"t":"Distortionless transmission","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#sin-distorsion","x":"03 Distortionless transmission A channel does not distort a signal if the output is an attenuated and delayed copy of the input, \\( y(t) = K\\,x(t - t_0) \\). In frequency, this requires two things over the whole band of the signal: \\[ |H(f)| = K \\] \\[ \\arg H(f) = -2\\pi f\\, t_0 \\] Constant magnitude and linear phase: all the components are attenuated equally and delayed by the same amount. If the first condition fails there is amplitude distortion; if the second fails, phase distortion, which does not change the power spectrum but does change the shape of the signal. The ear is not very sensitive to phase distortion, but digital data is: if the components of a pulse arrive with different delays, the pulse spreads and invades its neighbors (intersymbol interference). That is why modems and digital receivers have equalizers, filters that compensate for the magnitude and phase of the channel."},{"t":"Nonlinear systems","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#no-lineal","x":"04 Nonlinear systems A saturated amplifier or a mixer is not linear, and generates frequencies that were not at the input. With two tones \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\) at the input, harmonics and intermodulation products appear at \\( m f_1 \\pm n f_2 \\). The third-order ones, \\( 2f_1 - f_2 \\) and \\( 2f_2 - f_1 \\), are the most harmful: they fall right next to the signals, inside the same channel, and no filter separates them. The linearity of the amplifiers of a transmitter or of the front end of a receiver is specified with the third-order intercept point."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · The spectrum of a pulse With a pulse generator and the oscilloscope FFT, view the spectrum of rectangular pulses of 1 µs and 0.1 µs, and measure their first zeros. Activity 2 · Phase distortion Pass a square wave through an all-pass filter, which does not change the magnitude, and observe how its shape changes. Activity 3 · Intermodulation Inject two close tones into an amplifier and use a spectrum analyzer to measure the third-order products as the level is increased."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Giving a bandwidth without saying which one. At −3 dB and at 99 % they can differ by several times. Believing that a flat-magnitude filter does not distort. If its phase is not linear, it deforms the pulses. Using rectangular pulses in a band-limited channel. Their spectrum decays slowly and spills over into the neighboring channels. Forgetting intermodulation when raising the power. It grows three times faster than the signal, in decibels."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What bandwidth up to the first null does a 1 µs rectangular pulse have, in baseband? 1 MHz. What fraction of the energy of a rectangular pulse falls inside the first null? About 90 %. Where do the third-order intermodulation products of 100 and 101 MHz fall? At 99 and 102 MHz. What phase condition does distortionless transmission require? Phase linear with frequency: constant group delay."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Signals and systems in communications","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading B. P. Lathi and Zhi Ding. Modern Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 5th ed., Oxford University Press, 2019. Signal and system analysis oriented to communications, with bandwidths and distortion. Leon W. Couch. Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 8th ed., Pearson, 2013. The different definitions of bandwidth compared with examples. Simon Haykin and Michael Moher. Communication Systems. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. Distortionless transmission and nonlinear systems."},{"t":"Methods study and work measurement","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#metodos","x":"01 Methods study and work measurement Work study has two parts. Methods study records how a task is done, examines it critically —what is done, where, when, by whom and how— and proposes a simpler, safer and more productive way. It relies on charts: the flow process chart, with its symbols for operation, inspection, transport, delay and storage; the flow diagram on the plant floor plan; and the two-handed process chart for assembly stations. Work measurement determines how long the task takes with the chosen method. With stopwatch timing, the observed time is corrected by the performance rating of the worker and allowances for fatigue and personal needs are added: \\[ T_{est} = T_{obs} \\cdot \\frac{\\text{rating}}{100} \\cdot (1 + \\text{allowances}) \\] A worker observed at 50 s who works at 110 % of the normal pace, with 15 % allowances, gives a standard time of \\( 50 \\cdot 1.1 \\cdot 1.15 \\approx 63 \\) s. Predetermined motion time systems, such as MTM, and work sampling are also used."},{"t":"Markets, products and processes","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#mercado","x":"02 Markets, products and processes What and how much is produced is decided by the market: the analysis of demand, competition and prices defines the volume and the variety. Each product goes through a life cycle —introduction, growth, maturity and decline— that changes what matters at each stage: flexibility at the beginning, cost and efficiency at maturity. Depending on volume and variety, the process is organized in different ways: Process type Volume and variety Example in electronics Project A single, unique unit A radar station Job shop (by functions) Low volume, high variety Prototypes and made-to-order equipment Batch Medium volume Laboratory instruments Line High volume, low variety Assembly of phones or televisions Continuous Homogeneous product without interruption Manufacture of wafers or cables"},{"t":"Balancing a line","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#balanceo","x":"03 Balancing a line On a line, the product passes through successive stations and all of them have the same time to work: the cycle time, which is set by demand. Balancing the line means distributing the tasks among the stations while respecting the precedence order, without any station exceeding the cycle and using the smallest possible number of stations: \\[ c = \\frac{\\text{available time}}{\\text{demand}} \\] \\[ N_{min} = \\left\\lceil \\frac{\\sum t_i}{c} \\right\\rceil, \\quad \\eta = \\frac{\\sum t_i}{N\\,c} \\] The exact problem is combinatorial; in practice heuristics are used. The largest task time rule assigns to each station, among the available tasks that fit in the remaining time, the longest one. It does not always reach the theoretical minimum. Lab · balancing an assembly line Ten tasks to assemble a unit, in seconds: A inspect the board (15), B insert large components (45, after A), C solder connectors (30, after A), D touch up solder joints (25, after B), E mount the heat sink (20, after B), F electrical test (35, after C and D), G mount in the cabinet (30, after E and F), H wire the front panel (25, after G), I program (20, after F) and J final test and packing (30, after H and I). A 450-minute shift. Each bar is a station; the red line, the cycle time. Demand per shift ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Flow process chart Make the flow process chart for the assembly of a power supply in the laboratory and propose a better method by eliminating transports and delays. Activity 2 · Standard time Time a soldering task ten times, rate the pace and calculate the standard time with allowances. Activity 3 · Manual balancing Balance the laboratory line by hand for 540 units and explain why it cannot be brought down to six stations, even though the theoretical minimum suggests it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Measuring without having improved the method. An inefficient way of working gets standardized. Forgetting the allowances. A standard time without rest is impossible to sustain. Setting a cycle shorter than the longest task. The task has to be split or the station duplicated. Believing the heuristic gives the optimum. It is a good starting point, not a guarantee."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What cycle time corresponds to 300 units in a 450-minute shift? 90 s. How many stations are needed at minimum, if the tasks add up to 275 s? \\( \\lceil 275/90 \\rceil = 4 \\). What efficiency does the line have with those four stations? \\( 275/(4 \\cdot 90) \\approx 76 \\) %. What process is suitable for producing varied prototypes? A job shop organized by functions."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Analysis of work and processes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading International Labour Organization (George Kanawaty, ed.). Introduction to Work Study. 4th ed., ILO, 1992. (Spanish edition: “Introducción al estudio del trabajo”, 4th ed., ILO, 1996.) Methods study, flow process charts and work measurement. Benjamin W. Niebel and Andris Freivalds. Methods, Standards, and Work Design. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería industrial: métodos, estándares y diseño del trabajo”.) Methods and times, with many worked cases. Jay Heizer and Barry Render. Principles of Operations Management. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Principios de administración de operaciones”.) Process types and line balancing."},{"t":"The superheterodyne spectrum analyzer","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#super","x":"01 The superheterodyne spectrum analyzer The oscilloscope shows the signal in time; the spectrum analyzer, its power as a function of frequency. The classic analyzer is a self-tuning superheterodyne receiver: a local oscillator sweeps a range of frequencies, a mixer translates each input component to a fixed intermediate frequency, and there a narrow filter, the resolution bandwidth (RBW) filter, lets through only what falls within it. A detector measures the power, and the screen plots it as a function of the local oscillator frequency. Three controls govern the measurement: the span of frequencies swept, the RBW and the video bandwidth (VBW), a filter after the detector that smooths the trace. The presentation of spectra as an analysis tool was covered in spectra, in Signals and Systems."},{"t":"RBW: resolution, noise and time","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#rbw","x":"02 RBW: resolution, noise and time The RBW decides three things at once. Two signals are seen as separate only if their spacing is of the order of the RBW or greater. The noise entering the detector is proportional to the bandwidth, so the displayed noise floor drops 10 dB for each decade of RBW. And the filter needs time to respond, so the sweep cannot be faster than: \\[ t_{sweep} \\approx k\\, \\frac{\\mathrm{span}}{\\mathrm{RBW}^2} \\] \\[ P_{floor} = DANL_{1\\,Hz} + 10 \\log \\mathrm{RBW} \\] \\( k \\) is between 2 and 3 for analog Gaussian filters. Lowering the RBW by a factor of ten lowers the floor by 10 dB but makes the sweep a hundred times slower. \\( DANL \\) is the displayed average noise level, which is specified normalized to 1 Hz. When the two signals have very different levels, the resolution also depends on the filter’s selectivity: the skirt of the strong signal’s response can hide the weak one even if it is farther away than the RBW. Lab · two tones and the resolution filter Two close tones, the first at 0 dBm, seen with a Gaussian resolution filter, over a span of 200 kHz. The analyzer has a noise level of −150 dBm/Hz. The tones are considered resolved if between them the trace drops at least 3 dB relative to the weaker one. RBW = 1 kHz RBW = 3 kHz RBW = 10 kHz RBW = 30 kHz Spacing between tones = Level of the second tone ="},{"t":"FFT and real-time analyzers","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#otros","x":"03 FFT and real-time analyzers At low frequencies, and in modern analyzers also at the intermediate frequency, the spectrum is calculated by digitizing the signal and applying an FFT. With narrow RBWs it is much faster than sweeping, because it processes all the frequencies at once. Real-time analyzers compute overlapping FFTs with no dead time and can display signals that appear for microseconds, such as a Bluetooth frequency hop, which a sweep would miss. The same instruments measure phase noise, channel power, adjacent-channel leakage ratio and digital modulation quality."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Resolving two tones Combine two generators with a 5 kHz difference and lower the RBW until they are separated. Repeat with one of them 40 dB lower. Activity 2 · Noise floor With the input terminated in 50 Ω, measure the noise floor with several RBWs and verify the slope of 10 dB per decade. Activity 3 · Distortion of the analyzer itself Measure the harmonics of a generator with different input attenuations: if the level of the harmonic changes when the attenuation is changed, the analyzer is generating it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Using a large RBW to see a weak signal. The noise floor hides it. Sweeping faster than the filter allows. Amplitudes come out low and frequencies shifted; instruments warn with an uncalibrated alarm. Overloading the input mixer. Distortion products appear that are not in the signal. Confusing channel power with peak level. For modulated signals, it is measured by integrating over the bandwidth."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment By how much does the noise floor drop when going from 100 kHz to 1 kHz of RBW? 20 dB. How long does a 1 MHz sweep take with a 1 kHz RBW and \\( k = 2.5 \\)? \\( 2.5 \\cdot 10^6/10^6 = 2.5 \\) s. What noise level does a −150 dBm/Hz analyzer show with a 10 kHz RBW? −110 dBm. Why does a real-time analyzer see signals that a sweep misses? Because it processes all the frequencies continuously, with no dead time; the sweep looks at each frequency only for an instant."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Frequency-domain measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Keysight Technologies. Spectrum Analysis Basics. Application Note 150, Keysight. The classic reference on the superheterodyne analyzer, the RBW and the sweep time. Christoph Rauscher, Volker Janssen and Roland Minihold. Fundamentals of Spectrum Analysis. Rohde & Schwarz, 2001. A complete book on swept and FFT analyzers, with their sources of error. Clyde F. Coombs Jr. (ed.). Electronic Instrument Handbook. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. Spectrum and signal analyzers within the whole of instrumentation."},{"t":"What defines an antenna","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#parametros","x":"01 What defines an antenna An antenna is the transducer between a transmission line and space. The radiation unit covered radiation resistance, the pattern and directivity; to design a link you also need: Parameter What it tells you Gain \\( G = e \\cdot D \\) The directivity after subtracting the losses of the antenna; given in dBi Beamwidth The angle between the directions where the power falls to half (−3 dB) Impedance and bandwidth The range of frequencies where the SWR is acceptable, typically less than 1.5 or 2 Polarization The orientation of the electric field; the two antennas of the link have to match Front-to-back ratio How much less it radiates backward than forward An antenna works the same for transmitting as for receiving: by reciprocity, the pattern and the gain are the same. In reception, the gain translates into an effective area: \\[ A_e = \\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi}\\, G \\] The power delivered by the antenna is the incoming power density multiplied by its effective area. A half-wave dipole at 146 MHz has 0.55 m²; the same dipole at 2.4 GHz, 270 times less."},{"t":"Antennas in common use","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#tipos","x":"02 Antennas in common use Antenna Typical gain Use \\( \\lambda /4 \\) monopole over a ground plane 5.2 dBi, 36 Ω AM broadcasting (the tower is the monopole), mobile units, VHF radios Half-wave dipole 2.15 dBi, 73 Ω Reference; element of larger antennas Yagi-Uda 8 to 15 dBi Television, VHF and UHF links Patch on a board 6 to 9 dBi GPS, Wi-Fi, cellular telephony arrays Horn 10 to 25 dBi Feed for parabolic dishes, measurement standard Parabolic dish of diameter \\( D \\) \\( e\\,(\\pi D/\\lambda)^2 \\), 30 to 45 dBi Microwave and satellite links The monopole takes advantage of the image formed by the ground plane: it behaves as half a dipole, with half the resistance and twice the directivity, because it radiates only over the upper half-space. The parabolic dish concentrates the energy in a beam of approximate width \\( 70°\\, \\lambda / D \\): a 0.6 m dish at 11 GHz has 34 dBi and a 3° beam, and it has to be aimed with that precision."},{"t":"The Friis equation","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#friis","x":"03 The Friis equation Between two antennas in free space, separated by a distance \\( d \\) much greater than their size, the received power is: \\[ P_r = P_t\\, G_t\\, G_r \\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi d}\\right)^2 \\] \\[ L_{fs} = 32.45 + 20\\log f_{MHz} + 20\\log d_{km} \\] The factor in parentheses is the free-space loss, which the second expression gives in decibels. It is not an absorption: it is the power spreading over an ever larger sphere, seen through the effective area of an isotropic antenna. In decibels, the link is built by adding and subtracting, just like the budget of a line or that of a fiber: transmitter power, minus cables, plus gains, minus path loss. What arrives is compared with the receiver threshold, which starts from thermal noise, −174 dBm per hertz at room temperature, and adds the bandwidth, the noise figure and the signal-to-noise ratio that the modulation requires. The difference is the fade margin, the reserve for rain and for changes in propagation."},{"t":"Line of sight and Fresnel zones","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#fresnel","x":"04 Line of sight and Fresnel zones A microwave link needs more than just seeing the other antenna: the energy travels through a volume around the direct ray. The first Fresnel zone is the ellipsoid where the indirect path is up to half a wavelength longer than the direct one. Its radius at a point \\( d_1 \\) and \\( d_2 \\) from the ends is: \\[ r_1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\lambda\\, d_1 d_2}{d_1 + d_2}} \\] The design criterion is to keep at least 60% of the first zone free of obstacles. Over 30 km at 11 GHz, the radius at the center is 14 m. The most common obstacle on a long link is the Earth itself. The refraction of the atmosphere bends the rays downward, and the effect is modeled with an effective Earth radius of 4/3 the real one. Even so, at the center of a 30 km link the ground rises 13 m above the straight line joining the bases of the towers: the antennas have to be about 22 m high to clear 60% of the zone over flat terrain. Lab · a point-to-point microwave link Two identical dishes, at the same height over flat terrain. The graph shows the profile (the vertical scale is greatly exaggerated): the bulge of the Earth with an effective radius of 4/3, the line of sight and the first Fresnel zone, with the 60% marked. A 23 dBm radio, 2 dB of cable loss and a receiver with 28 MHz of bandwidth and a 6 dB noise figure that needs a 17 dB signal-to-noise ratio. 2.4 GHz 5.8 GHz 11 GHz 18 GHz 0.3 m antennas 0.6 m antennas 1.2 m antennas Distance = Antenna height ="},{"t":"Other propagation mechanisms","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#propagacion","x":"05 Other propagation mechanisms Mechanism Frequencies What takes advantage of it Ground wave Long and medium wave AM radio by day, hundreds of kilometers over moist ground or sea Ionospheric reflection 3 to 30 MHz Shortwave with worldwide reach, varying with the time of day and the solar cycle Line of sight VHF and above Television, cellular telephony, microwave links Multipath UHF and microwaves in cities Reflections off buildings; OFDM and MIMO turn them into an advantage Rain attenuation Above about 10 GHz Sets the margin needed on links from 18 to 80 GHz"},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics System What decides the link Radio link between base stations 0.3 to 1.2 m dishes at 6 to 38 GHz, with margin for rain Home Wi-Fi Antennas of a few dBi, walls and multipath more than distance Satellite television 60 cm dish at 36,000 km, with a low-noise amplifier at the focus Field telemetry A Yagi at each end to gain range with low-power transmitters Antenna installation and alignment are covered in antennas and in microwave links, in the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the gain With two identical antennas facing each other at a known distance in the far field, measure the attenuation between them with a generator and an analyzer. Solve for the gain of each from the Friis equation. Activity 2 · The pattern of a Yagi Rotate a UHF Yagi on an axis in 10° steps facing a fixed transmitter and plot the pattern. Measure the beamwidth and the front-to-back ratio. Activity 3 · Designing a real link With a terrain profile obtained from an elevation map, design a 5.8 GHz link between two points in the city: tower height, antennas and margin."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing that free-space loss depends on frequency by itself. It grows with frequency because the effective area of an antenna of fixed gain shrinks; with antennas of fixed area, the link improves as the frequency goes up. Designing with line of sight but without clearing the Fresnel zone. The link works in the test and drops out with changes in refraction. Forgetting polarization. Two crossed antennas lose 20 dB or more. Leaving no margin. An 18 GHz link with no margin drops out in the first storm."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the free-space loss over 30 km at 11 GHz? \\( 32.45 + 20\\log 11\\,000 + 20\\log 30 \\approx 142.8 \\) dB. What gain does a 0.6 m parabolic dish have at 11 GHz with 55% efficiency? \\( 0.55\\,(\\pi \\cdot 0.6 / 0.0273)^2 \\approx 2630 \\), that is, 34.2 dBi. How much does the received power change if the distance is doubled? It drops 6 dB, to a quarter. Why does a \\( \\lambda /4 \\) monopole have 36 Ω and not 73 Ω? Because with its image in the ground plane it forms a half-wave dipole, but only the upper half radiates: the same current delivers half the power."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Antennas and radio links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Constantine A. Balanis. Antenna Theory: Analysis and Design. 4th ed., Wiley, 2016. The types of antenna one by one, arrays, and the Friis equation with all its assumptions. Warren L. Stutzman and Gary A. Thiele. Antenna Theory and Design. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2012. More design-oriented, with good chapters on aperture antennas and parabolic dishes. Roger L. Freeman. Radio System Design for Telecommunications. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2007. The design of complete radio links: profiles, Fresnel zones, rain and availability."},{"t":"What a preliminary design contains","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#contenido","x":"01 What a preliminary design contains The preliminary design is the document that shows, before building, that the proposed solution will meet the specification. It is not the detailed design —the complete schematics and the board come later—, but the architecture decisions justified with calculations. A typical preliminary design includes: Part What it says Descriptive report The problem, the user, the scope and what is left out Specification The quantitative and verifiable requirements Block diagram The functions, the interfaces between them and the signals that connect them Selection of main components The microcontroller, the sensor, the radio, the power supply, with the discarded alternatives and why Sizing budgets Energy, measurement error, radio link, dissipation, response times Test plan How each point of the specification will be verified Preliminary costs and schedule The priced bill of materials and the stages"},{"t":"The sizing budgets","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#presupuestos","x":"02 The sizing budgets A budget distributes a limited resource among the blocks and verifies that it is enough with a margin. The error budget adds up the uncertainties of the sensor, signal conditioning and converter, as seen in Measurements; the link budget adds up powers, gains and losses down to the receiver sensitivity; the thermal budget verifies junction temperatures with the dissipation of each component, and feeds the reliability estimate. In a battery-powered device, the most important is the energy budget: with active and sleep duty cycles, the average current is \\[ \\bar I = \\frac{I_a\\,t_a + I_s\\,(T - t_a)}{T} \\] \\[ \\text{battery life} = \\frac{\\eta\\, C}{\\bar I} \\] \\( I_a \\) is the active current during \\( t_a \\), \\( I_s \\) the sleep current, \\( T \\) the period between wake-ups, \\( C \\) the battery capacity and \\( \\eta \\) the usable fraction, taken here as 0.8 to cover the cutoff voltage, temperature and aging. Lab · energy budget of a sensor node A node that wakes up every T seconds, measures and transmits for t a at current I a, and sleeps the rest of the time at I s. The curve is the battery life in years as a function of the period, on a logarithmic scale; the yellow dot is the chosen design. Sleep current I s = Active current I a = Active time t a = Period T = Battery Two alkaline AA cells (2500 mAh) CR2032 coin cell (225 mAh) 18650 lithium cell (3000 mAh)"},{"t":"The test plan","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#ensayos","x":"03 The test plan Each point of the specification needs a test that says how it will be measured, with what instrument, under what conditions and what the acceptance criterion is. Writing it into the preliminary design forces the specification to be verifiable and anticipates the instruments that will be needed. The degree’s laboratory tests —from Electronic Measurements I and II— are the basis; those for regulatory compliance, if the product requires them, are usually done in an external laboratory and should be budgeted. The manufacturing technology was defined in construction technology."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Block diagram Draw the block diagram of the final project with the interfaces, the supply voltages and the signals between blocks. Activity 2 · Measuring consumption With a sense resistor and the oscilloscope, measure the current profile of a microcontroller that sleeps and wakes up, and compare it with the budget. Activity 3 · Verification matrix Build a table with each specification, the test that verifies it and the instrument required."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Taking the sleep current from the microcontroller datasheet alone. Regulators, dividers, sensors and pull-ups also consume. Using the full nominal capacity of the battery. At low temperature or with current peaks considerably less is usable. Promising battery lives of decades. Battery self-discharge sets a limit of a few years to a decade. Leaving the test plan for the end. If a specification cannot be tested, it has to be rewritten now."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What average current does the lab node have with its initial values? About 38.3 μA. What battery life does that give with two AA cells? Almost 6 years: \\( 0.8 \\cdot 2500 / 0.0383 \\approx 52\\,000 \\) hours. And with a CR2032 coin cell? A little over half a year. What is the difference between a preliminary design and a detailed design? The preliminary design fixes and justifies the architecture with calculations; the detailed design produces the complete schematics, board and software."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The engineering preliminary design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Karl T. Ulrich and Steven D. Eppinger. Product Design and Development. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Diseño y desarrollo de productos”.) Product architecture and system-level design. Elecia White. Making Embedded Systems. 2nd ed., O'Reilly, 2024. Embedded systems architecture and power reduction. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. Low-power circuit design and component selection."},{"t":"Increase, decrease and extrema","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#crecimiento","x":"01 Increase, decrease and extrema The sign of the derivative tells you where the function is heading, and the points where it vanishes are the candidates for maximum or minimum: If on an interval… then f… \\( f'(x) > 0 \\) increases \\( f'(x) < 0 \\) decreases \\( f'(c) = 0 \\) or does not exist has a critical point at c: it has to be analyzed The first-derivative test settles each critical point by looking at how the sign of \\( f' \\) changes as it passes through it: from + to − is a maximum; from − to + is a minimum; if it does not change, it is not an extremum (like \\( x^3 \\) at the origin). A zero derivative does not guarantee an extremum \\( f(x) = x^3 \\) has \\( f'(0) = 0 \\) and at 0 there is neither a maximum nor a minimum: there is an inflection point with a horizontal tangent. That is why a critical point is a candidate, not a conclusion. To find the absolute extrema on a closed interval [a, b] —which exist, by Weierstrass — you compare the values at the critical points and at the two endpoints of the interval. The largest and the smallest of that list are the answer."},{"t":"Concavity and inflection","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#concavidad","x":"02 Concavity and inflection The second derivative measures how the slope changes: if \\( f'' > 0 \\) the curve is concave up and the slope increases; if \\( f'' < 0 \\), concave down. Where \\( f'' \\) changes sign there is an inflection point. From this comes the second-derivative test, quicker when it can be applied: if \\( f'(c) = 0 \\) and \\( f''(c) > 0 \\), there is a minimum at c; if \\( f''(c) < 0 \\), a maximum. If \\( f''(c) = 0 \\), the test is inconclusive and you have to go back to the first-derivative test. Lab · the function, its derivative and its second derivative The marked points are the critical and inflection points, computed live by checking where each derivative changes sign. f(x) = x³ − 3x f(x) = x⁴/4 − x² f(x) = 3·e −x² Damped oscillation: 3·e −0.4x ·sin(2x) Power delivered to a load R (with r = 2) f′ f″"},{"t":"Rolle, the mean value theorem and L’Hôpital","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#teoremas","x":"03 Rolle, the mean value theorem and L’Hôpital Lagrange’s theorem, or the mean value theorem If f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), there is at least one point c where the slope of the tangent equals the slope of the secant joining the endpoints: \\[ f' (c) = \\frac{f (b) -f (a)}{b -a} \\] Translated: if a car covers 120 km in one hour, at some instant its speedometer read exactly \\( 120\\text{ km/h} \\). Rolle’\\( s \\) theorem is the special case with \\( f(a) = f(b) \\), where that slope is zero. Consequences of Lagrange’s theorem are used without being named: a function with zero derivative on an interval is constant, and two functions with the same derivative differ by a constant —the basis of the indefinite integral — and the increase and decrease criteria of section 1. L’Hôpital’s rule For a \\( 0/0 \\) or \\( \\infty/\\infty \\) indeterminate form, the limit of the quotient equals the limit of the quotient of the derivatives, if the latter exists: \\[ \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f (x)}{g (x)} = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f' (x)}{g' (x)} \\] It can be repeated if the indeterminate form persists. It is not the derivative of the quotient: it is the two derivatives taken separately. And you have to check that the indeterminate form is present before applying it."},{"t":"Optimization: the design problem","d":"Applications of the derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada#optimizacion","x":"04 Optimization: the design problem Optimizing is the bread and butter of engineering: maximizing power, efficiency or bandwidth; minimizing consumption, cost or dissipation. The method is always the same: Write the quantity to optimize as a function of a single variable, using the relations of the problem to eliminate the others. Establish the domain that makes physical sense. Differentiate, set equal to zero and solve. Check that it is a maximum or a minimum, and also check the boundaries of the domain. Worked example · maximum power transfer A voltage source V with internal resistance \\( r \\) drives a load \\( R \\). The current is \\( i = V/(r + R) \\) and the power in the load is: \\[ P (R) = i^2 R = \\frac{V^2 R}{{\\left(r + R \\right)}^2} \\] Differentiating with the quotient rule: \\[ P' (R) = V^2 \\frac{\\left(r + R)^2 -R \\cdot 2 (r + R \\right)}{{\\left(r + R \\right)}^4} = V^2 \\frac{r -R}{{\\left(r + R \\right)}^3} \\] \\( P' \\) vanishes at \\( R = r \\), and since \\( P' > 0 \\) before and \\( P' < 0 \\) after, it is a maximum. The maximum power is \\( P = V^2/(4r) \\), and the efficiency at that point is barely 50%: half of the energy is dissipated inside the source. That is why the criterion is used with small signals —an antenna, a radio-frequency amplifier— and not in power distribution, where what matters is efficiency."}]);
